/* The workstation's references, each with a short summary in the workstation's words, for Form RS-1's Readings discussed
   (v23.97). Every reference Forms TD-1 and GB-1 cite, and the readings the BCBA added (v24.48: Kimball et al., 2023; v24.57: Sellers et al., 2016).
   Written by the release tooling (readings.py); do not edit by hand. */
const NBH_READINGS=[{"k":"adami2017","apa":"Adami, S., Falcomata, T. S., Muething, C. S., & Hoffman, K. (2017). An evaluation of lag schedules of reinforcement during functional communication training: Effects on varied mand responding and challenging behavior. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(3), 209–213.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0179-7","y":2017,"sum":"A 23-year-old man with autism whose self-injury and aggression were maintained by access to preferred items (seen at home) and a 10-year-old boy with autism whose aggression was maintained by escape from work (seen at school) first had brief prompted practice with each of several ways to ask: a tablet communication app, a picture card, a switch and, for the man, a spoken word."},{"k":"agana2025","apa":"Agana, T. R. T., Sidener, T. M., Rodriguez, N. M., Reeve, S. A., & Pane, H. M. (2025). An analysis of the generalization of pretend play from real objects to toys. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 58(3), 478–489.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70017","y":2025,"sum":"A 7-year-old girl with autism at an early intervention clinic was learning at a table to name features of animals, foods and objects. Right after she got an edible for an answer, the teacher briefly acted out a short pretend-play sequence with words, using toys the girl could not touch, and gave no reward for copying it."},{"k":"akers2018","apa":"Akers, J. S., Higbee, T. S., Pollard, J. S., & Reinert, K. S. (2018). Sibling-implemented script fading to promote play-based statements of children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 395–399.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0257-5","y":2018,"sum":"Three children with autism aged 4 to 7 were taught at home by a sibling who presented three recorded 3- to 4-word play lines every 30 s, guided the child to press the recorder and modeled the words if needed, and answered every comment the child made. Once the child said all three lines independently in 2 consecutive sessions the lines were cut one word at a time until the recorder was gone."},{"k":"albert2012","apa":"Albert, K. M., Carbone, V. J., Murray, D. D., Hagerty, M., & Sweeney-Kerwin, E. J. (2012). Increasing the mand repertoire of children with autism through the use of an interrupted chain procedure. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 5(2), 65–76.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391825","y":2012,"sum":"Three children with autism aged 5–8, who already asked for things they could see, first learned three favorite multi-step activities (such as toasting bread for a peanut-butter sandwich or putting a CD on to listen) with hands-on guidance that was faded. Then one needed item was left out: at that step the teacher waited 10 seconds, said the item's name if the child had not asked, and gave it as soon as the child said it."},{"k":"aldi2016","apa":"Aldi, C., Crigler, A., Kates-McElrath, K., Long, B., Smith, H., Rehak, K., & Wilkinson, L. (2016). Examining the effects of video modeling and prompts to teach activities of daily living skills. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(4), 384–388.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0127-y","y":2016,"sum":"Two 18-year-old young men with autism were each taught three home tasks (cooking frozen tortellini, setting the table, folding jeans, cleaning a counter and sink, cleaning a mirror) in their own homes with narrated point-of-view iPad videos; after an error the student rewatched that segment, and a gesture followed if needed."},{"k":"alexander1976","apa":"Alexander, R. N., Corbett, T. F., & Smigel, J. (1976). The effects of individual and group consequences on school attendance and curfew violations with predelinquent adolescents. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 221–226.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-221","y":1976,"sum":"Seven adolescent boys in county custody living at a residential treatment center (referred by the court for truancy, running away, assault, theft and probation violations) who attended three high schools for five class periods a day. Class attendance averaged 51% at baseline, 80% with an individual daily reward and 94–96% under the all-or-none group rule, falling to 57–60% each time the reward was withdrawn."},{"k":"allen2010","apa":"Allen, K. D., Wallace, D. P., Renes, D., Bowen, S. L., & Burke, R. V. (2010). Use of video modeling to teach vocational skills to adolescents and young adults with autism spectrum disorders. Education and Treatment of Children, 33(3), 339–349.","doi":"10.1353/etc.0.0101","y":2010,"sum":"Four adolescents and young adults with autism watched a video model and then learned to wear a mascot costume and entertain customers in a retail store, checked in role play and on the job; all four learned the skills and said they enjoyed the work."},{"k":"almas2022","apa":"Almås, I. K., Smith, D. P., Eldevik, S., & Eikeseth, S. (2022). Emergent intraverbal and reverse intraverbal behavior following listener training in children with autism spectrum disorder. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 38(1), 1–23.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-021-00164-3","y":2022,"sum":"Six boys with autism, ages 4–11, were first taught to touch the picture that answered each question in a field of three, with praise and tokens for correct touches and a point prompt after errors, to 8 of 9 correct for 2 sessions and then on a thinner schedule."},{"k":"alwahbi2024","apa":"Alwahbi, A. A. (2024). The efficacy of virtual positive behavior support in a special school for students with ASD. Journal of Behavioral Education, 33(1), 123–141.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-022-09486-1","y":2024,"sum":"Teachers in four elementary classrooms added a group contingency to the school’s warning system: tickets for following the rules, a lottery for small prizes, rewards that depended on the whole class, and a chart where each student recorded the tickets earned. Engaged time rose and inappropriate behavior fell for all twelve at-risk students aged 7 to 9."},{"k":"amatozech2006","apa":"Amato-Zech, N. A., Hoff, K. E., & Doepke, K. J. (2006). Increasing on-task behavior in the classroom: Extension of self-monitoring strategies. Psychology in the Schools, 43(2), 211–221.","doi":"10.1002/pits.20137","y":2006,"sum":"The student is taught what working looks like, then to mark the card at each cue; at first an adult marks too and the two compare, and agreement earns a small reward. The cues are spaced further apart as on-task behavior holds. Self-monitoring is among the best-supported classroom interventions for students with behavior problems."},{"k":"anderson2010","apa":"Anderson, C. M., & Borgmeier, C. (2010). Tier II interventions within the framework of school-wide positive behavior support: Essential features for design, implementation, and maintenance. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 3(1), 33–45.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391756","y":2010,"sum":"A Tier II support for disruptive or inattentive behavior: the student meets a coordinator each morning to get the point card and go over the expectations printed on it, hands it to each teacher, who rates how well the expectations were met, then checks out at the end of the day; the card goes home. The feedback and the adult’s attention throughout the day are the active parts."},{"k":"arco1991","apa":"Arco, L. (1991). Effects of outcome performance feedback on maintenance of client and staff behavior in a residential setting. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(4), 231–247.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060402","y":1991,"sum":"Eight group home staff were trained (workshop, modeling, rehearsal and feedback) to prompt and praise interaction between partners during paired games for four nonspeaking adults (25 to 33) with severe or profound intellectual disability, then got only feedback on the residents' results for 17 weeks, in an ABC design."},{"k":"argott2017","apa":"Argott, P. J., Townsend, D. B., & Poulson, C. L. (2017). Acquisition and generalization of complex empathetic responses among children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(2), 107–117.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0171-7","y":2017,"sum":"Four 10- to 12-year-olds with autism in a private center-based program were taught to answer an instructor's live displays of joy, frustration and pain with a fitting statement, tone of voice, facial expression and gesture (for example, saying \"That's great!\" with a high five)."},{"k":"aspiranti2011","apa":"Aspiranti, K. B., Skinner, C. H., McCleary, D. F., & Cihak, D. F. (2011). Using taped-problems and rewards to increase addition-fact fluency in a first grade general education classroom. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 4(2), 25–33.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391781","y":2011,"sum":"A rural general education class of 20 first graders with no identified disabilities practiced three sets of 15 addition facts. In each daily session after the math lesson, a tape read each problem and then its answer; students tried to write the answer first (\"beat the tape\") and corrected it after hearing it, then did a brief independent sprint sheet."},{"k":"aspiranti2019","apa":"Aspiranti, K. B., Bebech, A., Ruffo, B., & Skinner, C. H. (2019). Classroom management in self-contained classrooms for children with autism: Extending research on the color wheel system. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 143–153.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0264-6","y":2019,"sum":"Twenty-one students with autism in three self-contained K–5 classrooms learned the three rule sets from a social story with photographs, practiced examples and nonexamples of each color and every transition between colors, and then heard the story each morning while teachers praised and rewarded rule following and prompted gently when rules were broken."},{"k":"austin2015","apa":"Austin, J. L., & Tiger, J. H. (2015). Providing alternative reinforcers to facilitate tolerance to delayed reinforcement following functional communication training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 48(3), 663–668.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.215","y":2015,"sum":"The wait begins very short (a few seconds) and grows only after the student waits well at the current length. A clear signal starts the wait, and something to do during it helps. In the studies, waiting improved most when calm waiting itself was what produced the item."},{"k":"auten2026","apa":"Auten, E. M., & Peterson, K. M. (2026). The role of re-presentation in the treatment of liquid expulsion. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 59(1), e70052.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70052","y":2026,"sum":"Seventeen children aged 1 to 3 with severe feeding difficulties and escape-maintained refusal were given 2-ml drinks every 30 s by a hospital feeding team; the cup stayed at the lips until accepted, swallowing was praised, and for most children expelled liquid was put back in the mouth. Swallowing rose from 0% to about 90–100% of drinks for most children, best with re-presentation for 10 of 17."},{"k":"ayllon1974","apa":"Ayllon, T., & Roberts, M. D. (1974). Eliminating discipline problems by strengthening academic performance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 71–76.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-71","y":1974,"sum":"Five fifth-grade boys judged the most disruptive in a 38-pupil reading class did 15-minute written workbook assignments while, in an ABAB design, the whole class earned points for accuracy (2 points for at least 80% correct, 5 for 100%) that bought activities and privileges, with no consequence at all for disruption."},{"k":"ayllon1975","apa":"Ayllon, T., Layman, D., & Kandel, H. J. (1975). A behavioral-educational alternative to drug control of hyperactive children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 137–146.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-137","y":1975,"sum":"Three children aged 8 to 10 in a self-contained learning-disabilities class who took methylphenidate for hyperactivity were observed on medication, then off it after their doctors and parents agreed to stop it. Off medication, they earned a check on a card for each correct answer, first in daily math (10 addition problems in 10 minutes) and then also in reading comprehension (a 10-question workbook page), exchangeable later for items and activities such…"},{"k":"ayllon1976","apa":"Ayllon, T., Garber, S., & Pisor, K. (1976). Reducing time limits: A means to increase behavior of retardates. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 247–252.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-247","y":1976,"sum":"Three students aged 10 to 12 with mild intellectual disability in a self-contained public school class, two to three years behind in math, did a daily set of 20 problems at their own level and earned a marble token for each correct answer, traded later for privileges such as free time."},{"k":"azrin1969","apa":"Azrin, N. H., & Powell, J. (1969). Behavioral engineering: The use of response priming to improve prescribed self-medication. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 39–42.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-39","y":1969,"sum":"Six women aged 20 to 33 who worked at a state hospital took an inert pill every 30 minutes through the workday, for 4 days each with an ordinary pill case and their own watch, a timer that gave a brief beep, and a pocket dispenser that hummed softly until a knob was turned, which also dropped the next pill into the hand."},{"k":"azrin1971","apa":"Azrin, N. H., & Foxx, R. M. (1971). A rapid method of toilet training the institutionalized retarded. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 89–99.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-89","y":1971,"sum":"Scheduled visits (sits of a few minutes on a timer), extra fluids, immediate reinforcement for each void in the toilet and a brief, neutral response to accidents; the time between visits grows as the student stays dry, and then the student is taught to ask. School staff ran this in public school programs."},{"k":"azrin1988","apa":"Azrin, N. H., Besalel, V. A., Jamner, J. P., & Caputo, J. N. (1988). Comparative study of behavioral methods of treating severe self-injury. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 3(2), 119–152.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360030204","y":1988,"sum":"Nine people with long-standing self-injury that caused tissue damage (eight adults aged 21 to 35 with profound intellectual disability and one 13-year-old girl with autism) living in state institutions, seen one-to-one at a classroom-style table; the self-injury included face slapping, head and ear hitting, head banging, biting and eye gouging."},{"k":"bachmeyer2009","apa":"Bachmeyer, M. H. (2009). Treatment of selective and inadequate food intake in children: A review and practical guide. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 2(1), 43–50.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391736","y":2009,"sum":"For children with established but narrow eating, procedures that work without escape extinction include praise or a preferred food for each accepted bite, preferred items available through the meal, the new food presented together with a preferred one, a quick sequence of easy requests (empty spoon presentations) before a bite, and bite size or texture faded in gradually."},{"k":"baer1971","apa":"Baer, D. M., & Guess, D. (1971). Receptive training of adjectival inflections in mental retardates. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 129–139.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-129","y":1971,"sum":"Three residents of a state institution (aged 13, 12 and 7.5, with severe intellectual disability) were taught to point to pictures named by opposite adjectives, then by comparative (-er) forms and later by superlative (-est) forms, across 19 sets of four cards, earning tokens for a store on about every third correct answer, with a correction and short pause after errors."},{"k":"baer1973","apa":"Baer, A. M., Rowbury, T., & Baer, D. M. (1973). The development of instructional control over classroom activities of deviant preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 289–298.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-289","y":1973,"sum":"Three children aged 4 to 6 in a special preschool class for multiple behavior problems, including hyperactivity, language delays, autism-like mannerisms and extreme noncompliance, worked in a token room where three completions of a preacademic task (such as a puzzle, lotto card or shape sorter) earned a token for 5 minutes in a play area with a snack on request."},{"k":"bailey1969","apa":"Bailey, J., & Meyerson, L. (1969). Vibration as a reinforcer with a profoundly retarded child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(2), 135–137.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-135","y":1969,"sum":"A 7-year-old boy with profound intellectual disability who was blind, partly deaf, did not walk or speak, stayed in his crib and often hit or bit himself, had a padded lever mounted in the crib. After a 7-day baseline in which pressing did nothing, each press turned on 6 seconds of vibration from a unit under the bed springs for 21 days, followed by 23 days of extinction, and presses were counted automatically 24 hours a day; no mastery criterion was set…"},{"k":"bailey1970","apa":"Bailey, J. S., Wolf, M. M., & Phillips, E. L. (1970). Home-based reinforcement and the modification of pre-delinquents' classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(3), 223–233.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-223","y":1970,"sum":"Boys aged 11 to 15 living in a family-style group home for youths referred by the juvenile court carried a daily report card on which the teacher marked whether they had studied the whole period and obeyed the class rules, and an all-yes card earned the day's main home privileges (snacks, TV and going outdoors)."},{"k":"bailey1971","apa":"Bailey, J. S., Timbers, G. D., Phillips, E. L., & Wolf, M. M. (1971). Modification of articulation errors of pre-delinquents by their peers. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(4), 265–281.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-265","y":1971,"sum":"Two boys aged 13 and 12 living in a family-style group home for youths placed by the juvenile court, each with several speech-sound errors, practiced picture words with housemates aged 12 to 15 who earned home points for judging and correcting the words; the boys earned points for correct words and lost points for errors."},{"k":"ballard1975","apa":"Ballard, K. D., & Glynn, T. (1975). Behavioral self-management in story writing with elementary school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 387–398.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-387","y":1975,"sum":"In a regular third-grade class in New Zealand (37 children aged 8 to 9; 14 sampled for data), a daily 25-minute writing time with posted charts defining sentences, action words and describing words was followed by 8 minutes in which the children counted and recorded these in their own stories."},{"k":"barber1977","apa":"Barber, R. M., & Kagey, J. R. (1977). Modification of school attendance for an elementary population. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 41–48.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-41","y":1977,"sum":"All 212 children in grades 1 to 3 of a small-town Virginia elementary school whose attendance had ranked last or next to last among the county's 10 elementary schools. From January to April, attendance ran 3.97 to 6.07 percentage points above the school's average for the same months of the four prior years, which the authors equated to roughly 180 to 275 extra student-days a month, and by April the school ranked first of the county's 10 elementary…"},{"k":"barnard1977","apa":"Barnard, J. D., Christophersen, E. R., & Wolf, M. M. (1977). Teaching children appropriate shopping behavior through parent training in the supermarket setting. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 49–59.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-49","y":1977,"sum":"Three boys aged 5 and 6, whose families were already in a parent-training program, were taught by their mothers to stay within reach and to leave store products alone during ordinary trips to the family's supermarket (about 15 minutes each)."},{"k":"barrish1969","apa":"Barrish, H. H., Saunders, M., & Wolf, M. M. (1969). Good behavior game: Effects of individual contingencies for group consequences on disruptive behavior in a classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(2), 119–124.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-119","y":1969,"sum":"A fourth-grade class of 24, including seven students often sent to the principal, was split into two teams; the teacher marked the board whenever a team member talked without permission or left a seat without permission, and any team with five or fewer marks (or the fewest) earned victory tags, stars, lining up first for lunch and 30 minutes of free time for projects."},{"k":"barron2019","apa":"Barron, B. F., Verkuylen, L., Belisle, J., Paliliunas, D., & Dixon, M. R. (2019). Teaching \"then-later\" and \"here-there\" relations to children with autism: An evaluation of single reversals and transformation of stimulus function. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 167–175.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0216-1","y":2019,"sum":"Two 6-year-olds with autism at a clinic were first taught to point to the then and later (or here and there) picture of a pair, with praise and a gesture-then-verbal prompt after errors, then taught the swapped question for the first half and tested on the second half without feedback. Both mastered each step in 3 to 5 six-trial blocks, answered untrained picture sets at 100%, and answered the swapped question about their own drawings at 67% and 100%."},{"k":"barton1970","apa":"Barton, E. S. (1970). Inappropriate speech in a severely retarded child: A case study in language conditioning and generalization. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(4), 299–307.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-299","y":1970,"sum":"An 11-year-old boy with profound intellectual disability from a birth injury, living in a state institution, was shown magazine pictures and asked one simple who, what or where question about each, 50 per session, with praise and a candy for a relevant answer and a 10-second timeout (magazine closed, adult turned away) for an irrelevant one."},{"k":"bassette2018","apa":"Bassette, L., Kulwicki, J., Dieringer, S. T., Zoder-Martell, K. A., & Heneisen, R. (2018). The use of a multicomponent behavioral intervention to promote physical activity in adolescents with autism spectrum disorders across inclusive community settings. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 358–369.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00285-7","y":2018,"sum":"Three young adults aged 18–21 with autism learned squats, hip extensions and a dumbbell exercise at home from an app's video models, with least-to-most prompts on missed steps, a rep count that rose by 2 after each criterion, and a chosen activity after the session. Independent steps rose from 17–54% to 91–97%, and the skills held at a YMCA and at a community gym where the students chained the exercises into their own workout."},{"k":"beahm2023","apa":"Beahm, L. A., Ingvarsson, E. T., Funk, N., Haskins, L., & Frazier, J. (2023). Using an app-based token economy to increase engagement in daily living and vocational tasks with adults with developmental disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(4), 1047–1060.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-023-00774-4","y":2023,"sum":"Six adults with autism or Down syndrome at a day program, described as prompt-dependent, earned app points on a fixed ratio for each unit of a task box, exchanged at the end of 10 min for snacks or activities priced by preference. Engagement rose from 19–44% to 51–79% of intervals and stayed above baseline up to 8 weeks later."},{"k":"belisle2023","apa":"Belisle, J., Burke, R., Janota, T., Dennis, L., & Taylor, S. (2023). Promoting the emergence of vocational knowledge through equivalence-based instruction with a young adult with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(4), 1216–1221.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-023-00814-z","y":2023,"sum":"A 17-year-old with autism in a day-treatment school program was taught photo-to-title and title-to-duty matching with praise and a second-chance error correction. After mastery at 90% over three blocks, the untaught photo-to-duty and duty-to-photo relations emerged at 75–100% across three work sites."},{"k":"bennett1972","apa":"Bennett, C. W., & Ling, D. (1972). Teaching a complex verbal response to a hearing-impaired girl. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 321–327.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-321","y":1972,"sum":"A 3-year-old girl with severe hearing loss who wore two hearing aids and spoke in nouns and verbs only was taught, in a small clinic room, to describe action pictures in the form \"the (noun) is (verb)-ing\", first adding \"the\" and then \"is\", by imitating a spoken model and then when asked to tell about the picture, with praise and tokens exchanged for candy for each complete sentence and a model to repeat after errors."},{"k":"bennett1973","apa":"Bennett, P. S., & Maley, R. F. (1973). Modification of interactive behaviors in chronic mental patients. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 609–620.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-609","y":1973,"sum":"Four women with long-term schizophrenia on a state hospital token unit were split into an experimental pair, who over ten 30-minute sessions earned ward tokens for timed stretches of talking, then attending to the speaker, then asking each other questions and giving fitting answers, then completing question-and-answer exchanges, and a control pair given the same instructions and tokens regardless of what they did."},{"k":"bennett1974","apa":"Bennett, C. W. (1974). Articulation training of two hearing-impaired girls. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 439–445.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-439","y":1974,"sum":"Two 4-year-old girls with severe and profound hearing loss, who wore hearing aids, were taught in daily 100-trial sessions first to imitate the word fox and then to say it when shown its picture and asked what it was, and later the same for shop, earning praise and a token for each correct first try (25 tokens bought a candy) while errors were simply recorded."},{"k":"betancourt1971","apa":"Betancourt, F. W., & Zeiler, M. D. (1971). The choices and preferences of nursery school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(4), 299–304.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-299","y":1971,"sum":"Twenty-four preschoolers aged 4 and 5 named, each day from a picture board of 10 classroom jobs, the three jobs they wanted and the three they did not want, and earned tokens that bought afternoon activities such as art, blocks or cooking. After a phase in which every job paid the same, tokens were paid only for choosing the jobs a child had liked least (or, for one control group, liked most), then equal pay returned and the shift was repeated."},{"k":"betz2013","apa":"Betz, A. M., Fisher, W. W., Roane, H. S., Mintz, J. C., & Owen, T. M. (2013). A component analysis of schedule thinning during functional communication training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 46(1), 219–241.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.23","y":2013,"sum":"Requests are answered in one signaled period and not in the other, and the rule is told to the student; the periods alternate. Once requests come mostly in the \"available\" period, it is shortened step by step. This let teachers thin the answers to requests without problem behavior returning, and worked classwide in preschools."},{"k":"biberdorf1977","apa":"Biberdorf, J. R., & Pear, J. J. (1977). Two-to-one versus one-to-one student-teacher ratios in the operant verbal training of retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 506.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-506","y":1977,"sum":"Two children with intellectual disability in a residential center were taught to name pictures in a standard prompt-and-probe naming routine, with primary reinforcers delivered on a fixed-ratio schedule for correct answers. In a group condition the teacher switched between the two children after each reinforcer, error, omission or 10 seconds without a trial-starting response; in an individual condition she taught one child alone, and a second experiment…"},{"k":"bird1991","apa":"Bird, F., Hepburn, S., Rhodes, K., & Moniz, D. (1991). Multiple reinforcement contingencies to reduce aggression, self-injury and dysfunctional verbal behaviors in an adult who is sensory impaired. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(5), 367–383.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060506","y":1991,"sum":"One 26-year-old blind man with moderate intellectual disability in a staffed community apartment who hit, kicked and scratched staff, bit himself, ate inedible items, made himself gag, tore clothing and threw objects, mainly to escape less preferred tasks."},{"k":"black2016","apa":"Black, M. P., Skinner, C. H., Forbes, B. E., McCurdy, M., Coleman, M. B., Davis, K., & Gettelfinger, M. (2016). Cumulative instructional time and relative effectiveness conclusions: Extending research on response intervals, learning, and measurement scale. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(1), 58–62.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0114-3","y":2016,"sum":"Two 10-year-olds with functional delays in a self-contained classroom practiced unknown sight words on computer flashcards that showed each word for 1, 3 or 5 s before a recording read it; the student tried to read it first and then repeated it, three times per word each session. A word counted as learned after it was read within 2 s on two consecutive tests."},{"k":"bloh2025","apa":"Bloh, C., Bacon, L., Begel, B., Madara, K., & Koller, B. (2025). Comparing human video modeling to animated video modeling for learners with autism. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 41(2), 262–279.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-025-00224-y","y":2025,"sum":"Eight students with autism, ages 5–8, in self-contained classrooms watched a 40–60 s human or animated video in which an adult answered three prompts with a word, a facial expression and a gesture, then were given the same prompts by the teacher with no prompts or feedback. Three students reached 6 of 9 with both video types in 4–18 sessions, two with one type only, and three with neither; maintenance after 3 weeks was mixed."},{"k":"bloomfield2019","apa":"Bloomfield, B. S., Fischer, A. J., Clark, R. R., & Dove, M. B. (2019). Treatment of food selectivity in a child with avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder through parent teleconsultation. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 33–43.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0251-y","y":2019,"sum":"For children with established but narrow eating, procedures that work without escape extinction include praise or a preferred food for each accepted bite, preferred items available through the meal, the new food presented together with a preferred one, a quick sequence of easy requests (empty spoon presentations) before a bite, and bite size or texture faded in gradually."},{"k":"bohan2023","apa":"Bohan, C., & Smyth, S. (2023). The effect of schedule thinning on student behavior during the Caught Being Good Game. Behavior Modification, 47(3), 644–669.","doi":"10.1177/01454455221129993","y":2023,"sum":"Teachers in four elementary classrooms added a group contingency to the school’s warning system: tickets for following the rules, a lottery for small prizes, rewards that depended on the whole class, and a chart where each student recorded the tickets earned. Engaged time rose and inappropriate behavior fell for all twelve at-risk students aged 7 to 9."},{"k":"boles2008","apa":"Boles, R. E., Roberts, M. C., & Vernberg, E. M. (2008). Treating non-retentive encopresis with rewarded scheduled toilet visits. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(2), 68–72.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391730","y":2008,"sum":"For an elementary student with a serious emotional disturbance, scheduled toilet visits earned tokens toward a menu of prizes, and accidents were cleaned up without the attention they had drawn before; a team ran it at school and the grandmother at home. Toilet use rose and soiling fell in both places."},{"k":"bolstad1972","apa":"Bolstad, O. D., & Johnson, S. M. (1972). Self-regulation in the modification of disruptive classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(4), 443–454.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-443","y":1972,"sum":"The most disruptive children in 10 combined first- and second-grade public school classes were taught to keep their talk-outs, hitting and leaving their desks low during 30 minutes of independent seatwork by earning points for small school prizes, and some were then taught to tally these behaviors on a card, first checked against an observer's count and then used alone to award their own points."},{"k":"bondy1976","apa":"Bondy, A. S., & Erickson, M. T. (1976). Comparison of modelling and reinforcement procedures in increasing question-asking of mildly retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 108.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-108","y":1976,"sum":"Four groups of four children with mild intellectual disability (mean age 10) in two public elementary schools were prompted to ask questions about large photographs, and each question was answered at once. Over an ABAB design with six sessions per phase, one group earned a point per question, traded after the session for items such as candy and ice cream, one heard an adult model four questions per photograph, one got both, and a control group got the…"},{"k":"bord2017","apa":"Bord, S., Sidener, T. M., Reeve, K. F., & Sidener, D. W. (2017). Teaching on-task rollerblading and ice-skating to a child with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(2), 178–182.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0150-z","y":2017,"sum":"Colby, a 6-year-old with autism who could already move forward on skates, was taught to rollerblade on task around a cone route in a school gym and parks set up to resemble an ice rink; the adult's physical and nearness prompts were faded over eight levels, extra cones were removed, and snacks dropped into a cup (a sound he had learned to wait for) came less often."},{"k":"bornstein1976","apa":"Bornstein, P. H., & Quevillon, R. P. (1976). The effects of a self-instructional package on overactive preschool boys. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 179–188.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-179","y":1976,"sum":"Three 4-year-old boys in a Head Start class, referred for disruptive, overactive and inattentive behavior, each had one 2-hour session of self-instruction training away from class: an adult talked aloud through copying, block and sorting tasks, and the child moved in steps from doing the task while the adult said the words to saying them aloud, whispering, mouthing and finally thinking them, with a few candies paired with self-praise and quickly faded."},{"k":"bornstein1977","apa":"Bornstein, M. R., Bellack, A. S., & Hersen, M. (1977). Social-skills training for unassertive children: A multiple-baseline analysis. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 183–195.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-183","y":1977,"sum":"Four children aged 8 to 11 at a university elementary school, referred by teachers as passive and unable to stand up for themselves with peers, practised responding to nine role-played school conflicts (someone cutting in line, taking their seat or turn) with two adult actors."},{"k":"bouck2023","apa":"Bouck, E. C., & Long, H. (2023). Online delivery of a manipulative-based intervention package for finding equivalent fractions. Journal of Behavioral Education, 32(2), 313–333.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-021-09449-y","y":2023,"sum":"Three upper-elementary students (one with PDD-NOS, two at risk in math) were taught online in 30-min sessions: the teacher modeled one problem aloud, guided one, and then the student solved five, with a 10-s wait and least-to-most verbal prompts on each step, first with a fraction-strip app and then with numbers only."},{"k":"bourret2004","apa":"Bourret, J., Vollmer, T. R., & Rapp, J. T. (2004). Evaluation of a vocal mand assessment and vocal mand training procedures. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 37(2), 129–144.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2004.37-129","y":2004,"sum":"The request is taught to produce the item the problem behavior used to produce, honored at once at first; the item no longer follows problem behavior. The prompt is faded, then the delay between request and item is lengthened (see Wait for a requested item)."},{"k":"braam1990","apa":"Braam, C., & Malott, R. W. (1990). “I’ll do it when the snow melts”: The effects of deadlines and delayed outcomes on rule-governed behavior in preschool children. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 8, 67–76.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392848","y":1990,"sum":"Eight typically developing children aged 3½ to 4 in a co-op preschool were approached one at a time during free play and told to pick up toys or put together a puzzle, with spoken rules that did or did not state a deadline (now) and a reward (a pick from a box of stickers and small toys) given right after finishing or one week later."},{"k":"braam1991","apa":"Braam, S. J., & Sundberg, M. L. (1991). The effects of specific versus nonspecific reinforcement on verbal behavior. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 9, 19–28.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392857","y":1991,"sum":"Eight young adults aged 18 to 24 with severe intellectual disability and almost no communication, in a special-education center, were taught in matched pairs to sign the names of foods when shown a piece and asked “What is this?”, with a modeled sign and then hand-over-hand help, until they signed without prompts on at least 90% of trials in three sessions in a row (or 25 sessions had passed)."},{"k":"breeman2025","apa":"Breeman, S. L., Vladescu, J. C., Sidener, T. M., DeBar, R. M., & Gureghian, D. (2025). Generalized target behavior reductions and maintenance of effects following an augmented competing stimulus assessment sequence. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 58(3), 573–594.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70021","y":2025,"sum":"When problem behavior is maintained by its own sensory consequences, items that give a similar sensation compete with it best; engaging with them is prompted and praised, and the items are rotated."},{"k":"brewer2014","apa":"Brewer, A. T., Strickland-Cohen, K., Dotson, W., & Williams, D. C. (2014). Advance notice for transition-related problem behavior: Practice guidelines. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 117–125.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0014-3","y":2014,"sum":"A review with practice guidelines on advance notice (spoken warnings, timers, activity schedules) for problem behavior at transitions in people with intellectual and developmental disabilities, including children with autism at school."},{"k":"breyer1975","apa":"Breyer, N. L., & Allen, G. J. (1975). Effects of implementing a token economy on teacher attending behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 373–380.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-373","y":1975,"sum":"A transitional first-grade class of 15 students held back from second grade for academic and social difficulties, with mostly passive off-task behavior and some poking and hitting during a 48-minute reading period, and a veteran teacher who reprimanded far more than she praised."},{"k":"brigham1968","apa":"Brigham, T. A., & Sherman, J. A. (1968). An experimental analysis of verbal imitation in preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(2), 151–158.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-151","y":1968,"sum":"Three 4-year-old boys with typical language at a university preschool were asked to repeat words in sessions of about 20 minutes; each clear repetition of an English word earned candy or a token (traded for a toy) plus praise, while Russian words mixed into the list were never rewarded. Phases compared rewarding English repetitions, rewarding only when no repetition had just occurred, and handing out rewards with the English words whatever the child did."},{"k":"brigham1972a","apa":"Brigham, T. A., Finfrock, S. R., Breunig, M. K., & Bushell, D., Jr. (1972). The use of programmed materials in the analysis of academic contingencies. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(2), 177–182.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-177","y":1972,"sum":"Six kindergartners aged 5 worked through a 32-step handwriting program (lines drawn inside tube shapes, then dashed lines, then dashed lines between guidelines, then capital letters), moving up a step after one sheet at 80% correct, while tokens with praise were given either for correct items or all at once at the start of the session."},{"k":"brigham1972b","apa":"Brigham, T. A., Graubard, P. S., & Stans, A. (1972). Analysis of the effects of sequential reinforcement contingencies on aspects of composition. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(4), 421–429.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-421","y":1972,"sum":"Thirteen fifth-grade boys in a special class for students with academic failure and behavior problems, more than two years behind, wrote stories about twice a week on topics the teacher introduced, earning class points (traded for privileges, chiefly arts and crafts) first for sitting and working, then for each word written, then also for each different word and each word they had never used before (checked against an alphabetized list of their own past…"},{"k":"briscoe1975","apa":"Briscoe, R. V., Hoffman, D. B., & Bailey, J. S. (1975). Behavioral community psychology: Training a community board to problem solve. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 157–168.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-157","y":1975,"sum":"Nine members of a rural low-income community policy board, aged 15 to 69 (two were 15-year-old eighth graders), were taught one at a time in 30-minute sessions before weekly meetings to state the problem, offer solutions and ask what action would be taken, by whom, where, when and how."},{"k":"broden1970","apa":"Broden, M., Bruce, C., Mitchell, M. A., Carter, V., & Hall, R. V. (1970). Effects of teacher attention on attending behavior of two boys at adjacent desks. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(3), 199–203.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-205","y":1970,"sum":"Two second-grade boys at adjacent desks in a school in a low-income neighborhood, with measured IQs of 60 and 72 and considered the most disruptive in the class, received teacher praise and attention while they were attending to spelling and writing work (first one boy, then the other, then neither, then both), with off-task behavior ignored."},{"k":"broden1971","apa":"Broden, M., Hall, R. V., & Mitts, B. (1971). The effect of self-recording on the classroom behavior of two eighth-grade students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(3), 191–199.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-191","y":1971,"sum":"An eighth-grade girl doing poorly in history marked a plus or minus on a slip from her counselor whenever she remembered, to show whether she had been studying, and later her teacher added praise for studying; an eighth-grade boy tallied his own call-outs in math. An observer recorded study and call-outs in 10-second intervals, with reliability checks in each phase, in reversal designs."},{"k":"broden1977","apa":"Broden, M., Copeland, G., Beasley, A., & Hall, R. V. (1977). Altering student responses through changes in teacher verbal behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 479–487.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-479","y":1977,"sum":"Seven boys in an inner-city junior high special education class (placed there partly for limited spoken language) took part in a daily 25-minute class discussion. In one experiment the teacher asked more new questions and questions needing more than a one-word answer; in the second she told the class before each discussion to answer in sentences, paused about 5 seconds after a non-sentence answer, and then asked the class who could give a sentence answer…"},{"k":"brody1978","apa":"Brody, G. H., Lahey, B. B., & Combs, M. L. (1978). Effects of intermittent modelling on observational learning. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(1), 87–90.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-87","y":1978,"sum":"Ten 4-year-olds in a Head Start class took turns with an adult describing boxes of small toys; before any modeling, nine of them used almost no color or number words. For five children the adult described his own boxes with a color for every toy and a number for each group, and four of these children began using such words at about his rate on their next boxes, though their toys, colors and counts were different and nothing was given for it."},{"k":"bruhn2015","apa":"Bruhn, A., McDaniel, S., & Kreigh, C. (2015). Self-monitoring interventions for students with behavior problems: A systematic review of current research. Behavioral Disorders, 40(2), 102–121.","doi":"10.17988/BD-13-45.1","y":2015,"sum":"The student is taught what working looks like, then to mark the card at each cue; at first an adult marks too and the two compare, and agreement earns a small reward. The cues are spaced further apart as on-task behavior holds. Self-monitoring is among the best-supported classroom interventions for students with behavior problems."},{"k":"bryan2000","apa":"Bryan, L. C., & Gast, D. L. (2000). Teaching on-task and on-schedule behaviors to high-functioning children with autism via picture activity schedules. Journal of Autism and Developmental Disorders, 30(6), 553–567.","doi":"10.1023/A:1005687310346","y":2000,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to follow photo schedules with manual guidance from behind, faded gradually (graduated guidance), so that no adult prompt became part of the chain; they kept following them when the activities were reordered and new pictures were added."},{"k":"bucher1977","apa":"Bucher, B., & Mueller, H. H. (1977). Acquisition and generalization of compliance with complex five-component instructions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 507.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-507","y":1977,"sum":"A 7-year-old bilingual boy of typical intelligence, judged by his school to have trouble carrying out complex requests, was first taught to identify each object and action, then to carry out instructions of one, two, three, four and finally five parts (an action, an object, a position, a second object and a color), with three interchangeable words for each part and every possible response available on each trial."},{"k":"buell1968","apa":"Buell, J., Stoddard, P., Harris, F. R., & Baer, D. M. (1968). Collateral social development accompanying reinforcement of outdoor play in a preschool child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(2), 167–173.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-167","y":1968,"sum":"A 3-year-old girl in a university preschool, whose family had recently lived abroad and who rarely used the outdoor equipment or played with other children, received close teacher attention and approval whenever she used outdoor play equipment; for the first nine days teachers also placed her on one new piece of equipment each day for at least 30 seconds, and attention was later thinned and twice given for a few days regardless of what she did."},{"k":"burgess1971","apa":"Burgess, R. L., Clark, R. N., & Hendee, J. C. (1971). An experimental analysis of anti-litter procedures. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 71–75.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-71","y":1971,"sum":"Children at Saturday matinees in two neighborhood movie theaters (average audiences of about 160 and 220) were given, on different days, litterbags, litterbags plus a spoken instruction, extra trash cans, an anti-litter cartoon, or a litterbag plus a dime or a free movie ticket for each bag of litter turned in. The measure was the percent of the theater's total litter weight that ended up in trash cans, compared with baseline days in a reversal design."},{"k":"bushell1968","apa":"Bushell, D., Jr., Wrobel, P. A., & Michaelis, M. L. (1968). Applying \"group\" contingencies to the classroom study behavior of preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(1), 55–61.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-55","y":1968,"sum":"Twelve typically developing children aged 3 to 6 in a summer preschool class earned plastic tokens and praise from teachers who moved around the room during independent activities, a group Spanish lesson and peer study teams, and spent the tokens on a ticket to a daily 30-minute special event and on snack. Over 20 days the event was first earned, then handed out free while tokens were still given, then earned again."},{"k":"byra2018","apa":"Byra, K. L., White, S., Temple, M., & Cameron, M. J. (2018). An approach to cleanliness training to support bathroom hygiene among children with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(2), 139–143.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0205-9","y":2018,"sum":"Two boys aged 5 and 6 with autism were first taught to name and to point to clean versus dirty toilet paper with cards carrying graded smears of sunflower-seed butter, then practiced the whole routine on a doll while following a written script with a picture flowchart, with praise after each step and a small edible after the chain. Mastery on the doll took 26 and 55 sessions."},{"k":"callahan2022","apa":"Callahan, R., Kail, B., Lerman, D. C., Johnson-Patagoc, K., & Whitcher, D. (2022). Teaching adults with neurodevelopmental disabilities to interact successfully with others in a virtual format. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(4), 1279–1295.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-022-00681-0","y":2022,"sum":"Four adults with autism or intellectual disability in a weekly online social group were taught with behavioral skills training (rationale, models, practice with feedback), timed feedback and a token for meeting the goal. Encouraging statements rose from 0–5% to 15–30% of intervals, fell when tokens stopped, and came back when peers were reinforced for acknowledging the comments."},{"k":"cammilleri2008","apa":"Cammilleri, A. P., Tiger, J. H., & Hanley, G. P. (2008). Developing stimulus control of young children's requests to teachers: Classwide applications of multiple schedules. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 41(2), 299–303.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2008.41-299","y":2008,"sum":"Requests are answered in one signaled period and not in the other, and the rule is told to the student; the periods alternate. Once requests come mostly in the \"available\" period, it is shortened step by step. This let teachers thin the answers to requests without problem behavior returning, and worked classwide in preschools."},{"k":"campbell2024","apa":"Campbell, V. E., Higbee, T. S., Osos, J. A., Lindgren, N. A., & Ceriano, L. B. (2024). A comparison of telehealth-based instruction with or without instructive feedback. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 40(2), 99–117.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-023-00185-0","y":2024,"sum":"Two 10-year-olds with intellectual delays in a self-contained class practiced unknown words on a computer: each word appeared, a recording then read it, and the student tried to say it first and repeated it after, three times per word each session. Words read within 2 s on two tests in a row were counted as learned and swapped for new ones, and both students learned far more practiced than unpracticed words."},{"k":"cantrell1969","apa":"Cantrell, R. P., Cantrell, M. L., Huddleston, C. M., & Wooldridge, R. L. (1969). Contingency contracting with school problems. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(3), 215–220.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-215","y":1969,"sum":"Students in grades 1 to 11 referred for problems such as running away from school, refusing to attend or not finishing work were given written contracts, drafted by clinic staff from parent and teacher interviews, under which parents and teachers gave points for listed school and home behaviors (for one girl, each step of getting up, dressing, boarding the bus and staying in class) that the student traded for activities and privileges already available."},{"k":"carnine1976","apa":"Carnine, D. W. (1976). Effects of two teacher-presentation rates on off-task behavior, answering correctly, and participation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 199–206.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-199","y":1976,"sum":"Two low-achieving first graders in a four-child Distar beginning reading group were taught for 30 minutes a day by a teacher who either moved to the next item at once after the group answered (fast) or paused about 5 seconds first (slow), with general praise held steady by a reminder tone every 90 seconds."},{"k":"carr1978","apa":"Carr, E. G., Binkoff, J. A., Kologinsky, E., & Eddy, M. (1978). Acquisition of sign language by autistic children. I: Expressive labelling. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(4), 489–501.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-489","y":1978,"sum":"Four non-speaking adolescents with autism, ages 10 to 15, in a residential hospital program were taught the signs for five foods one at a time: the adult held up the object, said its name, molded the student's hands into the sign and faded the help, with praise and a piece of the food for correct signs, and mastered signs were mixed with the new sign 1 to 3."},{"k":"carr1985","apa":"Carr, E. G., & Durand, V. M. (1985). Reducing behavior problems through functional communication training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 18(2), 111–126.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1985.18-111","y":1985,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"carroll1987","apa":"Carroll, R. J., & Hesse, B. E. (1987). The effects of alternating mand and tact training on the acquisition of tacts. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 5, 55–65.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392820","y":1987,"sum":"Thirteen preschoolers with autism had 13 weeks of referent-based instruction at a university center: 9 minutes of play-based teaching around an item the child chose, then a 1-minute probe with the cue cards shuffled. The child first learned to ask for the item, with control moved over from echoing by quick runs of easy prompts and errorless help, then to label it and to fill in a spoken sentence about how it is used; the cards missed on the probe set the…"},{"k":"caruthers2015","apa":"Caruthers, C. E., Lambert, J. M., Chazin, K. M., Harbin, E. R., & Houchins-Juarez, N. J. (2015). Latency-based FA as baseline for subsequent treatment evaluation. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 8(1), 48–51.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0046-3","y":2015,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"cengher2016","apa":"Cengher, M., Shamoun, K., Moss, P., Roll, D., Feliciano, G., & Fienup, D. M. (2016). A comparison of the effects of two prompt-fading strategies on skill acquisition in children with autism spectrum disorders. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(2), 115–125.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0096-6","y":2016,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"chagolla2022","apa":"Chagolla, G. A., & Penrod, B. (2022). Decreasing number of food portions consumed by an adolescent female with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(1), 318–323.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-021-00647-8","y":2022,"sum":"A 17-year-old with autism agreed each week to a maximum number of servings of pizza or chips at dinner and a treat at the week's end if every meal met the goal, after a 10-min lesson on serving sizes. She wrote the food, the servings and whether she met her goal in a notebook kept at the table while an adult ate alongside without comment."},{"k":"chapman1974","apa":"Chapman, C., & Risley, T. R. (1974). Anti-litter procedures in an urban high-density area. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 377–383.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-377","y":1974,"sum":"Children aged 4 to 13 living in a low-income city housing project (132 took part) were, in turn, asked to help pick up litter, paid 10 cents per full bag of litter, or assigned particular yards and paid 10 to 40 cents only after an adult checked that no litter 2 inches or larger was left."},{"k":"charlop2002","apa":"Charlop-Christy, M. H., Carpenter, M., Le, L., LeBlanc, L. A., & Kellet, K. (2002). Using the picture exchange communication system (PECS) with children with autism: Assessment of PECS acquisition, speech, social-communicative behavior, and problem behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 35(3), 213–231.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2002.35-213","y":2002,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"chastain2026","apa":"Chastain, A. N., & Dixon, M. R. (2026). Using equivalence-based instruction to teach value-congruent action identification to autistic teens. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 19(3), 1413–1419.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-025-01152-y","y":2026,"sum":"Three teenagers with autism each chose three values and were taught, with praise, a varied 'no' for errors and verbal prompts after two consecutive errors, to answer what will happen if they do an action and which value that outcome fits. Two then answered the untaught value-to-action question at mastery after 4 and 7 blocks; the third needed direct feedback on two of three values before the third emerged."},{"k":"christophersen1972","apa":"Christophersen, E. R., Arnold, C. M., Hill, D. W., & Quilitch, H. R. (1972). The home point system: Token reinforcement procedures for application by parents of children with behavior problems. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(4), 485–497.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-485","y":1972,"sum":"Two sets of parents, coached for about 10 hours, ran point systems at home for five children aged 5 to 10 (one an 8-year-old girl with mild cerebral palsy in a class for students with mild intellectual disability), with written definitions of daily chores such as making the bed, emptying the trash and feeding a pet, points spent on everyday privileges, and point fines for bickering, whining and teasing; the second family also compared feedback slips, gold…"},{"k":"christy1975","apa":"Christy, P. R. (1975). Does use of tangible rewards with individual children affect peer observers?. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 187–196.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-187","y":1975,"sum":"In two small preschool groups of children aged about 3 to 6 referred for mild behavior problems, three children in each group in turn were told during a daily 20-minute clay activity that being seated when a tone sounded (on average once a minute) would earn a small treat such as candy, a raisin or a nut."},{"k":"cicero2002","apa":"Cicero, F. R., & Pfadt, A. (2002). Investigation of a reinforcement-based toilet training procedure for children with autism. Research in Developmental Disabilities, 23(5), 319–331.","doi":"10.1016/S0891-4222(02)00136-1","y":2002,"sum":"Scheduled visits (sits of a few minutes on a timer), extra fluids, immediate reinforcement for each void in the toilet and a brief, neutral response to accidents; the time between visits grows as the student stays dry, and then the student is taught to ask. School staff ran this in public school programs."},{"k":"cifuentes2026","apa":"Cifuentes, J., Goyal, M., & Shawler, L. A. (2026). Effects of antecedent and consequence secondary targets on intraverbal teaching. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 42(1), 1–25.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-026-00229-1","y":2026,"sum":"Children aged 4 to 11 with severe intellectual disability or autism in a residential centre, who could repeat words but named few pictures, learned one new picture at a time: they repeated its name after the adult and then named it on their own, with the new picture mixed among three they already knew, and it counted as learned when they named it at the start of three later sessions."},{"k":"cihak2008","apa":"Cihak, D. F., & Grim, J. (2008). Teaching students with autism spectrum disorder and moderate intellectual disabilities to use counting-on strategies to enhance independent purchasing skills. Research in Autism Spectrum Disorders, 2(4), 716–727.","doi":"10.1016/j.rasd.2008.02.006","y":2008,"sum":"Young adults with autism were first taught to buy items in one store, and the skill did not carry over to other stores; adding videotapes of people buying items in those stores, with questions about what they did, produced purchasing in all of them. Students with autism and intellectual disability learned to pay on their own with a counting-on, next-dollar strategy, used it in community stores and kept it for weeks."},{"k":"cihon2019","apa":"Cihon, J. H., Ferguson, J. L., Milne, C. M., Leaf, J. B., McEachin, J., & Leaf, R. (2019). A preliminary evaluation of a token system with a flexible earning requirement. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(3), 548–556.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00316-3","y":2019,"sum":"Three children aged 4 to 6 with autism who rarely commented at snack earned a tally mark on a dry-erase board for every comment and, at the end of the 3-minute snack, could pick a small toy from a treasure chest if their marks met or beat a 'magic number' the adult chose on the spot. Comments rose from near zero to steady, increasing rates for all three, and the number of new comments grew as well."},{"k":"cihon2019b","apa":"Cihon, J. H., Ferguson, J. L., Leaf, J. B., Leaf, R., McEachin, J., & Taubman, M. (2019). Use of a level system with flexible shaping to improve synchronous engagement. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 44–51.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0254-8","y":2019,"sum":"A 5-year-old girl in a university preschool spent much of free play near other children but rarely played with them, and often taunted them, turned down invitations or knocked over materials. When teachers gave her close attention and approval for about 80% of the session no matter what she was doing, cooperative play stayed under 5% of the day; when the same attention came only after cooperative play (other children were prompted to invite her, and at…"},{"k":"cihon2022","apa":"Cihon, J. H., Ferguson, J. L., Lee, M., Leaf, J. B., Leaf, R., & McEachin, J. (2022). Evaluating the Cool Versus Not Cool procedure via telehealth. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(1), 260–268.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-021-00553-z","y":2022,"sum":"A child and two adolescents with autism were taught greeting and conversation skills chosen with their parents, with behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback); the skills carried over to unscripted talks with new adults. Adolescents were also taught in small groups with the teaching interaction procedure; carry-over to typical peers was partial, so plan practice with them."},{"k":"clark1972","apa":"Clark, R. N., Burgess, R. L., & Hendee, J. C. (1972). The development of anti-litter behavior in a forest campground. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 1–5.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-1","y":1972,"sum":"Twenty-six children aged 6 to 14 from seven families camping in a large national forest campground were each given a big plastic bag, pointed to a general area, and offered their choice of a small item (a patch, badge, comic, ruler, pin or gum) for a bag of litter handed in that evening."},{"k":"clark1975a","apa":"Clark, H. B., Boyd, S. B., & Macrae, J. W. (1975). A classroom program teaching disadvantaged youths to write biographic information. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(1), 67–75.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-67","y":1975,"sum":"Six youths aged 14 to 16 with mild intellectual disability or a history of delinquency, in a prevocational class at a residential center, were taught one at a time to write nine items asked for on job applications (printed name, cursive signature, address, phone number, birth date, the date, and a reference’s name, address and job)."},{"k":"clark1975b","apa":"Clark, H. B., & Sherman, J. A. (1975). Teaching generative use of sentence answers to three forms of questions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(3), 321–330.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-321","y":1975,"sum":"Three residents of a state institution aged 15 to 17 with intellectual disability (one blind, one with Down syndrome) and four preschoolers aged 3 to 4 from low-income families, all with limited speech, were taught in individual 20- to 30-minute sessions to answer three kinds of question about a named job with a complete sentence in the past tense, the future (will) or the -ing form."},{"k":"clark1977","apa":"Clark, H. B., Greene, B. F., Macrae, J. W., McNees, M. P., Davis, J. L., & Risley, T. R. (1977). A parent advice package for family shopping trips: Development and evaluation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 605–624.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-605","y":1977,"sum":"Twelve families with children aged 4 to 12 (two group-home families, then ten families from the community) took 15- to 32-minute shopping trips on which the parent reviewed four store rules beforehand, held back a nickel of a 50-cent shopping allowance for each rule broken, and talked with the children about the shopping, first with experimenter guidance and then from a written advice package with quizzes and short practice trips."},{"k":"clarke2019","apa":"Clarke, S., & Duda, M. A. (2019). PBS goes to middle school: Building capacity of peer buddies to implement a PBS intervention with fidelity. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 204–208.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0253-9","y":2019,"sum":"A 13-year-old girl with autism and multiple disabilities who hit, screamed, dropped to the floor and ran off during a 45-min PE routine was supported by six trained peer buddies who gave a clear cue, showed a picture schedule, walked at her pace, offered preferred items and praised throughout. Engagement rose from a mean of 36% to 90% of intervals and challenging behavior fell from 69% to 13%."},{"k":"cocchiola2012","apa":"Cocchiola, M. A., Jr., Martino, G. M., Dwyer, L. J., & Demezzo, K. (2012). Toilet training children with autism and developmental delays: An effective program for school settings. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 5(2), 60–64.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391824","y":2012,"sum":"Scheduled visits (sits of a few minutes on a timer), extra fluids, immediate reinforcement for each void in the toilet and a brief, neutral response to accidents; the time between visits grows as the student stays dry, and then the student is taught to ask. School staff ran this in public school programs."},{"k":"coleman1970","apa":"Coleman, R. (1970). A conditioning technique applicable to elementary school classrooms. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(4), 293–297.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-293","y":1970,"sum":"Four public elementary students aged 8 to 12 (grades 1, 2, 2 and 5) referred for aggression, tantrums and roaming the classroom earned a click on a small desk counter, about every 10 seconds on average, for working during independent seatwork, and each click earned a piece of candy that was shared equally with the whole class after the session."},{"k":"copeland1974","apa":"Copeland, R. E., Brown, R. E., & Hall, R. V. (1974). The effects of principal-implemented techniques on the behavior of pupils. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 77–86.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-77","y":1974,"sum":"In three experiments in an overcrowded inner-city elementary school, the principal praised three often-absent kindergarten and first-grade children in class on days they came, praised three low-producing fifth-grade boys in his office whenever they met set goals in daily word-reading and 5-minute addition tutoring, and twice a week praised improving and top-scoring children in two third-grade classes (74 pupils) doing timed addition sheets."},{"k":"corey1972","apa":"Corey, J. R., & Shamow, J. (1972). The effects of fading on the acquisition and retention of oral reading. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 311–315.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-311","y":1972,"sum":"Fifty-two typically developing nursery school children aged 4 to 5 who could not read were taught to read six picture words aloud from a screen, either with the picture darkened in four steps behind the printed word until only the word remained or with picture and word shown together five times before the word alone, and in a second study half of each group touched the word before naming it; correct answers lit a clown's nose and brought candy about one…"},{"k":"cossairt1973","apa":"Cossairt, A., Hall, R. V., & Hopkins, B. L. (1973). The effects of experimenter's instructions, feedback, and praise on teacher praise and student attending behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(1), 89–100.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-89","y":1973,"sum":"Three third- and fourth-grade teachers in a low-income area of Kansas City were coached to praise attending, and 12 students they chose for low attending (5 boys, 7 girls) were observed. The consultant's instructions, daily feedback and then feedback plus praise for the teachers' own praise were added in a multiple baseline across teachers."},{"k":"cote2005","apa":"Cote, C. A., Thompson, R. H., & McKerchar, P. M. (2005). The effects of antecedent interventions and extinction on toddlers' compliance during transitions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 38(2), 235–238.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2005.143-04","y":2005,"sum":"Showing the coming transition on a short video just before it (video priming) reduced disruption for children with autism. A warning, a toy to carry or a visual schedule alone did not help in other studies; they worked once moving on earned reinforcement and problem behavior no longer delayed the move."},{"k":"craft1998","apa":"Craft, M. A., Alber, S. R., & Heward, W. L. (1998). Teaching elementary students with developmental disabilities to recruit teacher attention in a general education classroom: Effects on teacher praise and academic productivity. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 31(3), 399–415.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1998.31-399","y":1998,"sum":"Fourth graders with developmental disabilities were taught in a special education room to show their work to the general education teacher and ask for feedback a few times each session; teacher praise, the work completed and its accuracy rose in the general education classroom."},{"k":"craig1970","apa":"Craig, H. B., & Holland, A. L. (1970). Reinforcement of visual attending in classrooms for deaf children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 97–109.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-97","y":1970,"sum":"Three classes of seven children who are deaf (one class aged 9 to 10, two first-year primary classes aged 6 to 7) at a residential school for the deaf were taught to keep looking at the lesson (the teacher presenting, the board or their written work) while seated, through a desk light that flashed after each full 10 seconds of attending and was exchanged for candy, cereal or tokens after the lesson; praise alone was tested in one class."},{"k":"craighead1974","apa":"Craighead, W. E., Mercatoris, M., & Bellack, B. (1974). A brief report on mentally retarded residents as behavioral observers. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 333–340.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-333","y":1974,"sum":"Four women aged 17 to 40 (IQ 49 to 69) living in a state school and hospital, all able to read and write, were trained in pairs to rate other residents on a six-category time-sample checklist, and two undergraduates were trained the same way for comparison."},{"k":"crane1991","apa":"Crane, A. A., & Joyce, B. G. (1991). Brief report: Cool down: A procedure for decreasing aggression in adults with traumatic head injury. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(1), 65–75.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060106","y":1991,"sum":"Two men with traumatic brain injury (24 and 49) in a post-acute residential facility had twice-weekly 1-hour 'cool down' role plays (imagine leaving the upsetting situation, progressive muscle relaxation guided by muscle-tension biofeedback, then naming other ways to cope), while during the day staff prompted the routine after 10 (later 5) seconds of agitation, praised calming and 2–7 minutes of relaxation, and gave points for aggression-free hours and…"},{"k":"croner2018","apa":"Croner, J. S., Smith, S. L., Woods, J. E., Weiss, M. J., & Maguire, H. (2018). A pilot investigation of individual and dyad instructional arrangements. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(2), 115–128.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0234-z","y":2018,"sum":"A 9-year-old girl with intellectual disability, living in an institution and silent for about seven years, was asked twice a day to say words and then phrases of up to four words, earning a bite of a preferred food for each acceptable try; at first silent mouthing and whispers were rewarded, then only audible, clearly spoken repetitions. She came to repeat long phrases accurately in most trials."},{"k":"cuvo1978","apa":"Cuvo, A. J., Leaf, R. B., & Borakove, L. S. (1978). Teaching janitorial skills to the mentally retarded: Acquisition, generalization, and maintenance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 345–355.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-345","y":1978,"sum":"Six 13- to 15-year-olds with moderate intellectual disability (one also with autism) in a public school class learned a 181-step restroom-cleaning routine, one subtask a session, with least-to-most prompts (instruction, then modelling, then graduated guidance) for ordinary steps and most-to-least prompts for 20 hard steps, and praise plus a candy on a thinning schedule."},{"k":"dalrymple1989","apa":"Dalrymple, A. J. (1989). Sensory extinction of stereotyped object-dropping: Identification of a reinforcer for skill training. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(2), 99–111.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040204","y":1989,"sum":"Demands are faded in: the session begins with few demands (sometimes none) and more are added only while problem behavior stays low, until the full assignment is reached. Breaks earned by finishing a set amount of work (response chaining) did the same."},{"k":"dalton1973","apa":"Dalton, A. J., Rubino, C. A., & Hislop, M. W. (1973). Some effects of token rewards on school achievement of children with Down's syndrome. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 251–259.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-251","y":1973,"sum":"Thirteen children with Down syndrome aged 6 to 14 (IQ scores 30 to 64), living at home, were taught early arithmetic (counting to a number, counting claps and objects, naming numerals, equality, counting on) and early language in scripted small-group DISTAR lessons during an 8-week summer program, three half-days a week."},{"k":"danforth2016","apa":"Danforth, J. S. (2016). A flow chart of behavior management strategies for families of children with co-occurring attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder and conduct problem behavior. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(1), 64–76.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0103-6","y":2016,"sum":"This tutorial presents a flow chart that parents of children with ADHD and conduct problems are taught to follow through explanation, modeling and role-play: decide whether a direction must be followed, give a clear direction, praise the child as soon as compliance begins, give one warning, and use time-out for continued noncompliance or aggression. No new data are reported; the author points to earlier outcome studies of similar charts."},{"k":"daou2014","apa":"Daou, N. (2014). Conducting behavioral research with children attending nonbehavioral intervention programs for autism: The case of Lebanon. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 78–90.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0017-0","y":2014,"sum":"Three 5- and 6-year-olds with autism at a non-behavioral private school in Lebanon, with no earlier behavioral services, were taught to follow photo activity schedules of leisure activities (puzzles, games, books, snacks) using graduated guidance, a token system that was thinned, error correction and backward chaining."},{"k":"davis2023","apa":"Davis, T. N., Gerow, S., Wicker, M., Cosottile, D., Exline, E., Swensson, R., & Lively, P. (2023). Utilizing telehealth to coach parents to implement trial-based functional analysis and treatment. Journal of Behavioral Education, 32(4), 703–725.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-022-09468-3","y":2023,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"davistemple2014","apa":"Davis-Temple, J., Jung, S., & Sainato, D. M. (2014). Teaching young children with special needs and their peers to play board games: Effects of a least to most prompting procedure to increase independent performance. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(1), 21–30.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0001-8","y":2014,"sum":"Three 5- and 6-year-olds with special needs in a pre-K inclusion classroom, each paired with a typically developing peer, were taught three board games during free play, with each step modeled and named and errors corrected with least-to-most prompting."},{"k":"deitz1974","apa":"Deitz, S. M., & Repp, A. C. (1974). Differentially reinforcing low rates of misbehavior with normal elementary school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 622.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-622","y":1974,"sum":"Three general education students in grades 5 and 6 (two boys aged 11 and a girl aged 12) who often talked out or left their seats during 45-minute class periods. One boy's talk-outs fell from 4.45 to 1.83 per period, rose to 7.60 when stars stopped and fell to 1.20 when they returned; a girl's out-of-seat behavior fell from 6.10 to 0.16, returned to 6.00 in reversal and fell to 0.40 again."},{"k":"denton2016","apa":"Denton, T. F., & Meindl, J. N. (2016). The effect of colored overlays on reading fluency in individuals with dyslexia. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 191–198.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0079-7","y":2016,"sum":"Three readers with dyslexia and no other disability (ages 7, 11 and 32) first read 1-minute passages with and without the colored overlay they had picked as clearest, and the overlay never raised correct words per minute (it lowered the adult's)."},{"k":"derwas1993","apa":"Derwas, H., & Jones, R. S. P. (1993). Reducing stereotyped behavior using momentary DRO: An experimental analysis. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(1), 45–53.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080106","y":1993,"sum":"Five nonverbal boys and young men (7, 7, 14, 16 and 19) with severe intellectual disability, two in a day-school class and three in a residential school's family room, with high-rate finger scrabbling, head patting, ear poking, thumb sucking or throwing."},{"k":"dietz1973","apa":"Dietz, S. M., & Repp, A. C. (1973). Decreasing classroom misbehavior through the use of DRL schedules of reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 457–463.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-457","y":1973,"sum":"One 11-year-old boy with a moderate intellectual disability, picked by his teacher as the most disruptive student in a special class in a regular elementary school, whose talk-outs (talking without permission, singing or humming to himself, off-topic remarks) averaged 5.7 per 50-minute class."},{"k":"dixon1998","apa":"Dixon, M. R., Hayes, L. J., Binder, L. M., Manthey, S., Sigman, C., & Zdanowski, D. M. (1998). Using a self-control training procedure to increase appropriate behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 31(2), 203–210.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1998.31-203","y":1998,"sum":"Children and adults who always chose the small immediate reward came to choose the larger one when the wait (or the work) required for it began at zero and grew gradually. In children with autism, waits filled with a task were preferred and brought less disruptive behavior than empty waits."},{"k":"dixon2001","apa":"Dixon, M. R., & Cummings, A. (2001). Self-control in children with autism: Response allocation during delays to reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 34(4), 491–495.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2001.34-491","y":2001,"sum":"Children and adults who always chose the small immediate reward came to choose the larger one when the wait (or the work) required for it began at zero and grew gradually. In children with autism, waits filled with a task were preferred and brought less disruptive behavior than empty waits."},{"k":"dixon2019","apa":"Dixon, M. R., Blevins, A., Belisle, J., & Bethel, B. (2019). Teaching children with autism extended verbal utterances under audience control in the context of show-and-tell. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 194–198.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0250-z","y":2019,"sum":"Three 10-year-old boys with autism were taught by their teacher in their self-contained classroom. After the cue, an independent description earned praise; otherwise the teacher gave a full model to echo, then on later trials a partial model of 1 to 10 words, then a question prompt, stepping back up when a lighter prompt failed. All three went from 0 at baseline to two consecutive 10-trial blocks scoring at least 90 out of 100 within about 3 weeks."},{"k":"dixon2020","apa":"Dixon, D. R., Miyake, C. J., Nohelty, K., Novack, M. N., & Granpeesheh, D. (2020). Evaluation of an immersive virtual reality safety training used to teach pedestrian skills to children with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 13(3), 631–640.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-019-00401-1","y":2020,"sum":"Students with intellectual disabilities were taught the parts of crossing the street on a classroom model of city traffic; checked in real traffic, they crossed correctly, and training some parts also improved parts not yet trained."},{"k":"doherty2018","apa":"Doherty, A., Bracken, M., & Gormley, L. (2018). Teaching children with autism to initiate and respond to peer mands using picture exchange communication system (PECS). Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 279–288.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00311-8","y":2018,"sum":"Three preschool boys with autism who used PECS only with adults were taught in an ABA preschool with preferred items withheld beforehand and set beside a peer. Least-to-most prompts (point, elbow, hand-over-hand) worked for one child after a switch to most-to-least prompting; the other two needed an edible after each independent peer request, thinned to variable ratios of up to 9."},{"k":"doke1972","apa":"Doke, L. A., & Risley, T. R. (1972). The organization of day-care environments: Required vs. optional activities. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(4), 405–420.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-405","y":1972,"sum":"Fourteen children aged 4 and 5 in a 3-hour morning preschool serving low-income families, with three teachers each assigned to activity areas, moving through a fixed daily sequence of play, story, music, lesson and outdoor activities."},{"k":"dotson2010","apa":"Dotson, W. H., Leaf, J. B., Sheldon, J. B., & Sherman, J. A. (2010). Group teaching of conversational skills to adolescents on the autism spectrum. Research in Autism Spectrum Disorders, 4(2), 199–209.","doi":"10.1016/j.rasd.2009.09.005","y":2010,"sum":"A child and two adolescents with autism were taught greeting and conversation skills chosen with their parents, with behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback); the skills carried over to unscripted talks with new adults. Adolescents were also taught in small groups with the teaching interaction procedure; carry-over to typical peers was partial, so plan practice with them."},{"k":"dotson2017","apa":"Dotson, W. H., Rasmussen, E. E., Shafer, A., Colwell, M., Densley, R. L., Brewer, A. T., Alonzo, M. C., & Martinez, L. A. (2017). Evaluating the ability of the PBS children’s show Daniel Tiger’s Neighborhood to teach skills to two young children with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(1), 67–71.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0134-z","y":2017,"sum":"Two 5-year-old boys with autism who spoke in short phrases watched episodes of a children's TV show about tasting new foods and about stopping play, with no other teaching, in a small university research room. Probes after each viewing scored the percentage of steps done (touch, mouth, chew and swallow a food never eaten before; say OK, stay calm, stop playing within 15 s and walk to the adult)."},{"k":"dowdy2019","apa":"Dowdy, A., & Jacobs, K. W. (2019). An empirical evaluation of the disequilibrium model to increase independent seatwork for an individual diagnosed with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(3), 617–621.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00307-4","y":2019,"sum":"Demands are faded in: the session begins with few demands (sometimes none) and more are added only while problem behavior stays low, until the full assignment is reached. Breaks earned by finishing a set amount of work (response chaining) did the same."},{"k":"drabman1974a","apa":"Drabman, R., Spitalnik, R., & Spitalnik, K. (1974). Sociometric and disruptive behavior as a function of four types of token reinforcement programs. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 93–101.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-93","y":1974,"sum":"A first-grade class of 23 was split into four table groups, and during one afternoon hour the teacher gave up to 5 points every 20 minutes for classroom behavior, each point worth a minute of free time with art materials, clay or tumbling mats."},{"k":"drabman1974b","apa":"Drabman, R. S., & Lahey, B. B. (1974). Feedback in classroom behavior modification: Effects on the target and her classmates. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 591–598.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-591","y":1974,"sum":"A 10-year-old girl who was the most disruptive student in a private-school class of 13 and had no friends was given, at a timer after 15 minutes and every 10 minutes after that, a quiet 0-to-10 rating of her behavior by the teacher, and was told her total at the end of class, with no other reward."},{"k":"drash1991","apa":"Drash, P. W., & Tudor, R. M. (1991). A standard methodology for the analysis, recording, and control of verbal behavior. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 9, 49–60.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392860","y":1991,"sum":"One 4-year-old boy with a severe language delay and no functional speech, who imitated only a few sounds and answered fewer than half of echoic prompts, taught in sessions of about 40 minutes. Once only positive reinforcement was used, the child answered 86% of prompts in the next session (up from 42%) and his correct echoic responses rose from 11% to 57%. Over the last four sessions he answered more than 85% of prompts, with more than 45% correct echoics."},{"k":"drew2023","apa":"Drew, C. M., Machalicek, W., Crowe, B., Glugatch, L., Wei, Q., & Erturk, B. (2023). Parent-implemented behavior interventions via telehealth for older children and adolescents. Journal of Behavioral Education, 32(3), 585–604.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-021-09464-z","y":2023,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"drivas2019","apa":"Drivas, M. K., & Drevon, D. D. (2019). The effect of a sounding-out step for cover-copy-compare on spelling word acquisition. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(3), 514–522.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00322-5","y":2019,"sum":"Three second- and third-graders with below-average spelling (two with a speech-language impairment or a learning disability) practiced two lists of 5 unknown words a day for 6 to 7 weeks: study the word, cover it, write it, uncover and compare, and redo the word until correct, with praise; one list added saying the sounds aloud before covering."},{"k":"du2017","apa":"Du, L., Speckman, J., Medina, M., & Cole-Hatchard, M. (2017). The effects of an auditory matching iPad app on three preschoolers’ echoic and listener responses. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(2), 118–130.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0174-z","y":2017,"sum":"Three preschoolers aged 4–5 (two with autism, one with Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome) at a CABAS preschool, whose echoes were unclear, worked through a 22-phase iPad sound-matching program in which they pressed the button that sounded the same as a sample, moving from sounds to words, rhymes and short phrases; the app gave animation and applause for correct answers and a corrective model for errors, and each phase was mastered at 90% in two sessions or 100%…"},{"k":"ducharme1994","apa":"Ducharme, J. M., & Worling, D. E. (1994). Behavioral momentum and stimulus fading in the acquisition and maintenance of child compliance in the home. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 27(4), 639–647.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1994.27-639","y":1994,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"duker1991","apa":"Duker, P. C., Jol, K., & Palmen, A. (1991). The collateral decrease of self-injurious behavior with teaching communicative gestures to individuals who are mentally retarded. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(3), 183–196.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060304","y":1991,"sum":"Fourteen nonspeaking residents aged 11 to 35 with severe or profound intellectual disability and daily self-injury in six Dutch facilities were taught at least 10 single-movement request gestures each in one-to-one sessions four times a week (most-to-least and delayed prompting, with drinks and cookies) until they used them when asked what they wanted, while ward staff prompted and honored the gestures; one group also got 2 minutes of guided gesture…"},{"k":"dunkeljackson2018","apa":"Dunkel-Jackson, S. M., & Dixon, M. R. (2018). Promoting generalized advanced language skills of children in intensive behavioral intervention with Promoting the Emergence of Advanced Knowledge Generalization Module (PEAK–G). Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 289–306.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0204-x","y":2018,"sum":"Two 6- to 7-year-old boys with autism in an intensive program were taught in 10-trial discrete-trial blocks in which staff mumbled part of a statement and repeated it clearly only after the student asked; errors were followed by increasingly intrusive prompts."},{"k":"dunlap1994","apa":"Dunlap, G., dePerczel, M., Clarke, S., Wilson, D., Wright, S., White, R., & Gomez, A. (1994). Choice making to promote adaptive behavior for students with emotional and behavioral challenges. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 27(3), 505–518.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1994.27-505","y":1994,"sum":"Students with emotional and behavioral challenges were offered a choice among tasks of the same difficulty; with the choice, their engagement rose and their disruptive behavior fell compared with tasks assigned to them."},{"k":"durand1991","apa":"Durand, V. M., & Carr, E. G. (1991). Functional communication training to reduce challenging behavior: Maintenance and application in new settings. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 24(2), 251–264.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1991.24-251","y":1991,"sum":"Children whose problem behavior was maintained by escape from difficult tasks were taught to ask for assistance, and real help followed each request; problem behavior fell as the requests rose, and the effect carried to new teachers and settings. Teach on hard items mixed with easy ones, prompt the request before problem behavior, and fade the prompt."},{"k":"durand1999","apa":"Durand, V. M. (1999). Functional communication training using assistive devices: Recruiting natural communities of reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 32(3), 247–267.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1999.32-247","y":1999,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"dyer2013","apa":"Dyer, K., & Karp, R. (2013). A staff-training program to increase spontaneous vocal requests in children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 6(2), 42–60.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391805","y":2013,"sum":"The request is taught to produce the item the problem behavior used to produce, honored at once at first; the item no longer follows problem behavior. The prompt is faded, then the delay between request and item is lengthened (see Wait for a requested item)."},{"k":"edgerton2017","apa":"Edgerton, L., & Wine, B. (2017). Speak up: Increasing conversational volume in a child with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 407–410.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0168-2","y":2017,"sum":"An 11-year-old boy with autism at a private school who spoke too quietly answered 30 picture questions per session while a voice-meter app on an iPad showed whether each answer was too quiet, conversational or too loud. In intervention, each answer at conversational volume earned praise and a small edible with feedback, and he wore a green wristband as a reminder; the measure was the percentage of answers at conversational volume in an ABAB design."},{"k":"eid2017","apa":"Eid, A. M., Aljaser, S. M., AlSaud, A. N., Asfahani, S. M., Alhaqbani, O. A., Mohtasib, R. S., Aldhalaan, H. M., & Fryling, M. (2017). Training parents in Saudi Arabia to implement discrete trial teaching with their children with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 402–406.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0167-3","y":2017,"sum":"Three mothers in Saudi Arabia were taught with instructions, modeling, rehearsal and feedback to run discrete trial teaching with their children with autism (two aged 6, one aged 4) at a research center. Each child was taught one one-step instruction (touch head or touch tummy), with touching a cup in an array as the generalization task, and the child measure was the percentage of correct responses within 5 s in a multiple-probe design."},{"k":"elliott2024","apa":"Elliott, T.-C. C., Zawoyski, A. M., & Ayres, K. M. (2024). Using direct and indirect functional assessments to guide the selection of individualized academic interventions. Journal of Behavioral Education, 33(4), 912–948.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-023-09511-x","y":2024,"sum":"Four students aged 9–15 (one with autism and speech-language impairment, one with autism, ADHD and other diagnoses, one with a specific learning disability, one with a math deficit) seen 1:1 in a university clinic or by video call for multiplication, geometry transformations, spelling or reading comprehension."},{"k":"enders2026","apa":"Enders, O. G., & Kostewicz, D. (2026). Evaluating the effects of an algebraic frequency building intervention for students with disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 19(3), 1303–1316.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-025-01082-9","y":2026,"sum":"Three middle-school students with learning disabilities were taught a 7-step written procedure in one session (vocabulary, modeling, guided practice with least-to-most prompts, 7 of 8 independent problems per operation), then did a daily 1-min check sheet followed by three 1-min practice timings with feedback and the goal of beating their own score."},{"k":"epstein1976","apa":"Epstein, L. H., Parker, L., McCoy, J. F., & McGee, G. (1976). Descriptive analysis of eating regulation in obese and nonobese children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(4), 407–415.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-407","y":1976,"sum":"Six typically developing 7-year-olds (three of them overweight) were observed at school lunch about twice a week for six months. Each child was told to put the utensil down after every bite and reminded before entering the lunch line, and later an observer praised improvement after the meal, in a multiple baseline across children."},{"k":"erfanian1990","apa":"Erfanian, N., & Miltenberger, R. G. (1990). Brief report: Contact desensitization in the treatment of dog phobias in persons who have mental retardation. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(1), 55–60.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050106","y":1990,"sum":"Three children with autism aged 5, 7 and 10 who protested and fled from dogs worked in a gym with one large, calm therapy dog. While watching a video or playing a game, each child was prompted to move 5 feet closer at a time, and 1 foot at a time inside 10 feet, earning praise and a favorite snack for each step and moving back a foot if he or she resisted; now and then the child was asked to look at the dog and answer a question about it."},{"k":"erhard2026","apa":"Erhard, P., Falcomata, T. S., Nesselrode, R., Vargas Londoño, F., & Ramirez-Cristoforo, A. (2026). Evaluation of instructive feedback as a strategy for generalizing tacts across primary and secondary languages. Behavior Modification, 50(1), 32–59.","doi":"10.1177/01454455251364284","y":2026,"sum":"Children aged 4 to 11 with severe intellectual disability or autism in a residential centre, who could repeat words but named few pictures, learned one new picture at a time: they repeated its name after the adult and then named it on their own, with the new picture mixed among three they already knew, and it counted as learned when they named it at the start of three later sessions."},{"k":"ewry2016","apa":"Ewry, D. M., & Fryling, M. J. (2016). Evaluating the high-probability instructional sequence to increase the acceptance of foods with an adolescent with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(4), 380–383.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0098-4","y":2016,"sum":"For children with established but narrow eating, procedures that work without escape extinction include praise or a preferred food for each accepted bite, preferred items available through the meal, the new food presented together with a preferred one, a quick sequence of easy requests (empty spoon presentations) before a bite, and bite size or texture faded in gradually."},{"k":"fabry1978","apa":"Fabry, P. L., & Reid, D. H. (1978). Teaching foster grandparents to train severely handicapped persons. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(1), 111–123.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-111","y":1978,"sum":"Five adolescent girls aged 15 to 18 with profound intellectual disability and physical disabilities (wheelchair users with spastic or athetoid limb movements and no self-help skills) living in a state institution were taught for 30 minutes a day by foster-grandparent volunteers, whom a staff teacher coached to instruct, model, verbally prompt, hand-over-hand guide and praise head turning, reaching and object handling with rattles, blocks, play dough,…"},{"k":"fallon2025","apa":"Fallon, M. J., Luczynski, K. C., Rodriguez, N. M., Felty, C., & Rahaman, J. A. (2025). A preliminary analysis of teaching children with autism spectrum disorder self-protection skills for bullying situations. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 58(2), 287–302.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.2938","y":2025,"sum":"A 9-year-old girl with autism in a general education class swore, threatened and hit when teachers touched her things, touched her or chatted near her; her aggression served escape and attention. During work, teachers did one of these things about four times a session and stopped within 3 to 5 s whatever she did."},{"k":"farb1978","apa":"Farb, J., & Throne, J. M. (1978). Improving the generalized mnemonic performance of a Down's syndrome child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 413–419.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-413","y":1978,"sum":"A 6-year-old girl with Down syndrome (IQ about 50) was taught digit strings of three, four and five digits in 20-minute sessions three days a week, with praise and candy or potato chips for correct repetitions; after an error the item was re-presented and closer attempts were reinforced (first the right first digit, then the right last digit), and when that failed a short pause between digit groups was added and then shortened."},{"k":"farnum1978","apa":"Farnum, M., & Brigham, T. A. (1978). The use and evaluation of study guides with middle school students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(1), 137–144.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-137","y":1978,"sum":"Twenty-four fifth graders (10 boys, 14 girls) in a general-education social studies class read assigned units from two textbooks and, in study-guide weeks, filled in a 10- to 20-blank guide covering the unit on Wednesday and Thursday; the guide was scored and handed back the next day and a short-answer quiz followed on Monday."},{"k":"farrell2008","apa":"Farrell, A., & McDougall, D. (2008). Self-monitoring of pace to improve math fluency of high school students with disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(2), 26–35.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391725","y":2008,"sum":"High school students with learning and emotional disabilities checked their pace when a vibrating cue went off, against marks on the worksheet, then graphed their results and set goals; correct answers per minute rose while errors stayed low. Younger students with mild disabilities graphed their own rate on a computer against an aim line, and two of three met or beat it nearly every session."},{"k":"fawcett1975","apa":"Fawcett, S. B., & Miller, L. K. (1975). Training public-speaking behavior: An experimental analysis and social validation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 125–135.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-125","y":1975,"sum":"A university student and three low-income women who gave talks for a neighborhood service center were taught to give a scripted talk with about 13 slides: opening and closing steps (stand facing the audience nearby, thank the host by name, greet the audience, introduce the topic, close with a greeting and ask for questions), a hand gesture with each slide, and looking at the audience."},{"k":"felixbrod1973","apa":"Felixbrod, J. J., & O'Leary, K. D. (1973). Effects of reinforcement on children's academic behavior as a function of self-determined and externally imposed contingencies. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 241–250.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-241","y":1973,"sum":"Twenty-four typical second graders in a Long Island public school worked alone on grade-level arithmetic sheets for up to 20 minutes in six sessions. Matched children either chose how many correct answers earned a point (traded for candy or toys), had the same standard set for them, or worked without reward. Correct answers, time at the task, rate, accuracy and the standards chosen were recorded and compared by statistics; no mastery criterion was set."},{"k":"ferritor1972","apa":"Ferritor, D. E., Buckholdt, D., Hamblin, R. L., & Smith, L. (1972). The noneffects of contingent reinforcement for attending behavior on work accomplished. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 7–17.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-7","y":1972,"sum":"Two groups of third graders at an inner-city school (14 and 9 children; mean age 10, mean IQ about 75) did a daily 20-minute drill of 100 mixed arithmetic problems while tokens, traded about weekly for treats, toys, activities and trips, were given for attending, for correct work, or for both, in reversal designs."},{"k":"figarola2008","apa":"Figarola, P. M., Gunter, P. L., Reffel, J. M., Worth, S. R., Hummel, J., & Gerber, B. L. (2008). Effects of self-graphing and goal setting on the math fact fluency of students with disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(2), 36–41.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391726","y":2008,"sum":"High school students with learning and emotional disabilities checked their pace when a vibrating cue went off, against marks on the worksheet, then graphed their results and set goals; correct answers per minute rose while errors stayed low. Younger students with mild disabilities graphed their own rate on a computer against an aim line, and two of three met or beat it nearly every session."},{"k":"fink1975","apa":"Fink, W. T., & Carnine, D. W. (1975). Control of arithmetic errors using informational feedback and graphing. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 461.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-461","y":1975,"sum":"Ten first graders in one classroom were told the number of errors on each daily arithmetic worksheet, written at the top of the sheet, and in two phases of an ABAB design also plotted that number on their own desk graphs. Every child made fewer worksheet errors in the graphing phases (a mean difference of about 2.7 errors). Only a one-page abstract was printed; the full report was deposited on microfiche."},{"k":"fisher1998","apa":"Fisher, W. W., Kuhn, D. E., & Thompson, R. H. (1998). Establishing discriminative control of responding using functional and alternative reinforcers during functional communication training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 31(4), 543–560.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1998.31-543","y":1998,"sum":"After the request is reliable, some requests are answered with \"not now\" and an alternative is offered; a calm \"okay\" is praised and often earns the item a little later. The denials are rare at first and grow more frequent. A second request, for an alternative, can be taught too, with signals for which one will be answered."},{"k":"fisher2000","apa":"Fisher, W. W., Thompson, R. H., Hagopian, L. P., Bowman, L. G., & Krug, A. (2000). Facilitating tolerance of delayed reinforcement during functional communication training. Behavior Modification, 24(1), 3–29.","doi":"10.1177/0145445500241001","y":2000,"sum":"The wait begins very short (a few seconds) and grows only after the student waits well at the current length. A clear signal starts the wait, and something to do during it helps. In the studies, waiting improved most when calm waiting itself was what produced the item."},{"k":"fisher2018","apa":"Fisher, W. W., Greer, B. D., Fuhrman, A. M., Saini, V., & Simmons, C. A. (2018). Minimizing resurgence of destructive behavior using behavioral momentum theory. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 51(4), 831–853.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.499","y":2018,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"fisher2019","apa":"Fisher, W. W., Retzlaff, B. J., Akers, J. S., DeSouza, A. A., Kaminski, A. J., & Machado, M. A. (2019). Establishing initial auditory-visual conditional discriminations and emergence of initial tacts in young children with autism spectrum disorder. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 52(4), 1089–1106.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.586","y":2019,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"fixsen1972","apa":"Fixsen, D. L., Phillips, E. L., & Wolf, M. M. (1972). Achievement Place: The reliability of self-reporting and peer-reporting and their effects on behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 19–30.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-19","y":1972,"sum":"Six boys aged 12 to 16 living in a community group home run on a point system marked a 21-item room-cleaning checklist each morning for their own room and for a buddy’s room. Their marks were compared item by item with an adult observer’s check across reversal and multiple-baseline conditions that added daily practice with feedback on each standard and points for agreement; 17 of 21 matching items was the target."},{"k":"fjellstedt1973","apa":"Fjellstedt, N., & Sulzer-Azaroff, B. (1973). Reducing the latency of a child's responding to instructions by means of a token system. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(1), 125–130.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-125","y":1973,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy attending a special class for students with emotional and behavioral disabilities, who followed directions very slowly, was seen in a room outside his class for 17 half-hour sessions. He earned a token with praise for completing each direction (come to the room, put toys away and sit, start work, put toys away and line up, return to class and get ready to go home) within 3 minutes and later 2 minutes, or 15 seconds to start work, with…"},{"k":"fluharty2024","apa":"Fluharty, A. S. E., LeJeune, L. M., & Samudre, M. D. (2024). Using a group stimulus preference assessment to design an effective group contingency. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 17(4), 1008–1022.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-024-01003-2","y":2024,"sum":"Twenty middle-school students with disabilities in two self-contained sections voted on rewards; the teacher then posted the rule, read it as they entered, checked each student at the bell, and gave those who were prepared up to 10 min of the top-voted activity after the opening video while the others did an assignment."},{"k":"foxx1987a","apa":"Foxx, R. M., McMorrow, M. J., Faw, G. D., Kyle, M. S., & Bittle, R. G. (1987). Cues-Pause-Point language training: Structuring trainer statements to provide students with correct answers to questions. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 2(2), 103–115.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360020204","y":1987,"sum":"Two young adults with severe intellectual disability (a 20-year-old man in a group home who sometimes echoed unfamiliar questions and a 19-year-old woman living in an institution who answered them tangentially or with “I don’t know”) were taught to answer a question with the key word from a sentence the trainer had just said."},{"k":"foxx1987b","apa":"Foxx, R. M., McMorrow, M. J., Hernandez, M., Kyle, M., & Bittle, R. G. (1987). Teaching social skills to emotionally disturbed adolescent inpatients. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 2(2), 77–88.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360020202","y":1987,"sum":"Five adolescents aged 11 to 20 on an institutional unit (conduct disorder, adjustment disorder, borderline intellectual functioning) played a modified board game (Sorry) whose cards described 48 everyday situations in six areas (compliments, criticism, politeness, social interaction, questions and answers, social confrontation), moving a piece only after a fitting answer."},{"k":"foxx1988","apa":"Foxx, R. M., Marchand-Martella, N. E., Martella, R. C., Braunling-McMorrow, D., & McMorrow, M. J. (1988). Teaching a problem-solving strategy to closed head-injured adults. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 3(3), 193–210.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360030304","y":1988,"sum":"Three adults aged 21 to 24 with lasting effects of severe closed head injuries sustained at 16 to 18, who were moving from supervised to independent living, practiced answering 32 problem cards (community travel, medicines, alcohol and drugs, stating one’s rights, emergencies and injuries) in a small group, with a cue card of five steps (restate the problem, when it must be solved, where to look for help, whom to talk to, what to say), trainer modeling,…"},{"k":"foxx1989","apa":"Foxx, R. M., Kyle, M. S., Faw, G. D., & Bittle, R. G. (1989). Problem-solving skills training: Social validation and generalization. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(4), 269–288.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040402","y":1989,"sum":"Three adults aged 21 to 24 with lasting effects of closed head injuries sustained at 16 to 18, moving from supervised to independent living, took turns answering problem cards about community travel, medicines, alcohol and drugs, their rights, and emergencies and injuries, each posed as a friend’s problem."},{"k":"foxx1990","apa":"Foxx, R. M., & Faw, G. D. (1990). Problem-solving skills training for psychiatric inpatients: An analysis of generalization. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(3), 159–176.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050304","y":1990,"sum":"Three adults aged 21 to 24 with lasting effects of closed head injuries sustained at 16 to 18, moving from supervised to independent living, took turns answering problem cards about community travel, medicines, alcohol and drugs, their rights, and emergencies and injuries, each posed as a friend’s problem."},{"k":"frampton2017","apa":"Frampton, S. E., Robinson, H. C., Conine, D. E., & Delfs, C. H. (2017). An abbreviated evaluation of the efficiency of listener and tact instruction for children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(2), 131–144.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0175-y","y":2017,"sum":"Eight boys with autism aged 2 to 15 in a clinic-based verbal behavior program had unknown pictures split into sets taught either as names (saying the name when asked what the picture was) or as listener responses (touching the named picture)."},{"k":"frampton2019","apa":"Frampton, S. E., Thompson, T. M., Bartlett, B. L., Hansen, B., & Shillingsburg, M. A. (2019). The use of matrix training to teach color-shape tacts to children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 320–330.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00288-4","y":2019,"sum":"Six children aged 4–5 with autism, who already named the colors and shapes alone, were taught only the three diagonal combinations of a 3 × 3 matrix in nine-trial sessions with praise and preferred items, and a model-and-re-present correction for errors. After 3–7 sessions three children named all 27 trained and untrained combinations; two more did so after booster sessions, and the gains held at 4 weeks."},{"k":"frankcrawford2021","apa":"Frank-Crawford, M. A., Hallgren, M. M., McKenzie, A., Gregory, M. K., Wright, M. E., & Wachtel, L. E. (2021). Mask compliance training for individuals with intellectual and developmental disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 14(4), 883–892.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-021-00583-7","y":2021,"sum":"A teenage boy (about 16) had worn his orthodontic headgear only a few hours a day for years, and his mother checked it five times a day at times he could not predict. Praise at the checks, with no more scolding when it was off, raised wearing only from 25% to 36% of checks, and money marked on a calendar and paid at the end of the month raised it to 60%; paying 25 cents on the spot at each check with the bands in place brought wearing to about 97%."},{"k":"frederiksen1976","apa":"Frederiksen, L. W., Jenkins, J. O., Foy, D. W., & Eisler, R. M. (1976). Social-skills training to modify abusive verbal outbursts in adults. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 117–125.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-117","y":1976,"sum":"Two men aged 48 and 26 on psychiatric wards of a veterans' hospital, with histories of abusive verbal outbursts, practiced seven role-played provoking situations (three drawn from their own lives) in half-hour sessions using instructions with a reason, a video model, rehearsal and immediate feedback."},{"k":"frisch1974","apa":"Frisch, S. A., & Schumaker, J. B. (1974). Training generalized receptive prepositions in retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 611–621.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-611","y":1974,"sum":"Three children with intellectual disability living in a state institution (a 3-year-old boy with no speech and an 11-year-old girl and 4-year-old boy who followed only simple directions) were taught to follow directions such as put the doll next to (under, on top of) the cup, one example at a time, with praise and a taste of ice cream for correct responses, physical guidance that was faded, and after errors a no and a 10-second silent pause before a…"},{"k":"fruchtman2025","apa":"Fruchtman, T., Jessel, J., Pan, B., McLeod, S., & Rajaraman, A. (2025). The performance-based IISCA can inform effective and socially meaningful skill-based treatment. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 18(4), 921–940.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-024-01036-7","y":2025,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"garcia1971","apa":"Garcia, E., Baer, D. M., & Firestone, I. (1971). The development of generalized imitation within topographically determined boundaries. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 101–112.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-101","y":1971,"sum":"Four residents of an institution aged 8 to 14 with severe intellectual disability, who did not imitate at the start, were taught over 11 months to copy small hand movements, larger whole-body actions and (for two) vowel sounds, using modeling, physical guidance that was faded, and praise with a bite of a sweet."},{"k":"garcia1973","apa":"Garcia, E., Guess, D., & Byrnes, J. (1973). Development of syntax in a retarded girl using procedures of imitation, reinforcement, and modelling. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 299–310.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-299","y":1973,"sum":"A 10-year-old girl with severe intellectual disability, living in a state institution and speaking only in single words, was taught in 10- to 15-minute daily sessions to describe an object, or a pair of objects, in a full sentence."},{"k":"garcia1974","apa":"Garcia, E. (1974). The training and generalization of a conversational speech form in nonverbal retardates. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 137–149.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-137","y":1974,"sum":"Two residents of a state institution, a 12-year-old girl and an 18-year-old young man with profound intellectual disability who had a few words and could imitate, were taught three sentences about catalog pictures (asking what a picture was, naming it after hearing its name, and saying yes when asked whether they wanted it), then the three as a chain, through modeling after errors and reinforcement."},{"k":"garcia1976","apa":"Garcia, E. E. (1976). The development and generalization of delayed imitation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(4), 499.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-499","y":1976,"sum":"Four children with intellectual disability (three who already imitated and one who did not) were taught with prompting, fading and consequences to copy motor actions either right away (within 5 seconds) or after a delay (more than 5 and up to 25 seconds after the model). An ABAB design alternated the two kinds of training, and untaught actions were probed throughout to see which timing the children used; only a one-page summary was printed."},{"k":"garcia1977a","apa":"Garcia, E. E., & Trujillo, A. (1977). The effect of experimenter facial orientation during imitation maintenance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 95.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-95","y":1977,"sum":"Abstract only (the full report was deposited with an auxiliary publication service). Three children with intellectual disability who already imitated at high levels under a maintenance procedure, with every imitation reinforced, were shown two kinds of modelled actions (standing and sitting types)."},{"k":"garcia1977b","apa":"Garcia, E. E., & Batista-Wallace, M. (1977). Parental training of the plural morpheme in normal toddlers. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 505.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-505","y":1977,"sum":"Three mothers taught their typically developing children, aged 19 to 25 months, to name singular and plural sets of items, praising correct names and modeling the correct name after an error. Correct naming of trained and never-trained items was measured, then reversed naming (plural names for single items and singular names for sets) was taught and the original teaching was reinstated; only the abstract was printed (full text deposited)."},{"k":"gardner1991","apa":"Gardner, R., III, & Heward, W. L. (1991). Case study: Improving the social interactions of a group home resident with severe and multiple disabilities. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(1), 39–50.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060104","y":1991,"sum":"A man of 43 with severe intellectual disability and hearing and vision loss in a group home was taught to start an interaction by saying the staff member's name or signing 'talk' and waiting, to keep his hands to himself, and to stop repeating the same question, first with modeling and role play before sessions plus staff praise and correction, then with poker-chip tokens (and token loss for problem behavior) traded for outings."},{"k":"gardner1994","apa":"Gardner, R., III, Heward, W. L., & Grossi, T. A. (1994). Effects of response cards on student participation and academic achievement: A systematic replication with inner-city students during whole-class science instruction. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 27(1), 63–71.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1994.27-63","y":1994,"sum":"Instead of calling on one student at a time, every student answers each question on a card; the teacher looks, praises and corrects. Responding and quiz scores rose, and in a later study disruptive behavior fell."},{"k":"gavidia2022","apa":"Gavidia, V. L., Bergmann, S., & Rader, K. A. (2022). The use of instructive feedback to promote emergent tact and intraverbal control: A replication. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 38(2), 95–120.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-022-00171-y","y":2022,"sum":"A boy with autism aged 3 to 4 heard one fact about each picture during naming trials (before the question or after praise) with no response required; he learned the facts and then answered the questions with no picture at 80–90% for most sets (cifuentes2026)."},{"k":"geiger2010","apa":"Geiger, K. B., Carr, J. E., & LeBlanc, L. A. (2010). Function-based treatments for escape-maintained problem behavior: A treatment-selection model for practicing behavior analysts. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 3(1), 22–32.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391755","y":2010,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"geiger2012","apa":"Geiger, K. B., Carr, J. E., LeBlanc, L. A., Hanney, N. M., Polick, A. S., & Heinicke, M. R. (2012). Teaching receptive discriminations to children with autism: A comparison of traditional and embedded discrete trial teaching. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 5(2), 49–59.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391823","y":2012,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"ghaemmaghami2016","apa":"Ghaemmaghami, M., Hanley, G. P., & Jessel, J. (2016). Contingencies promote delay tolerance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 49(3), 548–575.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.333","y":2016,"sum":"The wait begins very short (a few seconds) and grows only after the student waits well at the current length. A clear signal starts the wait, and something to do during it helps. In the studies, waiting improved most when calm waiting itself was what produced the item."},{"k":"ghaemmaghami2018","apa":"Ghaemmaghami, M., Hanley, G. P., Jessel, J., & Landa, R. (2018). Shaping complex functional communication responses. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 51(3), 502–520.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.468","y":2018,"sum":"Once a one-word request is reliable, longer and more socially expected requests are shaped one part at a time (getting attention, then the request, then the polite word), each part reinforced as it is added, while problem behavior stays low."},{"k":"gladstone1975","apa":"Gladstone, B. W., & Sherman, J. A. (1975). Developing generalized behavior-modification skills in high-school students working with retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 169–180.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-169","y":1975,"sum":"Seven high-school volunteers aged 15 to 18 were trained with a 25-minute video model, rehearsal with the experimenter acting as the child, and praise and corrective feedback every 5 minutes during their sessions to teach children aged 6 to 14 with profound intellectual disability in an institution to follow “bring ball”, and then, with no more coaching, to teach a second child to follow “sit down” and “come here”."},{"k":"glass1993","apa":"Glass, M., Houlihan, D., Fatis, M., & Levine, H. (1993). Brief report: Compliance in the classroom: Using the “thumbs up” procedure to increase student compliance to teacher requests. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(4), 281–288.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080406","y":1993,"sum":"In a regular fifth-grade class of 23 students, the math teacher ran a classwide game: she gave a thumbs-up before an instruction, and each student who returned the thumbs-up and started within 15 s earned a point on a card, with students reaching 60% (later 70%) of possible points entered in a prize drawing for games or movies."},{"k":"glover1976","apa":"Glover, J., & Gary, A. L. (1976). Procedures to increase some aspects of creativity. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 79–84.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-79","y":1976,"sum":"Eight typically developing fourth and fifth graders in a summer school class wrote, for 10 minutes each day, all the uses they could think of for a common classroom object. After a 5-day baseline, two teams earned points for one feature of their lists at a time, changed every 5 days (number of different uses, different kinds of use, words per use, kinds of use never given before), with early recess and a snack for the winning team, or both teams if close;…"},{"k":"glynn1970","apa":"Glynn, E. L. (1970). Classroom applications of self-determined reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 123–132.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-123","y":1970,"sum":"One hundred twenty-eight ninth-grade girls in four intact classes at a Toronto school read a short history or geography passage each day, took a 20-item multiple-choice quiz and scored it themselves, and, by class, received tokens for small prizes at a set rate of one per four correct answers, chose their own number of tokens from zero to five, received a randomly paired classmate's amount, or received none."},{"k":"glynn1973","apa":"Glynn, E. L., Thomas, J. D., & Shee, S. M. (1973). Behavioral self-control of on-task behavior in an elementary classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(1), 105–113.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-105","y":1973,"sum":"A typical second-grade class of 37 children aged 6 and 7 in Auckland, New Zealand, of whom 8 were observed, first earned class free time for being on task at random checks during a 30-minute reading lesson. Then each child checked a box on a name card at each of about ten unpredictable taped beeps only if on task at that moment and took 1 minute of free time with toys per check."},{"k":"glynn1974","apa":"Glynn, E. L., & Thomas, J. D. (1974). Effect of cueing on self-control of classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 299–306.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-299","y":1974,"sum":"In a regular third-grade class of 34 in Auckland, the nine children judged hardest to manage were observed during a daily language lesson while the whole class judged and recorded whether they were on task at random taped tones and traded each check for a minute of free time."},{"k":"goetz1973","apa":"Goetz, E. M., & Baer, D. M. (1973). Social control of form diversity and the emergence of new forms in children's blockbuilding. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 209–217.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-209","y":1973,"sum":"Three typically developing 4-year-old girls in a university preschool, whose block play was sparse or repetitive, built with blocks alone with a teacher every few days. The teacher gave warm descriptive praise for each kind of structure (from a list of 20 defined forms, such as tower, ramp, arch or enclosure) the first time it appeared in a session, then for repeats only, then again for new forms."},{"k":"goetz1975","apa":"Goetz, E. M., Holmberg, M. C., & LeBlanc, J. M. (1975). Differential reinforcement of other behavior and noncontingent reinforcement as control procedures during the modification of a preschooler's compliance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(1), 77–82.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-77","y":1975,"sum":"A typically developing girl aged 3 years 7 months in a university preschool who rarely followed teachers’ requests got a teacher’s closeness (within 3 feet) and specific praise each time she complied within 30 seconds. In comparison phases the same attention came regardless of compliance, or came after she did not comply with sympathetic remarks; compliance (requests followed within 30 seconds, from 10-second interval records in the last half hour of the…"},{"k":"graves2005","apa":"Graves, T. B., Collins, B. C., Schuster, J. W., & Kleinert, H. (2005). Using video prompting to teach cooking skills to secondary students with moderate disabilities. Education and Training in Developmental Disabilities, 40(1), 34–46.","doi":"","y":2005,"sum":"Each step is shown on a short video clip just before the student does it (video prompting). Secondary students with moderate disabilities learned to make a microwave food, a stovetop food and a sandwich with video prompts and constant time delay; with computer-presented video prompts, two of three adults with developmental disabilities learned a ten-step microwave task."},{"k":"gray1969","apa":"Gray, B. B., Baker, R. D., & Stancyk, S. E. (1969). Performance determined instruction for training in remedial reading. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(4), 255–263.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-255","y":1969,"sum":"Nine boys averaging 9 and a half years old, referred as remedial readers and already knowing letter names and common letter sounds, worked in pairs with a teacher for 30 minutes twice a week for 3 months, reading phonics word lists and then graded-reader sentences aloud for poker-chip tokens traded for candy, with the turn passing to the partner after an error."},{"k":"green1993","apa":"Green, C. W., Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H. (1993). Integrating instructional procedures into traditional congregate care situations for people with severe disabilities. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(4), 243–262.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080404","y":1993,"sum":"In a public residential facility, direct-care staff were trained (a 20-minute meeting, about 20 minutes of modeling on the unit, then feedback after observations) to give brief teaching trials on each person's own goals during group leisure periods."},{"k":"greenwood1974b","apa":"Greenwood, C. R., Hops, H., Delquadri, J., & Guild, J. (1974). Group contingencies for group consequences in classroom management: A further analysis. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 413–425.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-413","y":1974,"sum":"Three first- to third-grade general education classes (20 to 28 students) had, in sequence during reading and math, posted and practiced rules, then a light and clock that the teacher ran only while the whole class followed the rules with the class percent posted, then that plus teacher praise and a class-voted activity earned by beating the previous average and later by reaching 80% on a growing number of sessions."},{"k":"greer2016","apa":"Greer, B. D., Fisher, W. W., Saini, V., Owen, T. M., & Jones, J. K. (2016). Functional communication training during reinforcement schedule thinning: An analysis of 25 applications. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 49(1), 105–121.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.265","y":2016,"sum":"Requests are answered in one signaled period and not in the other, and the rule is told to the student; the periods alternate. Once requests come mostly in the \"available\" period, it is shortened step by step. This let teachers thin the answers to requests without problem behavior returning, and worked classwide in preschools."},{"k":"greer2023","apa":"Greer, B. D., Shahan, T. A., Fisher, W. W., Mitteer, D. R., & Fuhrman, A. M. (2023). Further evaluation of treatment duration on the resurgence of destructive behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 56(1), 166–180.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.956","y":2023,"sum":"The request is taught to produce the item the problem behavior used to produce, honored at once at first; the item no longer follows problem behavior. The prompt is faded, then the delay between request and item is lengthened (see Wait for a requested item)."},{"k":"grob2019","apa":"Grob, C. M., Lerman, D. C., Langlinais, C. A., & Villante, N. K. (2019). Assessing and teaching job-related social skills to adults with autism spectrum disorder. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 52(1), 150–172.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.503","y":2019,"sum":"Taught with behavioral skills training: the steps described with a reason for each, modeled, rehearsed in role play with feedback, then checked in new situations. Adolescents with learning disabilities learned to accept negative feedback in group training; adults with autism learned to respond to a supervisor’s correction at work, with cue cards and several examples when needed."},{"k":"grow2013","apa":"Grow, L., & LeBlanc, L. (2013). Teaching receptive language skills: Recommendations for instructors. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 6(1), 56–75.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391791","y":2013,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"grow2017a","apa":"Grow, L. L., & Van Der Hijde, R. (2017). A comparison of procedures for teaching receptive labeling of sight words to a child with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(1), 62–66.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0133-0","y":2017,"sum":"Jon, a 6-year-old with autism who spoke in sentences but had never been taught to read, was taught at his kitchen table to point to one of three spoken sight words. Teaching all three words together from the start was compared with a shortened sequence that first taught each word against a single distractor; both used a prompt delay that grew from 0 to 5 s, with tokens for correct answers."},{"k":"grow2017b","apa":"Grow, L. L., Kodak, T., & Clements, A. (2017). An evaluation of instructive feedback to teach play behavior to a child with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(3), 313–317.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0153-9","y":2017,"sum":"A 7-year-old girl with autism in an early intervention clinic had table lessons on naming features of animals, foods and objects, and right after the reward for some answers the teacher briefly acted out a pretend-play sequence with toys and words (instructive feedback), with no reward for copying it."},{"k":"guercio2015","apa":"Guercio, J. M., & Cormier, R. J. (2015). Blending stimulus fading procedures with forward chaining to address treatment resistance in an adult with an autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 8(2), 215–218.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0060-5","y":2015,"sum":"A 23-year-old man with autism, who hit, destroyed property and hurt himself when told to ride a van to his day program, was taught a 10-step routine running from putting on his shoes to walking into the program. Steps were added one at a time by forward chaining, with a preferred video after the final step, and the familiar people who rode with him at first (his mother, the consultant, the house manager) were removed one by one until a single staff member…"},{"k":"guess1968","apa":"Guess, D., Sailor, W., Rutherford, G., & Baer, D. M. (1968). An experimental analysis of linguistic development: The productive use of the plural morpheme. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(4), 297–306.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-297","y":1968,"sum":"A 10-year-old girl with severe intellectual disability living in an institution, who had learned single words and short phrases in an earlier program, was shown one object or a pair and asked what she saw, with bites of food for the correct singular or plural name and a model of the correct name after an error; each new word went through one-object, two-object and mixed stages, each ending after three correct in a row."},{"k":"guess1969","apa":"Guess, D. (1969). A functional analysis of receptive language and productive speech: Acquisition of the plural morpheme. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 55–64.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-55","y":1969,"sum":"Two 13-year-old boys with Down syndrome and intellectual disability living in an institution, who did not use plurals but could say the s and z sounds, were first taught to point to one object or a pair when told to point to the singular or plural name, then taught to say the singular or plural name of one object or a pair, and finally taught to point the reverse way, with chips traded for sweets or toys after about every third correct answer."},{"k":"guess1973","apa":"Guess, D., & Baer, D. M. (1973). An analysis of individual differences in generalization between receptive and productive language in retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 311–329.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-311","y":1973,"sum":"Four residents of a state institution with severe intellectual disability (aged 11, 13, 20 and 21; three with Down syndrome), who named objects but did not use plurals, were taught with tokens and praise to name one or two objects and, separately, to point to one or two objects when asked, each with a different plural ending (-s or -es)."},{"k":"gunby2010","apa":"Gunby, K. V., Carr, J. E., & LeBlanc, L. A. (2010). Teaching abduction-prevention skills to children with autism. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 43(1), 107–112.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2010.43-107","y":2010,"sum":"Boys with autism aged 6 to 8 were taught with behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback) and feedback in the real setting to say no to a stranger’s lure, leave at once for a safe place and tell a familiar adult; all three learned it and kept it a month later."},{"k":"guralnick1976","apa":"Guralnick, M. J. (1976). The acquisition of prepositional motor responses in handicapped children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(4), 500.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-500","y":1976,"sum":"Three preschool children with disabilities were taught to place objects as directed by three pairs of position words, using a specially devised prompting technique. Probe trials at each stage checked whether the concept carried over to untaught items, and the study tested how much the children relied on the objects named in the direction; only a one-page summary was printed, so ages, settings and criteria are not given."},{"k":"hagopian1998","apa":"Hagopian, L. P., Fisher, W. W., Thibault Sullivan, M., Acquisto, J., & LeBlanc, L. A. (1998). Effectiveness of functional communication training with and without extinction and punishment: A summary of 21 inpatient cases. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 31(2), 211–235.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1998.31-211","y":1998,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"hagopian2011","apa":"Hagopian, L. P., Boelter, E. W., & Jarmolowicz, D. P. (2011). Reinforcement schedule thinning following functional communication training: Review and recommendations. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 4(1), 4–16.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391770","y":2011,"sum":"This review with practice recommendations covers 19 FCT studies with people aged 2–34 who had intellectual disability or autism and showed aggression, self-injury or property destruction. It describes four ways to make a newly taught request practical. The first is lengthening the delay after a request, signaled with a wait cue. The second is demand fading, which requires more work before a break request is honored."},{"k":"hains1989","apa":"Hains, A. A. (1989). An anger-control intervention with aggressive delinquent youths. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(3), 213–230.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040305","y":1989,"sum":"Four boys aged 15–17 with conduct disorder and histories of violent offenses, living in a state facility for youth, met one at a time with a therapist twice a week for five weeks. The therapist first helped each youth break his anger into triggers, reactions and results, then modeled four problem-solving steps on short story dilemmas, had him write calming reminders of his own on a card, and taught him to think ahead about what an outburst would lead to,…"},{"k":"hall1968","apa":"Hall, R. V., Lund, D., & Jackson, D. (1968). Effects of teacher attention on study behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(1), 1–12.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-1","y":1968,"sum":"Five third graders and one first grader at two elementary schools in a low-income urban neighborhood, nominated by their teachers for disrupting class or dawdling, were observed during a 30-minute seatwork period. After baseline, each teacher saw the student's graph and was taught to give brief praise or a touch on the shoulder after about a minute of continuous study (at first when an observer raised a colored card) and to stop attending to non-study…"},{"k":"hall1968a","apa":"Hall, R. V., Panyan, M., Rabon, D., & Broden, M. (1968). Instructing beginning teachers in reinforcement procedures which improve classroom control. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(4), 315–322.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-315","y":1968,"sum":"Three first-year teachers (grades 1, 6 and 7) in low-income urban and small-town public schools, with classes described as out of control, were shown their class data and asked to praise studying more often; the first-grade teacher later let good studiers play a favorite quiet game after reading, and the seventh-grade teacher took seconds off the class break for each disruption."},{"k":"hall1970","apa":"Hall, R. V., Cristler, C., Cranston, S. S., & Tucker, B. (1970). Teachers and parents as researchers using multiple baseline designs. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(4), 247–255.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-247","y":1970,"sum":"Three studies were run by teachers and a parent: a fifth-grade class of 25 whose names were posted on a chart for returning on time from recess; three tenth graders whose D or F French quiz grades required staying after school for tutoring; and a 10-year-old girl whose bedtime moved one minute earlier for each minute under 30 she spent on each of three activities."},{"k":"hall1971","apa":"Hall, R. V., Fox, R., Willard, D., Goldsmith, L., Emerson, M., Owen, M., Davis, F., & Porcia, E. (1971). The teacher as observer and experimenter in the modification of disputing and talking-out behaviors. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 141–149.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-141","y":1971,"sum":"Six teachers in a graduate course each ran a reversal study in their own class, from first grade to junior high (including special classes for students with mild intellectual disability or emotional disturbance): they ignored arguing or calling out and gave attention, praise and in some classes a game, favorite activity or end-of-week treat for starting work without argument or for raising a hand and waiting."},{"k":"hall1972","apa":"Hall, R. V., Axelrod, S., Tyler, L., Grief, E., Jones, F. C., & Robertson, R. (1972). Modification of behavior problems in the home with a parent as observer and experimenter. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 53–64.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-53","y":1972,"sum":"Four parents in a college course each ran a reversal-design study at home: a teenage boy’s wearing of orthodontic headgear (praise, then money at five unpredictable daily checks), a 10-year-old girl’s household chores (points traded for pennies and wanted items), a 4-year-old’s whining (ignoring) and a 5-year-old’s dressing time (loss of TV)."},{"k":"hall1987","apa":"Hall, G., & Sundberg, M. L. (1987). Teaching mands by manipulating conditioned establishing operations. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 5, 41–53.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392819","y":1987,"sum":"Three children with autism aged 5–8, who already asked for things they could see, first learned three favorite multi-step activities (such as toasting bread for a peanut-butter sandwich or putting a CD on to listen) with hands-on guidance that was faded. Then one needed item was left out: at that step the teacher waited 10 seconds, said the item's name if the child had not asked, and gave it as soon as the child said it."},{"k":"hanley2001","apa":"Hanley, G. P., Iwata, B. A., & Thompson, R. H. (2001). Reinforcement schedule thinning following treatment with functional communication training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 34(1), 17–38.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2001.34-17","y":2001,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"hanley2007","apa":"Hanley, G. P., Heal, N. A., Tiger, J. H., & Ingvarsson, E. T. (2007). Evaluation of a classwide teaching program for developing preschool life skills. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 40(2), 277–300.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2007.57-06","y":2007,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"hanley2014","apa":"Hanley, G. P., Jin, C. S., Vanselow, N. R., & Hanratty, L. A. (2014). Producing meaningful improvements in problem behavior of children with autism via synthesized analyses and treatments. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 47(1), 16–36.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.106","y":2014,"sum":"After the request is reliable, some requests are answered with \"not now\" and an alternative is offered; a calm \"okay\" is praised and often earns the item a little later. The denials are rare at first and grow more frequent. A second request, for an alternative, can be taught too, with signals for which one will be answered."},{"k":"hansen2019","apa":"Hansen, B., DeSouza, A. A., Stuart, A. L., & Shillingsburg, M. A. (2019). Clinical application of a high-probability sequence to promote compliance with vocal imitation in a child with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 199–203.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00280-y","y":2019,"sum":"A 9-year-old girl with intellectual disability, living in an institution and silent for about seven years, was asked twice a day to say words and then phrases of up to four words, earning a bite of a preferred food for each acceptable try; at first silent mouthing and whispers were rewarded, then only audible, clearly spoken repetitions. She came to repeat long phrases accurately in most trials."},{"k":"hardiman1975","apa":"Hardiman, S. A., Goetz, E. M., Reuter, K. E., & LeBlanc, J. M. (1975). Primes, contingent attention, and training: Effects on a child’s motor behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 399–409.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-399","y":1975,"sum":"A girl of 4½ with cerebral palsy in a university preschool, who avoided the large-motor equipment, was observed for 20 minutes of outdoor play in 10-second intervals for nearness, touching, unskilled use and skilled use of six activities."},{"k":"harding2009","apa":"Harding, J. W., Wacker, D. P., Berg, W. K., Lee, J. F., & Dolezal, D. (2009). Conducting functional communication training in home settings: A case study and recommendations for practitioners. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 2(1), 21–33.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391734","y":2009,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"haring1987","apa":"Haring, T. G., Kennedy, C. H., Adams, M. J., & Pitts-Conway, V. (1987). Teaching generalization of purchasing skills across community settings to autistic youth using videotape modeling. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 20(1), 89–96.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1987.20-89","y":1987,"sum":"Young adults with autism were first taught to buy items in one store, and the skill did not carry over to other stores; adding videotapes of people buying items in those stores, with questions about what they did, produced purchasing in all of them. Students with autism and intellectual disability learned to pay on their own with a counting-on, next-dollar strategy, used it in community stores and kept it for weeks."},{"k":"harris1973a","apa":"Harris, V. W., & Sherman, J. A. (1973b). Use and analysis of the \"good behavior game\" to reduce disruptive classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 405–417.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-405","y":1973,"sum":"A fifth-grade class of 22 and a sixth-grade class of 28 played the good behavior game: two teams, a mark on the board for each instance of talking, leaving a seat or throwing things without permission, and 10 minutes' early dismissal for any team at or under a set number of marks or with fewer marks than the other team."},{"k":"harris1973b","apa":"Harris, V. W., & Sherman, J. A. (1973a). Effects of peer tutoring and consequences on the math performance of elementary classroom students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 587–597.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-587","y":1973,"sum":"Twenty-four fourth graders in a low-income area, and later 25 fifth graders, spent 15 minutes in groups of two or three helping each other with math problems identical or similar to those they would next work alone, before one of two daily 20-minute sessions built from matched halves of the same assignment; some phases added early recess for 90% correct, and one compared tutoring with 15 minutes of studying alone."},{"k":"harris1974","apa":"Harris, V. W., & Sherman, J. A. (1974). Homework assignments, consequences, and classroom performance in social studies and mathematics. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 505–519.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-505","y":1974,"sum":"In two sixth-grade general education classes, students got nightly social studies reading plus 15 fill-in questions that matched the next day's team quiz game, and in different phases could leave school 10 or 15 minutes early for at least 80% correct (in some phases also staying in at recess to fix the homework); in a third sixth-grade class, math homework matching one of two daily math periods earned early lunch and early dismissal, first for handing it…"},{"k":"hart1968","apa":"Hart, B. M., Reynolds, N. J., Baer, D. M., Brawley, E. R., & Harris, F. R. (1968). Effect of contingent and non-contingent social reinforcement on the cooperative play of a preschool child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(1), 73–76.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-73","y":1968,"sum":"A typically developing 5-year-old girl in a university preschool, often near other children but rarely playing with them, was studied in a reversal design. Teachers gave her close attention and approval either at random for about 80% of the session or only after cooperative play, which was shaped from her answers to peers whom teachers had prompted to invite her, and then thinned at the end."},{"k":"hart1968a","apa":"Hart, B. M., & Risley, T. R. (1968). Establishing use of descriptive adjectives in the spontaneous speech of disadvantaged preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(2), 109–120.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-109","y":1968,"sum":"Fifteen 4- and 5-year-olds from very low-income families in a year-long preschool were sampled during free play (15-minute longhand records of everything a child said) for color, number, size and shape words, with and without a following noun."},{"k":"hart1974","apa":"Hart, B., & Risley, T. R. (1974). Using preschool materials to modify the language of disadvantaged children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 243–256.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-243","y":1974,"sum":"Twelve Black 4-year-olds from low-income families in a Kansas City preschool had most play materials kept in view but given out only on request during free play, with the requirement raised in steps over the school year: the material's name, then a describing word with the name (later a color word), then a compound sentence giving the reason."},{"k":"hart1975","apa":"Hart, B., & Risley, T. R. (1975). Incidental teaching of language in the preschool. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 411–420.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-411","y":1975,"sum":"Eleven children aged about 4½ to 5 from low-income families (PPVT IQs 51–101) were recorded for 15 minutes a day of free play across a preschool year. With play materials kept in sight but out of reach, a teacher who was asked for an item asked what it was for and prompted, only as much as needed, a sentence that gave the reason, then handed it over with praise; later she had the child ask a nearby classmate instead."},{"k":"hasazi1972","apa":"Hasazi, J. E., & Hasazi, S. E. (1972). Effects of teacher attention on digit-reversal behavior in an elementary school child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(2), 157–162.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-157","y":1972,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy in a basic skills class who reversed the digits of nearly every two-digit sum did 20 addition problems each morning; in treatment the teacher praised correctly ordered answers and gave reversals no comment or extra help. Reversals per day were counted from the worksheets, rescored by an independent judge, in an ABAB design over 28 school days, with no mastery criterion."},{"k":"hauserman1973","apa":"Hauserman, N., Walen, S. R., & Behling, M. (1973). Reinforced racial integration in the first grade: A study in generalization. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 193–200.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-193","y":1973,"sum":"In a first-grade class of 25, data were kept on five Black children who always ate and played only with one another; every child in the class could earn tickets for sitting with, and staying through lunch beside, a classmate different from the day before, each ticket bought a cookie, candy or pretzel at afternoon recess, and one short phase assigned partners by drawing names."},{"k":"haydon2024","apa":"Haydon, T., & Carnahan, C. (2024). Implementing a picture prompt and proximity intervention in a classroom with an adult learner: A case study. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 17(2), 638–642.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-024-00924-2","y":2024,"sum":"Six students in grades 1 and 3 in a low-income urban neighborhood, picked for disrupting class or dawdling, had been getting most of their teacher's attention while they were off task. Their teachers saw the baseline graph and then went to the student with brief praise or a pat on the shoulder after about a minute of steady work (at first when an observer held up a card) and stopped responding to off-task behavior; for a girl who did no work at all,…"},{"k":"hazel1982","apa":"Hazel, J. S., Schumaker, J. B., Sherman, J. A., & Sheldon, J. (1982). Application of a group training program in social skills and problem solving to learning disabled and non-learning disabled youth. Learning Disability Quarterly, 5(4), 398–408.","doi":"10.2307/1510923","y":1982,"sum":"Taught with behavioral skills training: the steps described with a reason for each, modeled, rehearsed in role play with feedback, then checked in new situations. Adolescents with learning disabilities learned to accept negative feedback in group training; adults with autism learned to respond to a supervisor’s correction at work, with cue cards and several examples when needed."},{"k":"heffernan2016","apa":"Heffernan, L., & Lyons, D. (2016). Differential reinforcement of other behaviour for the reduction of severe nail biting. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 253–256.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0106-3","y":2016,"sum":"One 4-year-old boy with autism in an early intensive preschool program whose nail biting, most likely when alone, had caused visible tissue damage; a functional behavior assessment pointed to automatic reinforcement. Nail biting dropped from a baseline mean of 38 per session to about 5 per session at the 20-s interval, met the thinning criterion by session 7, and averaged about 2 per session while the interval was stretched to 60 min."},{"k":"henson1975","apa":"Henson, F. O., II (1975). An investigation of the effects of token reinforcement on divergent verbal responding. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 459.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-459","y":1975,"sum":"Six public-school children (two described as gifted, two as average and two with learning disabilities) answered spoken items from three verbal parts of a children’s creativity test, earning one token for each appropriate answer, traded for items on a menu. Multiple-baseline and reversal designs showed large increases in the number of answers, but no spread across the three kinds of item or across conditions; agreement on appropriateness was 99%."},{"k":"herbert1972","apa":"Herbert, E. W., & Baer, D. M. (1972). Training parents as behavior modifiers: Self-recording of contingent attention. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(2), 139–149.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-139","y":1972,"sum":"Two mothers of 5-year-olds from a special preschool class (a boy diagnosed with autism and a girl described as hyperactive) counted their own attention to the child’s appropriate behavior on a wrist counter during an hour at home. An observer recorded the mother’s attention and the child’s appropriate and inappropriate behavior in 10-second intervals across counting and no-counting conditions, with follow-up checks up to 24 weeks later; there was no…"},{"k":"herman1971","apa":"Herman, S. H., & Tramontana, J. (1971). Instructions and group versus individual reinforcement in modifying disruptive group behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 113–119.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-113","y":1971,"sum":"Six Head Start children aged 5 to 6 who were often off their mats and talking at rest time were split into two matched groups of three; one group earned ping-pong ball tokens toward a small toy individually and the other as a group, first with no explanation of the rules and then with exact instructions about lying quietly on the mat. Time sampling of 10-second intervals measured inappropriate behavior in an empty classroom and later in the regular class."},{"k":"hermann1973","apa":"Hermann, J. A., de Montes, A. I., Dominguez, B., Montes, F., & Hopkins, B. L. (1973). Effects of bonuses for punctuality on the tardiness of industrial workers. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 563–570.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-563","y":1973,"sum":"Six male factory workers aged 19 to 40 in Mexico City who had been late 10 or more times the year before received a daily slip worth about 16 US cents for clocking in by their start time, and cashed the slips each week. Time-card records of late arrivals over 77 weeks were compared across an ABABAB design and with six similar workers who received no bonus; there was no mastery criterion."},{"k":"hersen1976","apa":"Hersen, M., & Bellack, A. S. (1976). A multiple-baseline analysis of social-skills training in chronic schizophrenics. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 239–245.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-239","y":1976,"sum":"Two men aged 19 and 27 with schizophrenia in a hospital day program practiced eight role-played scenes, half involving praise and half involving an unreasonable request (for example a boss asking them to stay late yet again), with male and female partners."},{"k":"hester1977","apa":"Hester, P., & Hendrickson, J. (1977). Training functional expressive language: The acquisition and generalization of five-element syntactic responses. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 316.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-316","y":1977,"sum":"Abstract only (the full report was deposited with an auxiliary publication service). Three young children with language delays in a regional early intervention program were taught in individual sessions to describe live actions of familiar people with familiar objects using an expanded who-action-object sentence form they had not used before."},{"k":"hittner1994","apa":"Hittner, J. B. (1994). Case study: The combined use of imipramine and behavior modification to reduce aggression in an adult male diagnosed as having autistic disorder. Behavioral Interventions, 9(2), 123–139.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360090207","y":1994,"sum":"A 25-year-old man with autism and severe intellectual disability living in an intermediate care facility and attending a day-treatment program, with daily aggression to people and property and self-injury (biting, hitting, scratching, hair pulling)."},{"k":"hobbs1976","apa":"Hobbs, T. R., & Holt, M. M. (1976). The effects of token reinforcement on the behavior of delinquents in cottage settings. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 189–198.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-189","y":1976,"sum":"125 boys aged 12 to 15 (mean IQ 75; 30% with mild or moderate intellectual disability) committed by the courts to a state training school, in three living units compared with one untreated unit, where staff were concerned about assault (cursing, threats, hitting, kicking, shoving), little peer interaction and disorder when walking as a group."},{"k":"hoch2009","apa":"Hoch, H., Taylor, B. A., & Rodriguez, A. (2009). Teaching teenagers with autism to answer cell phones and seek assistance when lost. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 2(1), 14–20.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391733","y":2009,"sum":"Teenagers with autism with severe communication deficits were taught at school and then in the community to answer a cell phone when lost and follow the directions given, seeking help from an unfamiliar adult by handing over a communication card; they did so in community sites where they were never taught and with their parents."},{"k":"hood2017","apa":"Hood, S. A., Luczynski, K. C., & Mitteer, D. R. (2017). Toward meaningful outcomes in teaching conversation and greeting skills with individuals with autism spectrum disorder. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 50(3), 459–486.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.388","y":2017,"sum":"A child and two adolescents with autism were taught greeting and conversation skills chosen with their parents, with behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback); the skills carried over to unscripted talks with new adults. Adolescents were also taught in small groups with the teaching interaction procedure; carry-over to typical peers was partial, so plan practice with them."},{"k":"hopkins1968","apa":"Hopkins, B. L. (1968). Effects of candy and social reinforcement, instructions, and reinforcement schedule leaning on the modification and maintenance of smiling. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(2), 121–129.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-121","y":1968,"sum":"Two boys with intellectual disability, aged 10 and 8, at a university day school took walks around the school while the experimenter and an observer counted whether each boy smiled within 5 seconds of a greeting from a person they met."},{"k":"hopkins1971","apa":"Hopkins, B. L., Schutte, R. C., & Garton, K. L. (1971). The effects of access to a playroom on the rate and quality of printing and writing of first and second-grade students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 77–87.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-77","y":1971,"sum":"Fourteen first graders and 10 second graders in a rural two-grade classroom copied a daily chalkboard assignment by printing or cursive; in baseline they waited at their seats when done, and in the experimental phases they went to a partitioned playroom of toys as soon as their paper was scored, with the total time for work plus play later shortened from 50 to 35 minutes."},{"k":"horner1971","apa":"Horner, R. D. (1971). Establishing use of crutches by a mentally retarded spina bifida child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(3), 183–189.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-183","y":1971,"sum":"A 5-year-old boy with moderate intellectual disability, spina bifida, hydrocephalus and paralysis of the legs (with leg braces), living in an institution, who had met six months of physical therapy gait training with tantrums and physical resistance. The boy learned all six parallel-bar steps; when the drink was withheld he stopped responding after four tries, and one free reinforcer restored full success."},{"k":"horner1975","apa":"Horner, R. D., & Keilitz, I. (1975). Training mentally retarded adolescents to brush their teeth. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(3), 301–309.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-301","y":1975,"sum":"Toothbrushing is broken into steps; each step is prompted from least to most help as needed and the help is faded, with praise for each step done."},{"k":"houlihan1991b","apa":"Houlihan, D., Sloane, H. N., Jenson, W. R., & Levine, H. D. (1991). Treating preschool children with multiple behavior problems: Testing for the response covariation phenomenon. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(5), 321–340.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060503","y":1991,"sum":"Four preschoolers (3 to 5 years) with severe noncompliance in special preschool classrooms got 15 simple 'do' requests in each 45-minute table session and earned a poker chip for complying within 20 seconds, or in other phases for being on task at signaled moments (three chips bought a sticker, stamp or eraser), in counterbalanced reversal designs."},{"k":"houten1974","apa":"Van Houten, R., Morrison, E., Jarvis, R., & McDonald, M. (1974). The effects of explicit timing and feedback on compositional response rate in elementary school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 547–555.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-547","y":1974,"sum":"Children in one second-grade and two fifth-grade classes (17 to 21 students each) wrote a 10-minute composition on a set topic each day; in the experimental phases the period was openly timed with a stopwatch, each child counted the words they had written, each child's best score so far was posted, and they were told to try to beat it, with no praise or prizes."},{"k":"houten1975b","apa":"Van Houten, R., Hill, S., & Parsons, M. (1975). An analysis of a performance feedback system: The effects of timing and feedback, public posting, and praise upon academic performance and peer interaction. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 449–457.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-449","y":1975,"sum":"In two fourth-grade classes in a low-income area of Halifax (19 and 20 children), daily composition writing for 20 or 10 minutes was scored as words per minute: announcing the timing and having children count their own words about doubled the rate, posting each child’s best score on the board raised it further and its removal lowered it, and teacher praise helped one class only; on-task behavior and children’s talk about their scores rose with the rate."},{"k":"houten1976","apa":"Van Houten, R., & Thompson, C. (1976). The effects of explicit timing on math performance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 227–230.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-227","y":1976,"sum":"Twenty second graders placed in one class because of poor school performance worked for 30 minutes a day on worksheets of 100 addition or subtraction facts, either untimed or in repeated 1-minute timed bursts that began on a start cue and ended with a line drawn after the last answer."},{"k":"howard1988","apa":"Howard, J. S., & Rice, D. E. (1988). Establishing a generalized autoclitic repertoire in preschool children. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 6, 45–59.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392828","y":1988,"sum":"Three students with autism aged 9 to 11 at a special school were taught with PEAK direct-training programs in 10-trial discrete-trial blocks, with least-to-most prompts that were stepped down after each success and reinforcers the student chose."},{"k":"huffman2016","apa":"Huffman, R. W., Sainato, D. M., & Curiel, E. S. L. (2016). Correspondence training using special interests to increase compliance during transitions: An emerging technology. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(1), 25–33.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0100-1","y":2016,"sum":"Showing the coming transition on a short video just before it (video priming) reduced disruption for children with autism. A warning, a toy to carry or a visual schedule alone did not help in other studies; they worked once moving on earned reinforcement and problem behavior no longer delayed the move."},{"k":"hundert1976","apa":"Hundert, J. (1976). The effectiveness of reinforcement, response cost, and mixed programs on classroom behaviors. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 107.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-107","y":1976,"sum":"Six elementary school children went through baseline, training and token-withdrawal phases; in training each child had, in counterbalanced order, tokens earned for paying attention and for correct arithmetic, free tokens that were lost for inattention and low arithmetic output, and a mix of the two."},{"k":"hundert1978","apa":"Hundert, J., & Bucher, B. (1978). Pupils' self-scored arithmetic performance: A practical procedure for maintaining accuracy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(2), 304.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-304","y":1978,"sum":"Two special-education classes of boys (four aged about 10 and 17 aged about 15) answered 20-minute arithmetic assignments in pen, scored their own papers in pencil from a projected key and reported the score. When points for the self-reported scores were introduced, 13 of the 17 older students inflated their scores by more than two problems a session."},{"k":"ingvarsson2007","apa":"Ingvarsson, E. T., Tiger, J. H., Hanley, G. P., & Stephenson, K. M. (2007). An evaluation of intraverbal training to generate socially appropriate responses to novel questions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 40(3), 411–429.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2007.40-411","y":2007,"sum":"Preschoolers were taught to say they did not know, and then to add a request to be told, for questions they could not answer; both responses carried over to new questions and teachers. For three of four children it also spread to questions they could answer, so known questions are kept in the mix and answering them is reinforced."},{"k":"ishikawa2019","apa":"Ishikawa, N., Omori, M., & Yamamoto, J. (2019). Modeling training of child's echoic conversational response for students with autism spectrum disorder: To be a good listener. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 1–11.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0271-7","y":2019,"sum":"A child and two adolescents with autism were taught greeting and conversation skills chosen with their parents, with behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback); the skills carried over to unscripted talks with new adults. Adolescents were also taught in small groups with the teaching interaction procedure; carry-over to typical peers was partial, so plan practice with them."},{"k":"israel1977","apa":"Israel, A. C., & Brown, M. S. (1977). Correspondence training, prior verbal training, and control of nonverbal behavior via control of verbal behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 333–338.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-333","y":1977,"sum":"Sixteen Head Start children averaging 4 years 8 months were asked before a 15-minute free play what they would play with and answered in an \"I\" sentence; in two groups of eight, a snack was earned either for naming a target toy (dinosaurs) and later for both naming it and then playing with it, or for naming and playing from the start. In a final phase only naming a new toy (cars) earned the snack, to test whether saying had come to control doing."},{"k":"iwata1974","apa":"Iwata, B. A., & Bailey, J. S. (1974). Reward versus cost token systems: An analysis of the effects on students and teacher. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 567–576.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-567","y":1974,"sum":"Fifteen students in an elementary special education class (mean age 10, mean IQ about 70) did math seatwork while a taped tone sounded every 3 to 5 minutes; one group earned a token at each tone if no class rule had been broken, the other started with 10 tokens and lost one for each interval with a broken rule, the groups later switched, and 6 and then 8 tokens earned the morning snack, with occasional surprise prizes for top scores."},{"k":"jackson1974","apa":"Jackson, D. A., & Wallace, R. F. (1974). The modification and generalization of voice loudness in a fifteen-year-old retarded girl. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 461–471.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-461","y":1974,"sum":"A 15-year-old girl with mild intellectual disability in a public school program for students with emotional disturbance, whose speech was almost inaudible, read single words aloud in a small sound-treated booth and earned a token (for items she chose) for each word loud enough to trip a voice-operated switch, with the loudness needed raised a step whenever she met it on about 80% of words; the training was later moved step by step toward her class."},{"k":"jackson2021","apa":"Jackson, M. L., Nuñez, R. M., Maraach, D., Wilhite, C. J., & Moschella, J. D. (2021). Teaching comprehension of double-meaning jokes to young children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 54(3), 1095–1110.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.838","y":2021,"sum":"Four typically developing 5- to 6-year-olds who could not yet explain simple jokes heard 5 jokes and 5 literal statements per session; correct explanations earned specific praise, and errors were followed by a leading question, a specific question, a drawing of both meanings and finally a model to repeat, with the joke re-presented at each step."},{"k":"jacobson1969","apa":"Jacobson, J. M., Bushell, D., Jr., & Risley, T. (1969). Switching requirements in a Head Start classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 43–47.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-43","y":1969,"sum":"About 30 children in a parent-staffed Head Start preschool class (18 in the second year; no disabilities reported), with frequent moves from one free-choice activity area to another as the target. With a one-row task, the group's average fell from 5.5 switches per hour on the first day to about 2 per hour within 13 days and stayed there for about 100 more school days; for five children, more rows meant fewer switches."},{"k":"jenkins2017","apa":"Jenkins, S. R., LeBlanc, L. A., & Lambert, G. P. (2017). Treating food approval-seeking behavior: One bite at a time. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 411–416.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0170-8","y":2017,"sum":"A 6-year-old boy with autism at a center-based program ate only when an adult prompted or approved each bite, sometimes sitting for 90 minutes without eating. At lunch (foods he chose from home, never earned or withheld), the therapist told him to take a set number of bites, raising the number from 2 to 17 after three successes in a row, then added a general instruction to eat the lunch, and finally gave one general instruction at the start, with praise;…"},{"k":"jensen1993","apa":"Jensen, C. C., & Heidorn, S. D. (1993). Ten-year follow-up of a successful treatment of self-injurious behavior. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(4), 263–280.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080405","y":1993,"sum":"A man with profound intellectual disability, treated at 27 in a state developmental center, who had blinded himself by eye gouging and who struck, rubbed and dug at his face and head as soon as simple requests were made. Self-injury that had caused blindness, and had kept him in restraints for most waking hours, fell to low levels during the 1979–80 treatment, and by its end he ate and dressed himself and worked up to 45 minutes without self-injury."},{"k":"jessel2018","apa":"Jessel, J., Ingvarsson, E. T., Metras, R., Kirk, H., & Whipple, R. (2018). Achieving socially significant reductions in problem behavior following the interview-informed synthesized contingency analysis: A summary of 25 outpatient applications. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 51(1), 130–157.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.436","y":2018,"sum":"The wait begins very short (a few seconds) and grows only after the student waits well at the current length. A clear signal starts the wait, and something to do during it helps. In the studies, waiting improved most when calm waiting itself was what produced the item."},{"k":"johnson1974b","apa":"Johnson, M., & Bailey, J. S. (1974). Cross-age tutoring: Fifth graders as arithmetic tutors for kindergarten children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 223–232.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-223","y":1974,"sum":"Five kindergartners who could not count objects to 10 or name the numerals 1 to 10 were tutored 20 minutes a day by trained fifth graders, with flashcards for counting aloud, counting objects, pointing to a named numeral and naming a numeral in the ranges 0 to 10, 11 to 20, the tens and 21 to 99, while five matched classmates were not tutored."},{"k":"johnson1977","apa":"Johnson, M. S., & Bailey, J. S. (1977). The modification of leisure behavior in a half-way house for retarded women. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 273–282.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-273","y":1977,"sum":"Fourteen women aged 17 to 33 with mild intellectual disability, living in a community half-way house that taught independent-living skills, were offered an evening leisure program of familiar activities (painting, clay, puzzles, cards) and two new crafts (loom weaving and latch-hook rugs)."},{"k":"johnston1972","apa":"Johnston, J. M., & Johnston, G. T. (1972). Modification of consonant speech-sound articulation in young children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 233–246.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-233","y":1972,"sum":"Twelve children aged 6 to 8 with severe articulation problems, whose target consonant had been taught earlier in speech therapy but was seldom said correctly elsewhere, took part in small-group talking activities and free play in a summer program, where a speech therapist or kindergarten teacher counted every correct and incorrect use of each child's sound in ordinary talk."},{"k":"jones1975","apa":"Jones, F. H., & Eimers, R. C. (1975). Role playing to train elementary teachers to use a classroom management \"skill package\". Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 421–433.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-421","y":1975,"sum":"Two regular third-grade classes of about 28 students each in a suburban school (about a third described as having learning or behavior problems), with talking to neighbors, leaving seats and calling out during seatwork and discussions as targets; the teachers learned the package through role play."},{"k":"jones1977c","apa":"Jones, J. C., Trap, J., & Cooper, J. O. (1977). Technical report: Students' self-recording of manuscript letter strokes. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 509–514.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-509","y":1977,"sum":"Twenty-two first graders aged 6 to 8 in a regular inner-city classroom, all with at least 75% on a check of prerequisite skills, were taught in groups of seven or eight to align a plastic evaluative overlay over their writing, judge each letter stroke against the overlay, and trace and total their correct strokes on a self-recording form, through demonstration, praise, prompting questions and feedback on training and practice sheets."},{"k":"jones2014","apa":"Jones, A. S., & Zarcone, J. R. (2014). Comparison of prompting strategies on two types of tasks with children diagnosed with autism spectrum disorders. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 51–60.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0010-7","y":2014,"sum":"Three adolescents (13, 14 and 17) with autism and mild to severe intellectual disability, hospital inpatients for aggression, property destruction or self-injury, were taught matching tasks and picking out named pictures in a therapy room."},{"k":"kale1968","apa":"Kale, R. J., Kaye, J. H., Whelan, P. A., & Hopkins, B. L. (1968). The effects of reinforcement on the modification, maintenance, and generalization of social responses of mental patients. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(4), 307–314.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-307","y":1968,"sum":"Three men aged 34 to 41 with long-term schizophrenia, in a psychiatric hospital for 12 to 20 years and never greeting staff, were approached by a staff member several times a day; a greeting within 10 seconds earned a cigarette and friendly talk, first after a spoken prompt with a cigarette held up, then with the prompts faded and cigarettes given less and less often until only talk followed."},{"k":"kapoor2025","apa":"Kapoor, G., Vostanis, A., Mejía-Buenaño, S., & Langdon, P. E. (2025). Using precision teaching to improve typically developing student's mathematical skills via teleconferencing. Journal of Behavioral Education, 34(1), 109–138.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-023-09520-w","y":2025,"sum":"High school students with learning and emotional disabilities checked their pace when a vibrating cue went off, against marks on the worksheet, then graphed their results and set goals; correct answers per minute rose while errors stayed low. Younger students with mild disabilities graphed their own rate on a computer against an aim line, and two of three met or beat it nearly every session."},{"k":"kasson2017","apa":"Kasson, E. M., & Wilson, A. N. (2017). Preliminary evidence on the efficacy of mindfulness combined with traditional classroom management strategies. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(3), 242–251.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0160-x","y":2017,"sum":"Two teachers of a general education third-grade class were trained to use an attention signal, timed transitions with class rewards, praise for on-task students and a color incentive chart; researchers then led short mindfulness exercises (quiet game, paced breathing, body and sense awareness) two or three times a week, and two students added self-monitoring."},{"k":"kaufman1972","apa":"Kaufman, K. F., & O'Leary, K. D. (1972). Reward, cost, and self-evaluation procedures for disruptive adolescents in a psychiatric hospital school. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 293–309.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-293","y":1972,"sum":"Sixteen adolescents (average age 15; 14 boys and 2 girls) with psychiatric diagnoses, reading delays and frequent disruption at a psychiatric hospital school were placed in two matched after-school reading classes with posted rules, where the teacher rated each student every 15 minutes and either gave up to 10 tokens or took tokens from 10 given at the start, tokens buying candy, toys, cosmetics and records; in a final phase the students rated themselves…"},{"k":"kazdin1973a","apa":"Kazdin, A. E. (1973a). The effect of vicarious reinforcement on attentive behavior in the classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(1), 71–78.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-71","y":1973,"sum":"Four students aged 8 to 12 with moderate intellectual disability in a laboratory-school class of 14 were observed in two seated pairs during daily handwriting seatwork. An adult beside each pair praised one child by name after every fifth 5-second interval of attentive work (seated, working, not talking), later after inattentive intervals as a test, while the neighbor was never praised."},{"k":"kazdin1973d","apa":"Kazdin, A. E., & Klock, J. (1973). The effect of nonverbal teacher approval on student attentive behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 643–654.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-643","y":1973,"sum":"In a university laboratory school class of 12 students aged 7 to 12 with moderate intellectual disability doing handwriting seatwork, the teacher was coached to smile at and touch students right after they were working, without adding spoken praise."},{"k":"kazdin1975c","apa":"Kazdin, A. E., Silverman, N. A., & Sittler, J. L. (1975). The use of prompts to enhance vicarious effects of nonverbal approval. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(3), 279–286.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-279","y":1975,"sum":"Four children aged 7 to 9 with mild intellectual disability (IQ 70–76), chosen by their special-class teacher for disruption and inattention, were observed in two side-by-side pairs during daily seatwork in 10-second intervals. In each pair one child received several approving pats from an aide or the teacher after every 30 seconds of continuous attentive work; that child’s attention rose from about 50% to 80–90% of intervals and fell in reversal."},{"k":"kazdin1977b","apa":"Kazdin, A. E. (1977b). The influence of behavior preceding a reinforced response on behavior change in the classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 299–310.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-299","y":1977,"sum":"Three children aged 8 and 9 with intellectual disability (IQ 68 to 75) in a special-education class at a university demonstration school, chosen for inattention and disruption during seatwork, received praise (and in the second experiment teacher-given check marks traded for small items, candy or extra recess) for 10 seconds of working."},{"k":"keilitz1973","apa":"Keilitz, I., Tucker, D. J., & Horner, R. D. (1973). Increasing mentally retarded adolescents' verbalizations about current events. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 621–630.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-621","y":1973,"sum":"Three 15-year-old boys with borderline or moderate intellectual disability at a state hospital and training center watched a taped network newscast of 6 to 14 stories and told an adult what they had seen, either after the whole newscast or with the tape stopped after each story, with or without poker-chip tokens worth a penny each and praise for each correct part."},{"k":"kifer1974","apa":"Kifer, R. E., Lewis, M. A., Green, D. R., & Phillips, E. L. (1974). Training predelinquent youths and their parents to negotiate conflict situations. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 357–364.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-357","y":1974,"sum":"Three teenagers aged 13, 16 and 17 who had been before a juvenile court (two living in family-style group homes) and one parent each came to weekly classroom sessions, where trainers talked through a written conflict scenario, its possible responses and their likely results, then explained, modeled and had the pair role-play three negotiation steps (state your position and ask for the other's, name the issue, suggest options), with praise, smiles and…"},{"k":"kim2023","apa":"Kim, S., Kang, V., Kim, H., Wang, J., & Gregori, E. (2023). Online literacy instruction for young Korean dual language learners in general education. Journal of Behavioral Education, 32(4), 743–767.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-022-09470-9","y":2023,"sum":"Eight 4- to 5-year-old dual-language learners received two scripted Read It Again-PreK lessons per 30-min online session from a bilingual tutor who repeated statements in the home language when needed. Tested right after each lesson with the definition and two printed words, they went from 4–28% correct at baseline to 97–99% during teaching and 80–100% at maintenance 1–4 weeks later."},{"k":"kimball2023","apa":"Kimball, R. T., Greer, B. D., Fuhrman, A. M., & Lambert, J. M. (2023). Relapse and its mitigation: Toward behavioral inoculation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 56(2), 282–301.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.971","y":2023,"sum":"Why problem behavior a plan brought down can come back, and how to prepare for it. Resurgence: the replacement skill stops paying off (a missed request, a thinner schedule). Renewal: a new adult, room or school. Reinstatement: problem behavior pays off again by mistake. Spontaneous recovery: after a long weekend or holiday. Staff help: honor the plan’s signals, keep reinforcing the replacement skill, never pay off problem behavior, and flag changes ahead."},{"k":"kirby1970","apa":"Kirby, F. D., & Toler, H. C., Jr. (1970). Modification of preschool isolate behavior: A case study. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(4), 309–314.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-309","y":1970,"sum":"A 5-year-old boy who rarely played with peers at a university nursery school was asked each morning to offer every child a choice of two kinds of candy and hand out the one chosen, and then received praise, a nickel and a candy. His time with other children during the following 60-minute free play was recorded in 10-second intervals (agreement 83 to 95% in baseline) across baseline, the helper job, a reversal, the job again and the job on two days a week;…"},{"k":"kirby1972","apa":"Kirby, F. D., & Shields, F. (1972). Modification of arithmetic response rate and attending behavior in a seventh-grade student. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 79–84.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-79","y":1972,"sum":"A 13-year-old seventh grader of average ability did 20 multiplication problems a day; in treatment he brought his work for immediate marking and praise after units that grew from 2 to 16 problems (later 5 to 20). Correct answers per minute and the percentage of 10-second checks spent attending were measured in an ABAB design over 24 school days, with no mastery criterion."},{"k":"kiyak2023","apa":"Kiyak, U. E., & Toper, O. (2023). Teaching science facts to students with autism spectrum disorders via telehealth. Journal of Behavioral Education, 32(3), 605–615.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-022-09497-y","y":2023,"sum":"Three boys with autism aged 13 and 14 were taught four facts each over video calls with simultaneous prompting (question, immediate full-answer model, student repeats) in 12-trial lessons twice a day, with a no-prompt probe before each lesson. All reached 100% on three consecutive probes in 8 to 9 lessons and kept it at 1, 2 and 4 weeks and with a new adult; classmates who only watched learned 80–100% of the facts."},{"k":"kliebert2011","apa":"Kliebert, M. L., Tiger, J. H., & Toussaint, K. A. (2011). An approach to identifying the conditions under which response interruption will reduce automatically reinforced problem behavior. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 4(1), 17–26.","doi":"10.1007/bf03391771","y":2011,"sum":"Two learners with autism: a 12-year-old boy with additional diagnoses whose skin picking caused open wounds (clinic therapy room) and a 19-year-old man who twirled other people's hair (empty classrooms at his school). Immediate interruption cut skin picking and hair twirling to low or near-zero levels each time it was applied in the treated setting."},{"k":"knapczyk1973","apa":"Knapczyk, D. R., & Livingston, G. (1973). Self-recording and student teacher supervision: Variables within a token economy structure. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 481–486.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-481","y":1973,"sum":"Thirteen seventh to ninth graders in a junior high special education class for students with mild intellectual disability answered 6 to 48 comprehension questions on a daily reading assignment at their own level, wrote their own percent correct in a record book, and earned play money by accuracy band toward weekly learning games and longer-term craft, cooking or field-trip rewards."},{"k":"knapczyk1974","apa":"Knapczyk, D. R., & Livingston, G. (1974). The effects of prompting question-asking upon on-task behavior and reading comprehension. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 115–121.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-115","y":1974,"sum":"Two junior-high students with mild intellectual disability in a special class (an eighth-grade girl and a ninth-grade boy) who asked no questions were told by the teacher and aide, a few times over the first days, to raise a hand and ask whenever a word or direction in their reading work was unclear, and each question was answered promptly."},{"k":"koegel1977a","apa":"Koegel, R. L., & Rincover, A. (1977). Research on the difference between generalization and maintenance in extra-therapy responding. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 1–12.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-1","y":1977,"sum":"Nine autistic boys aged 7 to 12 living in an institution, with no or echolalic speech, were each taught in a small laboratory room one new response, either motor imitation to \"Do this\" or touching a named body part, using hand-over-hand guidance that was delayed and faded, with candy and praise for every correct response."},{"k":"koegel1992","apa":"Koegel, L. K., Koegel, R. L., Hurley, C., & Frea, W. D. (1992). Improving social skills and disruptive behavior in children with autism through self-management. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 25(2), 341–353.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1992.25-341","y":1992,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to tell an answer from no answer, to record their own answers and to take a reward for meeting a goal; their answering rose and their disruptive behavior fell, also where no adult was present."},{"k":"kohlenberg1973a","apa":"Kohlenberg, R. J. (1973). Operant conditioning of human anal sphincter pressure. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 201–208.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-201","y":1973,"sum":"A 13-year-old boy with lifelong continuous soiling and weak sphincter tone after bowel surgery, an inpatient on a hospital pediatric ward, was taught in 15 sessions over 5 days to raise and hold sphincter pressure. A nurse placed a small water-filled balloon at the sphincter, the boy watched a water column that rose with pressure, and nickels dropped into a jar first at 10-second checks above a line and then for progressively longer unbroken holds."},{"k":"kolko1989","apa":"Kolko, D. J. (1989). Inpatient intervention for chronic functional encopresis in psychiatrically disturbed children. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(3), 231–252.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040306","y":1989,"sum":"For an elementary student with a serious emotional disturbance, scheduled toilet visits earned tokens toward a menu of prizes, and accidents were cleaned up without the attention they had drawn before; a team ran it at school and the grandmother at home. Toilet use rose and soiling fell in both places."},{"k":"komaki1977","apa":"Komaki, J., & Barnett, F. T. (1977). A behavioral approach to coaching football: Improving the play execution of the offensive backfield on a youth football team. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 657–664.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-657","y":1977,"sum":"Five 9- and 10-year-old boys who played the offensive backfield positions on a youth football team were coached on three plays, each broken into a five-stage checklist that the coach explained and modeled, with feedback and praise for completed stages right after each scrimmage play and at the practice after each game."},{"k":"kourassanisvelasquez2019","apa":"Kourassanis-Velasquez, J., & Jones, E. A. (2019). Increasing joint attention in children with autism and their peers. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 78–94.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0228-x","y":2019,"sum":"Young children with autism were taught to respond to joint attention bids, then to start them, with behavioral prompting, reinforcement and gradual fading, in play."},{"k":"krantz1989","apa":"Krantz, P. J., Ramsland, S. E., & McClannahan, L. E. (1989). Conversational skills for autistic adolescents: An autistic peer as prompter. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(3), 171–189.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040303","y":1989,"sum":"A child and two adolescents with autism were taught greeting and conversation skills chosen with their parents, with behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback); the skills carried over to unscripted talks with new adults. Adolescents were also taught in small groups with the teaching interaction procedure; carry-over to typical peers was partial, so plan practice with them."},{"k":"krantz1993","apa":"Krantz, P. J., & McClannahan, L. E. (1993). Teaching children with autism to initiate to peers: Effects of a script-fading procedure. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 26(1), 121–132.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1993.26-121","y":1993,"sum":"The student first reads short scripts (written or recorded) to start a conversation; the scripts are faded from the end of each line backwards until the student speaks without them, and the student goes on to say things never scripted."},{"k":"lahey1971","apa":"Lahey, B. B. (1971). Modification of the frequency of descriptive adjectives in the speech of Head Start children through modeling without reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(1), 19–22.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-19","y":1971,"sum":"Ten Head Start children aged 4 to 4.75 years took turns with an adult describing boxes of small toys in a single session; for five of them the adult used a color word with every toy and a number word with every group, and for the other five he used no adjectives."},{"k":"lahey1973b","apa":"Lahey, B. B., McNees, M. P., & Brown, C. C. (1973). Modification of deficits in reading for comprehension. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 475–480.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-475","y":1973,"sum":"Two sixth graders aged 11 whose oral reading was on grade level but whose comprehension tested two years lower read short passages from a sixth-grade reader aloud and answered one to four factual questions after each, with a penny and praise for each correct answer in some phases."},{"k":"lahey1974a","apa":"Lahey, B. B., & Drabman, R. S. (1974). Facilitation of the acquisition and retention of sight-word vocabulary through token reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 307–312.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-307","y":1974,"sum":"Sixteen second graders each learned 30 sight words they had missed on a pretest, 10 per session, in one-to-one drill in which the adult first read each card and the child then read every card on later passes, hearing that it was right or being told the word."},{"k":"lalli1995","apa":"Lalli, J. S., Casey, S., & Kates, K. (1995). Reducing escape behavior and increasing task completion with functional communication training, extinction, and response chaining. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 28(3), 261–268.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1995.28-261","y":1995,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"lamarca2018","apa":"LaMarca, V., & LaMarca, J. (2018). Designing receptive language programs: Pushing the boundaries of research and practice. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 479–495.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0208-1","y":2018,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"lambert2006","apa":"Lambert, M. C., Cartledge, G., Heward, W. L., & Lo, Y. (2006). Effects of response cards on disruptive behavior and academic responding during math lessons by fourth-grade urban students. Journal of Positive Behavior Interventions, 8(2), 88–99.","doi":"10.1177/10983007060080020701","y":2006,"sum":"Instead of calling on one student at a time, every student answers each question on a card; the teacher looks, praises and corrects. Responding and quiz scores rose, and in a later study disruptive behavior fell."},{"k":"lambert2016","apa":"Lambert, J. M., Copeland, B. A., Karp, E. L., Finley, C. I., Houchins-Juarez, N. J., & Ledford, J. R. (2016). Chaining functional basketball sequences (with embedded conditional discriminations) in an adolescent with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 199–210.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0125-0","y":2016,"sum":"A 13-year-old with autism and an attention deficit disorder from a self-contained middle school class was taught basketball in a university gym: nine component skills through discrete trials with least-to-most prompting (mastery at 80% independent on two blocks in a row), then offense, defense and full-court drill sequences through forward chaining with a 5-second constant prompt delay."},{"k":"lambert2017","apa":"Lambert, J. M., Lopano, S. E., Noel, C. R., & Ritchie, M. N. (2017). Teacher-conducted, latency-based functional analysis as basis for individualized levels system in a classroom setting. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 422–426.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0200-1","y":2017,"sum":"One 8-year-old nonverbal second grader with autism in a public-school self-contained class whose screaming, biting and hitting were maintained by attention, escape from tasks and access to items, per a latency-based functional analysis run by his first-year teacher. Within about two days of intensive work (a Saturday and a Monday morning), disruption stopped and compliance and independent requests emerged while the work required grew from 2 to 10 tokens."},{"k":"lancioni1988a","apa":"Lancioni, G. E., Oliva, D., Adorni, A., Guzzini, F., Locatelli, M., & Spinaci, P. (1988). Promoting unsupervised activities with low-functioning blind persons: Evaluation of a computer-aided program. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 3(2), 85–99.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360030202","y":1988,"sum":"Four women aged 17 to 28 with severe intellectual disability who were blind (two also deaf) and lived in a residential institution were taught to carry out familiar household and simple job tasks in different parts of a room on their own."},{"k":"lancioni1988b","apa":"Lancioni, G. E., Smeets, P. M., & Oliva, D. (1988). A computer-aided program to supervise occupational engagement of severely mentally retarded persons. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 3(1), 1–17.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360030102","y":1988,"sum":"Two boys with severe intellectual disability (a 17-year-old who was deaf and saw with one eye, living in an institution, and a 7-year-old) who could follow picture cards but did little on their own learned to work through scheduled activities when a computer showed one drawing at a time each time they stepped on a mat, locked the next picture until sensors showed the task was done, and gave tokens at set points."},{"k":"lancioni1990","apa":"Lancioni, G. E., Oliva, D., & Bartolini, T. (1990). A blind woman who is mentally retarded promoting and sharing the occupational engagement of a dependent deaf-blind adolescent. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(3), 149–157.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050303","y":1990,"sum":"Six young men with autism (16–19 years) at a behavioral school could already follow schedules on their own. They were paired and taught to work from one schedule to clean or restock rooms at school. A teacher guided each partner through checking the schedule, crossing off a step, doing it and returning to the schedule, fading from hand-over-hand help to shadowing and then to standing at a distance, and she dropped small edibles into each learner's cup…"},{"k":"lancioni1991","apa":"Lancioni, G. E., Bouter, H. P., & Brouwer, J. A. (1991). Groups of individuals who are moderately mentally retarded sharing their activities with passive low-functioning companions. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(5), 341–354.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060504","y":1991,"sum":"Six adults (29 to 42) with moderate intellectual disability in the day center of a Dutch residential institute were taught, in two small groups, a 15-step 'assistant' job: planning on a picture board, then, cued by paired timer alarms, leading 20-minute activities with two inactive men with profound intellectual disability; they practiced first with a staff stand-in and prompts, then with correction only after errors, in a nonconcurrent multiple baseline."},{"k":"lancioni1993","apa":"Lancioni, G. E., Bellini, D., & Oliva, D. (1993). Brief report: Building choice opportunities within a robot-assisted occupational program: A case study. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(3), 219–226.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080305","y":1993,"sum":"A 36-year-old man who was totally blind and deaf, with intellectual disability, living in an institution, already worked in a program in which he took an object standing for a task from a supply window, matched its tag to a lid on a guide robot's keyboard and held the robot's handle as it led him to the work station."},{"k":"lancioni1994","apa":"Lancioni, G. E., Oliva, D., & Bracalente, S. (1994). An electronic guidance system for multihandicapped blind persons: Evaluating its effectiveness and likableness. Behavioral Interventions, 9(2), 93–103.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360090204","y":1994,"sum":"Two men aged 25 and 19 who were totally blind (the younger also deaf), each with paralysis on one side and intellectual disability, at a day activity center, learned to use a chest-worn device that stayed silent while they walked the right way to a chosen work station and vibrated when they strayed."},{"k":"lattal1969","apa":"Lattal, K. A. (1969). Contingency management of toothbrushing behavior in a summer camp for children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(3), 195–198.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-195","y":1969,"sum":"Eight boys aged 10 to 12 at a therapeutic summer camp, each with some behavior problem and gaps in self-care, were observed during the rest hour after lunch: first with no comment, then with a daily reminder to brush, then for 22 days with permission to go swimming given only after brushing, then with swimming removed or given regardless."},{"k":"lattimore2008","apa":"Lattimore, L. P., Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H. (2008). Simulation training of community job skills for adults with autism: A further analysis. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(1), 24–29.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391717","y":2008,"sum":"Adults with autism and profound intellectual disability learned job tasks (preparing folders and notebooks, assembling mailing boxes) in a classroom set up like the job, with a task analysis and prompting, before doing them at work; their performance at the job site rose right after, with the job’s own materials or with different ones, and an intensive version taught a task in one day."},{"k":"lattimore2009","apa":"Lattimore, L. P., Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H. (2009). Rapid training of a community job skill to nonvocal adults with autism: An extension of intensive teaching. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 2(1), 34–42.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391735","y":2009,"sum":"Adults with autism and profound intellectual disability learned job tasks (preparing folders and notebooks, assembling mailing boxes) in a classroom set up like the job, with a task analysis and prompting, before doing them at work; their performance at the job site rose right after, with the job’s own materials or with different ones, and an intensive version taught a task in one day."},{"k":"lavigna2022","apa":"LaVigna, G. W., Hughes, E. C., Potter, G., Spicer, M., Hume, L., Willis, T. J., & Huerta, E. (2022). Needed independent and dependent variables in multi-element behavior support plans addressing severe behavior problems. Perspectives on Behavior Science, 45(2), 421–444.","doi":"10.1007/s40614-022-00331-4","y":2022,"sum":"Three 4-year-old boys in a preschool intensive therapeutic classroom (one with autism, one with oppositional defiant disorder, one with prenatal drug exposure and no diagnosis) with daily aggression, property destruction, screaming, hiding and elopement maintained by tangibles, attention or escape."},{"k":"leaf2016a","apa":"Leaf, J. B., Cihon, J. H., Townley-Cochran, D., Miller, K., Leaf, R., McEachin, J., & Taubman, M. (2016). An evaluation of positional prompts for teaching receptive identification to individuals diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(4), 349–363.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0146-8","y":2016,"sum":"Six children with autism aged 4 to 9 at a private clinic were taught to touch named pictures of cartoon characters, sports teams or athletes in sets of three. The only prompt was moving the correct picture nearer the child (at the table edge, then 6 in. closer, then level with the others), stepping to less help after two correct answers and to more help after an error, with praise (and tokens for two children)."},{"k":"leaf2016b","apa":"Leaf, J. A., Leaf, J. B., Milne, C., Townley-Cochran, D., Oppenheim-Leaf, M. L., Cihon, J. H., Taubman, M., McEachin, J., & Leaf, R. (2016). The effects of the cool versus not cool procedure to teach social game play to individuals diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(1), 34–49.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0112-5","y":2016,"sum":"Eight children with autism aged 3 to 5, with IQs above 80, were taught three group games in a clinic social skills group: teachers acted each game out the right and the wrong way, the children judged each model and said why, and everyone role-played the game with feedback and a level chart. Probes in which only the rules were stated scored whether each child did every step correctly on every turn, with mastery at three consecutive correct probes."},{"k":"leaf2018","apa":"Leaf, J. B., Cihon, J. H., Ferguson, J. L., McEachin, J., Leaf, R., & Taubman, M. (2018). Evaluating three methods of stimulus rotation when teaching receptive labels. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 334–349.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0249-5","y":2018,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"leblanc2005","apa":"LeBlanc, L. A., Carr, J. E., Crossett, S. E., Bennett, C. M., & Detweiler, D. D. (2005). Intensive outpatient behavioral treatment of primary urinary incontinence of children with autism. Focus on Autism and Other Developmental Disabilities, 20(2), 98–105.","doi":"","y":2005,"sum":"Scheduled visits (sits of a few minutes on a timer), extra fluids, immediate reinforcement for each void in the toilet and a brief, neutral response to accidents; the time between visits grows as the student stays dry, and then the student is taught to ask. School staff ran this in public school programs."},{"k":"ledbettercho2016","apa":"Ledbetter-Cho, K., Lang, R., Davenport, K., Moore, M., Lee, A., O’Reilly, M., Watkins, L., & Falcomata, T. (2016). Behavioral skills training to improve the abduction-prevention skills of children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 266–270.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0128-x","y":2016,"sum":"Four boys with autism aged 9 to 12 who spoke in sentences were taught at a university clinic, using a rationale, rehearsal of the three steps, a 30-second video model and practice with feedback, to refuse, move away and tell a familiar adult when a stranger used a simple, authority, help-seeking or incentive lure. Unannounced probes on campus were scored 0 to 3 (one point per step), with mastery at a first-try score of 3 for every lure type."},{"k":"ledford2019","apa":"Ledford, J. R., Barton, E. E., Rigor, M. N., Stankiewicz, K. C., Chazin, K. T., Harbin, E. R., & Taylor, A. L. (2019). Functional analysis and treatment of pica on a preschool playground. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 176–181.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00283-9","y":2019,"sum":"One 4-year-old boy with Down syndrome in an inclusive childcare program who put inedible playground items in his mouth (pica), highest in the attention condition of a functional analysis run on the playground. In the FA pica was highest with attention. Three lower-effort attention plans (two momentary DRO variants at 1-min intervals and 1-min play prompts with an obstacle course) did not lower pica."},{"k":"lee1978","apa":"Lee, V. L. (1978). Teaching generalized receptive and productive behind-front discriminations to two retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(4), 529.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-529","y":1978,"sum":"Three children with autism, a 7-year-old girl with echolalic speech and two 5-year-old boys who had recently learned to name objects through imitation training, sat across from an adult who put a small felt circle in or on an object and asked where it was. Each session had 20 trials (10 for each word) in random order; a correct answer earned a bit of food with praise, and after an error the adult paused briefly and then said the right answer."},{"k":"leif2026","apa":"Leif, E., Roscoe, E., Rae, L., & Sheets, S. (2026). Component analysis of a self-monitoring intervention for increasing task engagement for individuals with developmental disabilities. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 59(2), e70053.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70053","y":2026,"sum":"The student is taught what working looks like, then to mark the card at each cue; at first an adult marks too and the two compare, and agreement earns a small reward. The cues are spaced further apart as on-task behavior holds. Self-monitoring is among the best-supported classroom interventions for students with behavior problems."},{"k":"lelaurin1972","apa":"LeLaurin, K., & Risley, T. R. (1972). The organization of day-care environments: \"Zone\" versus \"man-to-man\" staff assignments. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 225–232.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-225","y":1972,"sum":"About 39 children aged 3 to 5 each day (36 to 43) in a large community day-care room with four staff, during the daily move from lunch through hand washing and shoe removal to nap. With zone staffing a child spent about 10 minutes of the lunch-to-nap transition (range about 8 to 14) out of contact with planned activities, compared with about 21 minutes (range 17 to 33) when each adult walked a group of 6 to 12 children through every step and the group…"},{"k":"lewis2025","apa":"Lewis, T. K., & Cariveau, T. (2025). Remediation of the picture-text problem for learners exhibiting reading deficits. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 58(2), 329–343.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70001","y":2025,"sum":"Two 10-year-olds with intellectual delays in a self-contained class practiced unknown words on a computer: each word appeared, a recording then read it, and the student tried to say it first and repeated it after, three times per word each session. Words read within 2 s on two tests in a row were counted as learned and swapped for new ones, and both students learned far more practiced than unpracticed words."},{"k":"li2019","apa":"Li, A., Curiel, H., Ragotzy, S. P., & Poling, A. (2019). Using a lottery to promote physical activity by young adults with developmental disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(3), 612–616.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00292-8","y":2019,"sum":"Four young adults aged 21–25 with autism or intellectual disability in a transition program learned to read their step counter, received a goal each morning, and entered a daily prize drawing when the count met the goal. Three of the four walked more (for example, from about 5,900 to 8,500 steps a day); the one who rarely won the drawing did not change."},{"k":"libby2008","apa":"Libby, M. E., Weiss, J. S., Bancroft, S., & Ahearn, W. H. (2008). A comparison of most-to-least and least-to-most prompting on the acquisition of solitary play skills. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(1), 37–43.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391719","y":2008,"sum":"Children with autism learned to build Lego structures as chains of steps. Full physical guidance faded to lighter guidance (most-to-least) made fewer errors than least-to-most prompting but was sometimes slower; most-to-least with a short delay before each prompt was nearly as fast as least-to-most, with fewer errors."},{"k":"liberman1975","apa":"Liberman, R. P., Ferris, C., Salgado, P., & Salgado, J. (1975). Replication of the Achievement Place model in California. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(3), 287–299.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-287","y":1975,"sum":"In a family-style group home for boys aged 12 to 16 placed by a juvenile court, run by nonprofessional house parents on a point system traded for privileges, five experiments repeated Achievement Place procedures. For table setting, a bonus of 100 or 1,000 points, and then a trophy kept until the next perfect table, for meeting all eight items of a demonstrated checklist raised the share of days with a perfect table; it fell when rewards stopped and…"},{"k":"lomasmevers2017","apa":"Lomas Mevers, J. E., Call, N. A., & Miller, S. J. (2017). Practice recommendations for addressing problem behaviors in siblings with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 363–374.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0190-z","y":2017,"sum":"Identical twin 9-year-old boys with autism in a day-treatment program for severe behavior, both eloping to reach preferred items or activities (one also for attention), whose mother was often alone with both of them. FCT alone replaced elopement with requests for each boy, and the combined three-color schedule kept elopement low when one adult treated both boys together and later when their mother ran it at home."},{"k":"long1973","apa":"Long, J. D., & Williams, R. L. (1973). The comparative effectiveness of group and individually contingent free time with inner-city junior high school students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 465–474.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-465","y":1973,"sum":"The eight most disruptive students in a low-achieving class of 32 seventh graders at an inner-city junior high had structured lessons and posted rules, then end-of-class free time from which the whole class lost a minute for each rule violation, then an individual point sheet (points for being present and ready, for working quietly at unpredictable timer checks and for finishing the assignment) on which 12 of 16 points earned the free time, and finally…"},{"k":"lovitt1968","apa":"Lovitt, T. C., & Curtiss, K. A. (1968). Effects of manipulating an antecedent event on mathematics response rate. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(4), 329–333.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-329","y":1968,"sum":"An 11-year-old boy in a university class for students with behavior disorders completed a daily timed page of one type of subtraction problem in a small room, first just writing the answers, then saying each problem aloud before writing its answer, then writing only again, repeated with three problem types of rising difficulty. Correct answers and errors per minute were computed from each page; consequences did not change and no mastery criterion was set."},{"k":"lovitt1969","apa":"Lovitt, T. C., & Curtiss, K. A. (1969). Academic response rate as a function of teacher- and self-imposed contingencies. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 49–53.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-49","y":1969,"sum":"A 12-year-old in a university class for students with behavior disorders, who earned points for academic work that bought minutes in a free-time room, worked under points-per-work rates set and explained by the teacher or set by himself and written down, in two ABA replications; in a third experiment the teacher imposed the more generous rates he had chosen."},{"k":"lovitt1970","apa":"Lovitt, T. C., & Esveldt, K. A. (1970). The relative effects on math performance of single- versus multiple-ratio schedules: A case study. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(4), 261–270.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-261","y":1970,"sum":"A 12-year-old boy in a class for students with behavior disorders at a university laboratory school earned points for free time in a high-interest room for correct math answers, either at one flat rate (one point per 20 correct) or from tiered bands set from his own median rate, with nothing below that rate and richer payment at each higher band."},{"k":"lowe1976","apa":"Lowe, M. L., & Cuvo, A. J. (1976). Teaching coin summation to the mentally retarded. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(4), 483–489.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-483","y":1976,"sum":"Four learners aged about 10 to 18 (mean 14.8, mean IQ 57) with mild or moderate intellectual disability, from a children's center and a community workshop, who could count to 100 by ones and by fives and name coin values, were taught in 30-minute sessions four times a week to count coin sets by touching one finger beside a silver coin per five cents while counting by fives, adding pennies by ones."},{"k":"luczynski2013","apa":"Luczynski, K. C., & Hanley, G. P. (2013). Prevention of problem behavior by teaching functional communication and self-control skills to preschoolers. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 46(2), 355–368.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.44","y":2013,"sum":"Children whose problem behavior was maintained by escape from difficult tasks were taught to ask for assistance, and real help followed each request; problem behavior fell as the requests rose, and the effect carried to new teachers and settings. Teach on hard items mixed with easy ones, prompt the request before problem behavior, and fade the prompt."},{"k":"lugo2019","apa":"Lugo, A. M., McArdle, P. E., King, M. L., Lamphere, J. C., Peck, J. A., & Beck, H. J. (2019). Effects of presession pairing on preference for therapeutic conditions and challenging behavior. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 188–193.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0268-2","y":2019,"sum":"One 4-year-old girl with autism in an early-intervention clinic who screeched and refused during discrete-trial instruction; the study measured which pre-session condition she chose and her negative vocalizations. In a concurrent-chains preference test the student chose the pairing condition on almost every trial once choices differentiated, and kept choosing it across three therapist reversals, over both free play with the same toys and immediate table…"},{"k":"luiselli1987","apa":"Luiselli, J. K., & Evans, T. P. (1987). Assessing pharmacological and contingency management interventions with mentally retarded adolescents in a residential treatment program. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 2(3), 139–152.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360020303","y":1987,"sum":"One 18-year-old man with autism and severe intellectual disability in a private residential school whose very frequent front-to-back and side-to-side body rocking during seated prevocational assembly work (about 65 to 70% of intervals) cut his output and attention; he was also on a neuroleptic medication that was being reviewed."},{"k":"luiselli1991","apa":"Luiselli, J. K. (1991). Functional assessment and treatment of self-injury in a pediatric, nursing-care resident. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(5), 311–319.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060502","y":1991,"sum":"An 18-year-old nonspeaking young woman with Down syndrome, spastic quadriplegia, seizures and visual impairment in a pediatric nursing-care facility classroom who pressed her fingers into her eyes at high rates. Eye pressing fell from about 33 an hour without coverings to 15 with mittens and kept dropping as the gloves were thinned (about 2–6 an hour at the cut-down steps)."},{"k":"luiselli1994","apa":"Luiselli, J. K. (1994). Toilet training children with sensory impairments in a residential school setting. Behavioral Interventions, 9(2), 105–114.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360090205","y":1994,"sum":"Two 6-year-old boys at a residential school, one deaf with developmental delay and one deaf-blind at about a 2-year developmental level, were taken to the toilet on a schedule (every 60 or 90 minutes) by classroom and residence staff, sat up to 3 minutes, and received candy and praise (plus 1–2 minutes with a favorite vibrating toy for the deaf-blind boy) for urinating in the toilet; accidents were met with a change of clothes and no comment, and one boy…"},{"k":"lutzker1974","apa":"Lutzker, J. R., & Sherman, J. A. (1974). Producing generative sentence usage by imitation and reinforcement procedures. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 447–460.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-447","y":1974,"sum":"Three residents of a state institution with moderate intellectual disability (aged 6, 12 and 32) and two typically developing children aged 2 years 5 months were first taught the noun and action names in a set of pictures, then taught to answer What's happening?"},{"k":"macdonald1970","apa":"MacDonald, W. S., Gallimore, R., & MacDonald, G. (1970). Contingency counseling by school personnel: An economical model of intervention. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(3), 175–182.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-175","y":1970,"sum":"High school students in Hawaii who rarely attended (six ninth graders in a special motivation class, then 35 tenth and twelfth graders) were helped by school staff with brief training, who arranged agreements with parents or other adults in each student's life to give a valued privilege or item only for days the student attended; in the second study a comparison counselor relied on talks and rapport instead."},{"k":"macduff1993","apa":"MacDuff, G. S., Krantz, P. J., & McClannahan, L. E. (1993). Teaching children with autism to use photographic activity schedules: Maintenance and generalization of complex response chains. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 26(1), 89–97.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1993.26-89","y":1993,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to follow photo schedules with manual guidance from behind, faded gradually (graduated guidance), so that no adult prompt became part of the chain; they kept following them when the activities were reordered and new pictures were added."},{"k":"mace1988","apa":"Mace, F. C., Hock, M. L., Lalli, J. S., West, B. J., Belfiore, P., Pinter, E., & Brown, D. K. (1988). Behavioral momentum in the treatment of noncompliance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 21(2), 123–141.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1988.21-123","y":1988,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"mace2011","apa":"Mace, F. C., Pratt, J. L., Prager, K. L., & Pritchard, D. (2011). An evaluation of three methods of saying \"no\" to avoid an escalating response class hierarchy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(1), 83–94.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2011.44-83","y":2011,"sum":"After the request is reliable, some requests are answered with \"not now\" and an alternative is offered; a calm \"okay\" is praised and often earns the item a little later. The denials are rare at first and grow more frequent. A second request, for an alternative, can be taught too, with signals for which one will be answered."},{"k":"madsen1968","apa":"Madsen, C. H., Jr., Becker, W. C., & Thomas, D. R. (1968). Rules, praise, and ignoring: Elements of elementary classroom control. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(2), 139–150.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-139","y":1968,"sum":"Two second graders and a kindergartner in public-school classes, chosen for frequent disruptive behavior (out of seat, noise, talking out and some hitting), were observed 20 minutes a day, three days a week, while their teachers added in turn posted rules reviewed several times a day, ignoring of minor misbehavior, and praise for appropriate behavior, with a return to baseline in one class."},{"k":"madsen1974","apa":"Madsen, C. H., Jr., Madsen, C. K., & Thompson, F. (1974). Increasing rural Head Start children's consumption of middle-class meals. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 257–262.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-257","y":1974,"sum":"Forty-six Black children (mean age about 6) from low-income rural families in a summer Head Start program were served a school breakfast made of foods they did not eat at home. In an ABAB design, teachers praised children for tasting, eating and drinking and handed them pieces of sweetened cereal, and gave five small candies with praise to children who finished the tray; trays were weighed before and after eating, and the number of children eating was…"},{"k":"maeser1990","apa":"Maeser, N. C., & Thyer, B. A. (1990). Teaching boys with severe mental retardation to serve themselves during family-style meals. Behavioural Residential Treatment, 5(4), 239–246.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050403","y":1990,"sum":"Three boys aged 13, 13 and 16 with severe intellectual disability and seizure disorders, living in an intermediate care facility, who could not serve themselves from shared serving bowls at meals. In baseline the boys completed almost no steps in order; with 41 to 50 practice trials each, all three learned to complete all nine steps at regular breakfasts."},{"k":"magrab1977","apa":"Magrab, P. R., & Papadopoulou, Z. L. (1977). The effect of a token economy on dietary compliance for children on hemodialysis. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 573–578.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-573","y":1977,"sum":"Four children aged 11 to 18 on a pediatric hemodialysis unit were taught their diets by the dietitian and then earned points at each dialysis session for keeping weight gain between sessions within 2 pounds, potassium within limit and blood urea nitrogen within limit, with points posted on a chart and exchanged weekly for chosen non-food prizes or money."},{"k":"maki2024","apa":"Maki, K. E., Moody, M. E., Cullins, S. L., & Griffin, T. L. (2024). Examination of a modified incremental rehearsal approach to explore causal mechanisms. Journal of Behavioral Education, 33(1), 184–207.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-022-09482-5","y":2024,"sum":"A first-grade general education class of 20 children with no identified disabilities practiced three sets of 15 addition facts. After each math lesson, a tape read every problem and then its answer. The pause before the answer was 0, 1 or 2 seconds, and students tried to write the answer before the tape gave it, then wrote or corrected it. A short practice sheet on their own followed each session."},{"k":"maloney1973","apa":"Maloney, K. B., & Hopkins, B. L. (1973). The modification of sentence structure and its relationship to subjective judgements of creativity in writing. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 425–433.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-425","y":1973,"sum":"Fourteen fourth to sixth graders in a voluntary summer school wrote a 10-sentence story each day on a topic word and, in a two-team writing game, earned points first for each different describing word, then for each different action word, then for these plus different sentence starters, with early recess and a small piece of chocolate for team members who reached a set score."},{"k":"maloney1976","apa":"Maloney, D. M., Harper, T. M., Braukmann, C. J., Fixsen, D. L., Phillips, E. L., & Wolf, M. M. (1976). Teaching conversation-related skills to predelinquent girls. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 371.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-371","y":1976,"sum":"Four girls aged 13 to 15 living in a community group home for court-involved youth were taught, in daily 10-minute talks with an adult playing a visiting guest, to give full answers to the guest's 10 main questions, with points in the home's token system for taking part and for meeting set goals; some sessions were led by a houseparent and some by the girls themselves."},{"k":"mann1971","apa":"Mann, R. A., & Baer, D. M. (1971). The effects of receptive language training on articulation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(4), 291–298.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-291","y":1971,"sum":"Four typically developing preschoolers aged 4 to 4.75 learned over eight 20-minute sessions to point to four unfamiliar objects when they heard each one's made-up multisyllable name, earning praise and chips toward a toy, with a chip removed after an error."},{"k":"mansdorf1986","apa":"Mansdorf, I. J., & Burstein, Y. (1986). Case manager: A clinical tool for training residential treatment staff. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 1(3), 155–168.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360010302","y":1986,"sum":"In the first experiment, a 31-year-old man with profound intellectual disability in a large state residential facility, who followed about 3% of simple instructions such as “come here” and “stand up,” was taught by his assigned direct-care staff member using shaping, faded physical prompting and praise."},{"k":"marchandmartella1990","apa":"Marchand-Martella, N. E., & Martella, R. C. (1990). The acquisition, maintenance, and generalization of first-aid skills by youths with handicaps. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(4), 221–237.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050402","y":1990,"sum":"Four boys aged 7–11 living in a behavioral group home (diagnoses included bipolar disorder, attention problems, mild intellectual disability with emotional disturbance, and autistic-like behavior) learned first aid with a picture storybook that showed each step, a felt puppet marked with a ketchup cut or a red-marker burn, and a stocked first-aid kit."},{"k":"marholin1977","apa":"Marholin, D., II, & Steinman, W. M. (1977). Stimulus control in the classroom as a function of the behavior reinforced. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 465–478.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-465","y":1977,"sum":"Eight children aged 10 to 12 in a special class for academic and behavior problems did 30 minutes of math seatwork under no reinforcement, marks and praise for being on task (on individual variable-interval schedules), or marks for meeting an individual criterion of problems attempted and correct, with marks exchanged for a 30-minute free-time period (children who missed it did extra work in an adjacent room)."},{"k":"marlowe1978","apa":"Marlowe, R. H., Madsen, C. H., Jr., Bowen, C. E., Reardon, R. C., & Logue, P. E. (1978). Severe classroom behavior problems: Teachers or counsellors. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(1), 53–66.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-53","y":1978,"sum":"Six students in grades 1 and 3 in a low-income urban neighborhood, picked for disrupting class or dawdling, had been getting most of their teacher's attention while they were off task. Their teachers saw the baseline graph and then went to the student with brief praise or a pat on the shoulder after about a minute of steady work (at first when an observer held up a card) and stopped responding to off-task behavior; for a girl who did no work at all,…"},{"k":"martin1975","apa":"Martin, J. A. (1975). Generalizing the use of descriptive adjectives through modelling. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 203–209.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-203","y":1975,"sum":"Two children with intellectual disability, a boy of 8 at a school on a state-institution campus and a girl of 6 in a hospital rehabilitation unit, who knew color and size words receptively but seldom said them, repeated 12 short sentences a day about animals modeled by a teacher or nurse, with praise for correct repetition and correction until all 12 were said without error."},{"k":"mason2014","apa":"Mason, L. L., & Andrews, A. (2014). Referent-based verbal behavior instruction for children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 107–111.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0018-z","y":2014,"sum":"Thirteen children with autism aged 2 to 5 received referent-based instruction from graduate students at a university center, 90 minutes a day, 4 days a week, for 13 weeks, in play-based 10-minute blocks. Around an item the child chose, the same word was taught as a request, an echo, a label and a fill-in reply, using quick runs of easy prompts, errorless teaching and thinning to praise, and each block ended with a 1-minute probe of shuffled cue cards."},{"k":"may2019","apa":"May, M. E. (2019). Effects of differential consequences on choice making in students at risk for academic failure. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 154–161.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0267-3","y":2019,"sum":"Students with emotional and behavioral challenges were offered a choice among tasks of the same difficulty; with the choice, their engagement rose and their disruptive behavior fell compared with tasks assigned to them."},{"k":"mcallister1969","apa":"McAllister, L. W., Stachowiak, J. G., Baer, D. M., & Conderman, L. (1969). The application of operant conditioning techniques in a secondary school classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(4), 277–285.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-277","y":1969,"sum":"A lower-track junior–senior English class of 25 students aged 16 to 19 in a public high school (a similar class taught by the same teacher served as a comparison), with talking without permission and turning around in seats as the targets. Talking out fell from about 25% of intervals at baseline to an average of about 5% once praise and corrections began, while the comparison class stayed near 22%."},{"k":"mccarty1977","apa":"McCarty, T., Griffin, S., Apolloni, T., & Shores, R. E. (1977). Increased peer-teaching with group-oriented contingencies for arithmetic performance in behavior-disordered adolescents. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 313.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-313","y":1977,"sum":"Abstract only (the full report was deposited with an auxiliary publication service). Four adolescents with behavior disorders living in a psychiatric hospital worked multiplication problems under baseline, a group arrangement paying 5 cents for every correct problem, and a mixed arrangement in which no one could earn until each had worked at least three problems (an ABAC reversal design)."},{"k":"mckay2014","apa":"McKay, J. A., Weiss, J. S., Dickson, C. A., & Ahearn, W. H. (2014). Comparison of prompting hierarchies on the acquisition of leisure and vocational skills. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 91–102.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0022-3","y":2014,"sum":"Three adolescent girls (14 and 15) with intellectual disability and a 37-year-old man with autism, at a residential school and its adult program, were taught 12-step Lego structures and bolt, nut and washer assemblies by forward chaining, with an edible and praise for the step being taught."},{"k":"mckeel2015","apa":"McKeel, A. N., Rowsey, K. E., Belisle, J., Dixon, M. R., & Szekely, S. (2015). Teaching complex verbal operants with the PEAK relational training system. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 8(2), 241–244.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0067-y","y":2015,"sum":"Three students with autism aged 9 to 11 at a special school were taught with PEAK direct-training programs in 10-trial discrete-trial blocks, with least-to-most prompts that were stepped down after each success and reinforcers the student chose."},{"k":"mckenzie1974","apa":"McKenzie, T. L., & Rushall, B. S. (1974). Effects of self-recording on attendance and performance in a competitive swimming training environment. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 199–206.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-199","y":1974,"sum":"On a club swim team of 32 children aged 9 to 16, swimmers recorded their own attendance on a public board under rules added in steps (attend and swim, arrive on time, stay to the end), and in a second experiment checked off each work unit on a board at each lane that listed the session's workout."},{"k":"mclaughlin1972a","apa":"McLaughlin, T. F., & Malaby, J. (1972a). Intrinsic reinforcers in a classroom token economy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 263–270.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-263","y":1972,"sum":"A combined fifth- and sixth-grade public school class of 25 to 29 students in a low-income area, taught by the first author, earned points for completed assignments in spelling, language, handwriting and math, kept their own point sheets and traded points for classroom privileges they had chosen and ranked, while heavy point fines applied to incomplete work and to fighting or cheating."},{"k":"mclaughlin1972b","apa":"McLaughlin, T., & Malaby, J. (1972b). Reducing and measuring inappropriate verbalizations in a token classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 329–333.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-329","y":1972,"sum":"A combined fifth- and sixth-grade public school class of 25 to 27 students in a low-income area, already using a classroom point system, targeting calling out, talking without permission, noises and calling the teacher's name. Under 15-point fines the class averaged about 46 inappropriate verbalizations a day; with quiet points the mean fell to about 22 and the count reached 8 a day by the third week."},{"k":"mcreynolds1969","apa":"McReynolds, L. V. (1969). Application of timeout from positive reinforcement for increasing the efficiency of speech training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(3), 199–205.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-199","y":1969,"sum":"A 5-year-old boy with a brain injury who had no speech was taught to imitate sounds and then words built backward one sound at a time, with praise and a bite of ice cream for correct imitations; when babbling between trials or slipping back to a shorter form held up progress, the trainer turned away with the ice cream until he was quiet for about 30 seconds or said the full word."},{"k":"mcwilliams2025","apa":"McWilliams, M. S., & Hanson, R. J. (2025). The use of partial textual stimuli within an interactive task for increasing reports of past behavior with a child with autism. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 41(1), 57–67.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-025-00218-w","y":2025,"sum":"A 4-year-old girl with autism who could read short sentences was asked three scripted questions about each of three daily activities, with the first words of each answer printed on stacking blocks she stacked as she answered. Prompts to take her turn or echo the block words, praise for each correct answer and 5 min with preferred items for a fully correct set raised correct reports from about 0–1 of 3 to about 2.5–3 of 3, and her answers varied more…"},{"k":"medland1972","apa":"Medland, M. B., & Stachnik, T. J. (1972). Good-behavior game: A replication and systematic analysis. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 45–51.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-45","y":1972,"sum":"Two reading teams of 14 fifth graders in a public school played the Good Behavior Game (daily rules, a red and green light run by trained high school helpers, a team mark for each talk-out, out-of-seat or disruption, and extra recess or activity time for teams under a mark limit), followed by a return to baseline and phases of rules alone and rules plus lights."},{"k":"milby1970","apa":"Milby, J. B., Jr. (1970). Modification of extreme social isolation by contingent social reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 149–152.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-149","y":1970,"sum":"Two men aged 37 and 38 with chronic schizophrenia on an open ward of a veterans' hospital received staff approval (coming near, nodding and approving talk) whenever they were talking, working or playing with another patient or a staff member, and later also for being in the room where most social activity took place. Interaction was recorded in twelve 2-minute samples a day (agreement 88 to 100%) in an AB design, with no mastery criterion or follow-up."},{"k":"miller1976b","apa":"Miller, S. J., & Sloane, H. N., Jr. (1976). The generalization effects of parent training across stimulus settings. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 355–370.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-355","y":1976,"sum":"Five mothers of children aged 6 to 12 without functional speech, from a public school developmental unit, were coached at home (instructions, brief modelling, feedback after each session and a wrist counter) to prompt and answer their child's vocalizations during a 10-minute snack time and to run 10 daily imitation trials of one speech sound with praise and a small candy for each correct try."},{"k":"miller1977","apa":"Miller, M. A., Cuvo, A. J., & Borakove, L. S. (1977). Teaching naming of coin values—comprehension before production versus production alone. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 735–736.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-735","y":1977,"sum":"Fourteen students with intellectual disability (mean age about 13, mean mental age about 5, arithmetic at kindergarten level) were taught coin values either by pointing to the coin whose value was spoken and then saying values, or by saying the value of the coin pointed to only."},{"k":"miller2025","apa":"Miller, K., Lewis, T. K., Cariveau, T., & Brown, A. (2025). Comparison of matching the compound or elements as a differential problem-solving response. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 58(3), 522–534.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70011","y":2025,"sum":"Two 10-year-olds with intellectual delays in a self-contained class practiced unknown words on a computer: each word appeared, a recording then read it, and the student tried to say it first and repeated it after, three times per word each session. Words read within 2 s on two tests in a row were counted as learned and swapped for new ones, and both students learned far more practiced than unpracticed words."},{"k":"miltenberger2004","apa":"Miltenberger, R. G., Flessner, C., Gatheridge, B., Johnson, B., Satterlund, M., & Egemo, K. (2004). Evaluation of behavioral skills training to prevent gun play in children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 37(4), 513–516.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2004.37-513","y":2004,"sum":"Behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback) teaches the steps; many children knew them and still did not use them when a gun was found, so the skills are checked in place (in situ) and, when missed, trained right there. Generalization is planned: several settings, several people, practice that continues."},{"k":"miltenberger2008","apa":"Miltenberger, R. G. (2008). Teaching safety skills to children: Prevention of firearm injury as an exemplar of best practice in assessment, training, and generalization of safety skills. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(1), 30–36.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391718","y":2008,"sum":"Behavioral skills training (instructions, modeling, rehearsal, feedback) teaches the steps; many children knew them and still did not use them when a gun was found, so the skills are checked in place (in situ) and, when missed, trained right there. Generalization is planned: several settings, several people, practice that continues."},{"k":"minkin1976","apa":"Minkin, N., Braukmann, C. J., Minkin, B. L., Timbers, G. D., Timbers, B. J., Fixsen, D. L., Phillips, E. L., & Wolf, M. M. (1976). The social validation and training of conversational skills. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 127–139.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-127","y":1976,"sum":"Community adults rated videotaped 4-minute conversations of junior high and university females with unfamiliar adults, and the ratings were compared with counts of conversational questions, brief positive responses and time spent talking."},{"k":"mistr1992","apa":"Mistr, K. N., & Glenn, S. S. (1992). Evocative and function-altering effects of contingency-specifiying stimuli. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 10, 11–21.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392871","y":1992,"sum":"Nine typically developing 4-year-olds at a university preschool, chosen because they picked up toys on no more than one of four plain requests, were each given one spoken rule a day to pick up toys, naming a deadline (now, or before outside playtime) and whether a trip to a box of small prizes would follow on finishing."},{"k":"mithaug1976","apa":"Mithaug, D. E., & Wolfe, M. S. (1976). Employing task arrangements and verbal contingencies to promote verbalizations between retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 301–314.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-301","y":1976,"sum":"Four boys aged 10 to 12 with mild or moderate intellectual disability and major language delays worked in pairs, and later as a group of four, on 10-piece wooden puzzles in 20-minute daily sessions, earning a small candy for every second piece placed. Conditions alternated between each boy holding his own pieces and his pieces being held by his partner, with or without a rule that a piece taken without a spoken request was put back."},{"k":"mithaug1978","apa":"Mithaug, D. E., & Hanawalt, D. A. (1978). The validation of procedures to assess prevocational task preferences in retarded adults. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(1), 153–162.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-153","y":1978,"sum":"Students with emotional and behavioral challenges were offered a choice among tasks of the same difficulty; with the choice, their engagement rose and their disruptive behavior fell compared with tasks assigned to them."},{"k":"montgomery1993","apa":"Montgomery, R. W. (1993). The collateral effect of compliance training on aggression. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(1), 9–20.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080103","y":1993,"sum":"A 28-year-old man with autism and severe intellectual disability who lived in a large state facility and had a long history of serious aggression was given 15 requests a session (five to do something, five to stop or not do something, and five to wait, meaning stop, put his hands on the table and stay seated for 15 s) about every 45 s while he worked on tasks he liked."},{"k":"mullins2001","apa":"Mullins, J. L., & Christian, L. (2001). The effects of progressive relaxation training on the disruptive behavior of a boy with autism. Research in Developmental Disabilities, 22(6), 449–462.","doi":"10.1016/S0891-4222(01)00083-X","y":2001,"sum":"Progressive relaxation was taught step by step with modeling and practice until the boy could relax on cue; his disruptive behavior fell."},{"k":"murdock1977","apa":"Murdock, J. Y., Garcia, E. E., & Hardman, M. L. (1977). Generalizing articulation training with trainable mentally retarded subjects. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 717–733.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-717","y":1977,"sum":"Two girls aged about 9 with moderate intellectual disability (one with Down syndrome) in a public school special program were asked to name pictures of four words each that they could say after a model but not from the picture, with immediate praise for correct answers, the correct model after errors and points or candy at the end of the session."},{"k":"murphy2014","apa":"Murphy, J., & Zlomke, K. (2014). Positive peer reporting in the classroom: A review of intervention procedures. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 126–137.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0025-0","y":2014,"sum":"A review of 24 classroom studies (48 cases) of positive peer reporting and its written variant, tootling, with students from preschool to eighth grade (ages 4 to 16) in general education, self-contained special education and residential treatment classrooms. Students are taught with examples, modeling and a practice round to report a classmate's positive behavior in a short daily session, and earn points toward individual or group rewards."},{"k":"murphy2019","apa":"Murphy, A., Moore, J. W., Dart, E. H., Brewer, R. R., Ford, W. B., Lundy, M. P., & Ackley, M. (2019). Increasing conversation using restricted access and chain schedules of reinforcement. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 182–187.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00298-2","y":2019,"sum":"A 15-year-old with autism and mild intellectual disability who gave almost no words to questions was taught in a clinic by pausing his video game until his answer met a word requirement that rose from one to two to four words, with follow-up questions chained before the game restarted and finally a return question required. Answers rose from under one word to about four words, and to over nine words once he had to ask a question back."},{"k":"murphy2019b","apa":"Murphy, C., Lyons, K., Kelly, M., Barnes-Holmes, Y., & Barnes-Holmes, D. (2019). Using the Teacher IRAP (T-IRAP) interactive computerized programme to teach complex flexible relational responding with children with diagnosed autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 52–65.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00302-9","y":2019,"sum":"Five students aged 7–12 with moderate to severe autism at an ABA school practiced 'Like'/'Different' with shapes, then animal and food categories, in 20-min sessions that alternated tabletop cards with a free computer program (T-IRAP) that stops after an error until the right key is pressed; a 24-trial blocked-trials routine was added when a relation was hard."},{"k":"nardone1986","apa":"Nardone, M. J., Tryon, W. W., & O’Connor, K. (1986). The effectiveness and generalization of a cognitive-behavioral group treatment to reduce impulsive/aggressive behavior for boys in a residential setting. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 1(2), 93–103.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360010202","y":1986,"sum":"Ten boys aged 6 to 14 in a residential treatment center for children with emotional disturbance, referred for frequent fighting, attended 13 group sessions over six weeks with relaxation exercises, a four-step problem-solving routine practiced with picture stories (define the problem, think of plans, act on one, judge the result), games that set up the same kinds of conflict, and points exchanged for snacks, small toys and later a group outing."},{"k":"neef1977","apa":"Neef, N. A., Iwata, B. A., & Page, T. J. (1977). The effects of known-item interspersal on acquisition and retention of spelling and sightreading words. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 738.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-738","y":1977,"sum":"Six adolescents with intellectual disability were taught spelling and sight words missed on a pretest under three conditions compared in a multi-element design: alternating each unknown word with a known word (with the share of known words reduced over time), unknown words only, and unknown words only with frequent praise for working."},{"k":"neef1978","apa":"Neef, N. A., Iwata, B. A., & Page, T. J. (1978). Public transportation training: In vivo versus classroom instruction. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 331–344.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-331","y":1978,"sum":"Five young adults aged 18–24 with intellectual disability learned the chain in a classroom through a tabletop model with a doll, yes-or-no questions about slides, and role play on a mock bus, with descriptive praise, an explanation and re-try after errors, and modelling after a second error; each subcomponent was taught to 100% correct over two sessions before the next."},{"k":"nevill2025","apa":"Nevill, R. E., Crawford, M. F., Zarcone, J. R., Maquera, E., Rooker, G. W., & Schmidt, J. D. (2025). A retrospective consecutive controlled case series analysis of the assessment and treatment of elopement in children with autism in an inpatient setting. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 18(4), 903–920.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-024-00979-1","y":2025,"sum":"The request is taught to produce the item the problem behavior used to produce, honored at once at first; the item no longer follows problem behavior. The prompt is faded, then the delay between request and item is lengthened (see Wait for a requested item)."},{"k":"nevin1983","apa":"Nevin, J. A., Mandell, C., & Atak, J. R. (1983). The analysis of behavioral momentum. Journal of the Experimental Analysis of Behavior, 39(1), 49–59.","doi":"10.1901/jeab.1983.39-49","y":1983,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"newell2026","apa":"Newell, K. L., Ardoin, S. P., & Donaldson, J. M. (2026). Elementary students prefer extra reading practice before reading in front of peers. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 19(3), 1317–1328.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-025-01099-0","y":2026,"sum":"Three readers with dyslexia and no other disability (ages 7, 11 and 32) first read 1-minute passages with and without the colored overlay they had picked as clearest, and the overlay never raised correct words per minute (it lowered the adult's)."},{"k":"newsom1977","apa":"Newsom, C. D., & Simon, K. M. (1977). A simultaneous discrimination procedure for the measurement of vision in nonverbal children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 633–644.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-633","y":1977,"sum":"Eleven nonverbal children aged 5 to 16 diagnosed with autism or what was then called childhood schizophrenia were taught to walk from a chair 6.1 meters away and touch the correct one of two cards, moving through 16 fading steps from striped cards to a downward-pointing Snellen E versus a leftward one, with praise and a small edible for each correct choice and a program stop and correction procedure after errors."},{"k":"noel2016","apa":"Noel, C. R., & Getch, Y. Q. (2016). Noncontingent reinforcement in after-school settings to decrease classroom disruptive behavior for students with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 261–265.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0117-0","y":2016,"sum":"Two boys with autism aged 10 and 12 in a university after-school program whose negative talk (whining, threats, refusals) and property destruction, judged attention-maintained by interview and observation, required a one-to-one aide. Problem behavior fell from baseline means of 11 and 20 per session to about 3 and 7 with NCR, rose again when NCR was withdrawn, and dropped to about 1 per session when it returned."},{"k":"nohelty2022","apa":"Nohelty, K., Bradford, C. B., Hirschfeld, L., Miyake, C. J., & Novack, M. N. (2022). Effectiveness of telehealth direct therapy for individuals with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(3), 643–658.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-021-00603-6","y":2022,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"northgrave2019","apa":"Northgrave, J., Vladescu, J. C., DeBar, R. M., Toussaint, K. A., & Schnell, L. K. (2019). Reinforcer choice on skill acquisition for children with autism spectrum disorder: A systematic replication. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 401–406.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0246-8","y":2019,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"novack2019","apa":"Novack, M. N., Hong, E., Dixon, D. R., & Granpeesheh, D. (2019). An evaluation of a mobile application designed to teach receptive language skills to children with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 66–77.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00312-7","y":2019,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"nozaki1991","apa":"Nozaki, K., Mochizuki, A., Yairo, C., & Tsunoda, T. (1991). Signing environment in an institution: Teaching sign vocabulary to residents with mental retardation for communication with deaf peers. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(2), 79–95.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060202","y":1991,"sum":"In a Japanese institution, four deaf adults with intellectual disability and about 47 hearing residents with mild or moderate intellectual disability were taught 19 everyday signs through short large-group lessons (staff model, everyone imitates, individuals take turns with applause), wall posters, review lessons and a printed sign book, evaluated in a multiple baseline across three sign sets."},{"k":"obrien1972a","apa":"O'Brien, F., Bugle, C., & Azrin, N. H. (1972). Training and maintaining a retarded child's proper eating. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 67–72.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-67","y":1972,"sum":"Children with feeding disorders who could feed themselves but would not were seen in a hospital feeding program after their food refusal had been treated. A 6-year-old girl with Down syndrome, fed by her parents, was offered a small drink in an open cup every 30 s; if she did not drink within 8 s, the parent guided her hands to drink it and then guided five quick lifts of the empty cup to her lips, and self-drinking earned praise."},{"k":"obrien1972c","apa":"O'Brien, F., & Azrin, N. H. (1972). Developing proper mealtime behaviors of the institutionalized retarded. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(4), 389–399.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-389","y":1972,"sum":"Twenty-three adults with moderate to profound intellectual disability (average age about 33) at a state hospital were taught one to one at meals to use a spoon, a fork, a glass with one hand, a knife and fork for cutting, a knife for buttering and a napkin, with spoken instructions, then a demonstration, then hand-over-hand guidance faded as soon as possible, praise for each correct response and food returned after errors, followed by group meals in which…"},{"k":"oconner1989","apa":"O'Conner, Y. M., & Cuvo, A. J. (1989). Teaching dancercise to persons who are mentally handicapped: Programming transfer of stimulus control to a community setting. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(4), 289–311.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040403","y":1989,"sum":"Three young people aged 16–18 with mild or borderline intellectual disability, living in a transitional residential unit, learned a 40-minute taped aerobic dance routine a few exercise sets at a time in one-to-one sessions. The trainer described and slowly modeled each move, guided arms and legs after repeated misses, praised correct moves and sped up to the tape's pace; the youths also earned a token for a late bedtime for attending."},{"k":"oconnor1969","apa":"O'Connor, R. D. (1969). Modification of social withdrawal through symbolic modeling. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 15–22.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-15","y":1969,"sum":"Thirteen nursery-school children rated by their teachers as the most withdrawn, who interacted with peers in fewer than 5 of 32 observed intervals, were randomly assigned to watch alone a 23-minute film of 11 graded scenes in which a child joins other children at play and is welcomed, with a narrator describing what happens (6 children), or a nature film (7 children)."},{"k":"ohara2023","apa":"O'Hara, C., Fernand, J. K., Chhettri, A., & Fosua, B. (2023). Use of a preassessment to inform treatment of rapid eating. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(3), 860–866.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-022-00771-z","y":2023,"sum":"A 12-year-old girl with autism in general and special education classes ate burritos and chips so fast that she overate and took food from her siblings' plates. At a clinic table and then at home, she wore a pager that buzzed every 20 s and was told to chew each bite and wait for the buzz, which alone barely slowed her."},{"k":"olaff2025","apa":"Olaff, H. S., & Holth, P. (2025). Acquisition of incidental bidirectional naming: Isolating the effects of probing and mixed-operant instruction. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 41(2), 200–234.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-025-00221-1","y":2025,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"oleary1969","apa":"O'Leary, K. D., Becker, W. C., Evans, M. B., & Saudargas, R. A. (1969). A token reinforcement program in a public school: A replication and systematic analysis. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 3–13.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-3","y":1969,"sum":"Seven children in a public second-grade class of 21 (mean age about 7 years) were observed while the teacher added, one at a time, posted rules, a structured afternoon schedule, praise for appropriate behavior with ignoring of disruption, and then a token program in which she rated each child from 1 to 10 four times each afternoon for following the rules, the points being traded for small prizes after a delay of up to 3 days; tokens were withdrawn,…"},{"k":"oliver2014","apa":"Oliver, P., & Brady, M. P. (2014). Effects of covert audio coaching on parents’ interactions with young children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 112–116.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0015-2","y":2014,"sum":"Three mothers of 5- and 6-year-old boys with autism were coached at home through a radio earbud to use least-to-most prompts and praise during a 7- to 9-step home routine (an art project, bathing, dressing). Mothers' prompts and praise were recorded by partial interval and the children's independent steps by task analysis, in a multiple baseline across dyads, with untrained routines as generalization probes."},{"k":"omori2017","apa":"Omori, M., & Yamamoto, J.-i. (2018). Sentence reading comprehension by means of training in segment-unit reading for Japanese children with intellectual disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(1), 9–18.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0196-6","y":2018,"sum":"Two boys aged 6 and 8 with intellectual disability (one also with autism) from a mainstream Japanese elementary school could read letters and most single words but not whole sentences without stalling. On a computer, each phrase of a two-line hiragana sentence appeared one at a time in its place and was read aloud (with a spoken model after two misses), then the whole sentence was read and a matching picture shown; the measure was the percentage of…"},{"k":"omori2022","apa":"Omori, M., & Yamamoto, J. (2022). Segment-unit reading comprehension training for Japanese students with autism spectrum disorder and learning disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(3), 857–866.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-021-00671-8","y":2022,"sum":"Two boys aged 6 and 8 with intellectual disability (one also with autism), who could read most single words but stalled between them, practiced two-line sentences on a computer. Each phrase appeared one at a time in its own place in the sentence and the boy read it (hearing it read if he missed it twice); he then read the whole sentence and saw a picture of what it described, until all six practice sentences were read smoothly and correctly in two blocks…"},{"k":"oneill2018","apa":"O'Neill, S. J., McDowell, C., & Leslie, J. C. (2018). A comparison of prompt delays with trial-and-error instruction in conditional discrimination training. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 370–380.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0261-9","y":2018,"sum":"Three adolescents with autism and intellectual disability, inpatients for aggression, property destruction or self-injury, practiced matching and picking out named pictures in 16-trial sessions, earning food only for correct answers given on their own or after the spoken instruction alone."},{"k":"oreilly1989","apa":"O'Reilly, M. F., & Cuvo, A. J. (1989). Teaching self-treatment of cold symptoms to an anoxic brain injured adult. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 4(4), 359–375.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360040406","y":1989,"sum":"A 44-year-old woman with memory loss after an anoxic brain injury, in a residential rehabilitation program, practiced with photo sets showing a person's cold symptoms over three days, a box of first-aid supplies and medicines, and a phone with a phone book."},{"k":"osborne1969","apa":"Osborne, J. G. (1969). Free-time as a reinforcer in the management of classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(2), 113–118.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-113","y":1969,"sum":"Six girls aged 11 to 13 with severe to profound hearing loss and below-average test scores, in one class at a school for the deaf, were told that staying in their seats (asking before getting up) through each 15-minute work period would earn 5 minutes of free time; free time was then given regardless for 5 days, made contingent again, and the work periods were lengthened to 25 minutes."},{"k":"pace1993","apa":"Pace, G. M., Iwata, B. A., Cowdery, G. E., Andree, P. J., & McIntyre, T. (1993). Stimulus (instructional) fading during extinction of self-injurious escape behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 26(2), 205–212.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1993.26-205","y":1993,"sum":"Demands are faded in: the session begins with few demands (sometimes none) and more are added only while problem behavior stays low, until the full assignment is reached. Breaks earned by finishing a set amount of work (response chaining) did the same."},{"k":"packard1970","apa":"Packard, R. G. (1970). The control of \"classroom attention\": A group contingency for complex behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(1), 13–28.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-13","y":1970,"sum":"Four public elementary classes in a rural Michigan school (kindergarten, third, fifth and sixth grade; 25 to 34 students each) were taught to attend (seated and turned to the assigned work, quiet, and following the teacher's instructions) through a teacher-run timer and red light and a group contingency in which the class earned points, or play time in kindergarten, when its share of whole-class attending met a goal that rose in 5% steps."},{"k":"page1976","apa":"Page, T. J., Iwata, B. A., & Neef, N. A. (1976). Teaching pedestrian skills to retarded persons: Generalization from the classroom to the natural environment. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(4), 433–444.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-433","y":1976,"sum":"Five young men aged 16 to 25 with intellectual disability (IQ 55 to 85) at a downtown day program were taught five street-crossing skills (17 steps in all) by moving a doll on a tabletop model of four city blocks and saying what the doll was doing, with praise, error feedback, a second try and modelling. Each skill was taught to 90% or more correct in two consecutive 10-trial sessions, with review sessions before probes."},{"k":"page2017","apa":"Page, S. V., Griffith, K., & Penrod, B. (2017). Reduction of rapid eating in an adolescent female with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(1), 87–91.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0143-y","y":2017,"sum":"A 12-year-old girl with autism who ate very fast wore a vibrating pager set to 20-s intervals during family-style meals at a clinic and at home, first with a rule to chew and wait for the buzz and then with an adult also telling her to wait whenever she reached for a bite early. Mean time between bites (meal length divided by bites) rose from about 11 s to 26–34 s only after the spoken prompt was added, and a return to baseline confirmed the effect."},{"k":"paisey1993","apa":"Paisey, T. J. H., Whitney, R. B., & Wainczak, S. M. (1993). Case study: Noninvasive behavioral treatment of self-injurious hand stereotypy in a child with Rett syndrome. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(2), 133–145.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080206","y":1993,"sum":"A 3½-year-old girl with Rett syndrome who mouthed her hands much of the day and did not grasp objects or respond to requests was taught at home, with her mother as a co-instructor, to touch the switches of cause-and-effect toys, stand up and sit down when asked, hold a spoon and hand it over on request, and imitate the sound \"mah\"."},{"k":"paniagua1987","apa":"Paniagua, F. A. (1987). Management of hyperactive children through correspondence training procedures: A preliminary study. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 2(1), 1–23.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360020102","y":1987,"sum":"Three boys aged 7 to 10 with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder on a child psychiatric inpatient unit were observed in 10-minute sessions for inattention and overactivity. Two earned a chosen toy only when their answer to “Did you sit still?” and similar questions was true and their behavior met a criterion that rose in steps; for the third, a toy was shown and set aside in his bedroom after he promised to sit still and given only if he kept the…"},{"k":"paniagua1988","apa":"Paniagua, F. A., Pumariega, A. J., & Black, S. A. (1988). Clinical effects of correspondence training in the management of hyperactive children. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 3(1), 19–40.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360030103","y":1988,"sum":"Three boys aged 6 to 9 with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder on a child psychiatric inpatient unit were reinforced with small toys for true reports about sitting still and paying attention, for promises they then kept, or for promises with the toy set aside in advance, with a criterion for intervals without inattention, overactivity or noise that rose in steps."},{"k":"parsons1993","apa":"Parsons, M. B., Reid, D. H., & Green, C. W. (1993). Preparing direct service staff to teach people with severe disabilities: A comprehensive evaluation of an effective and acceptable training program. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(3), 163–185.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080302","y":1993,"sum":"Four studies evaluated a program of four 2-hour classes and on-the-job feedback that taught newly hired residential staff to follow a task analysis, prompt from least to most help, reinforce after the last step and correct errors with more help."},{"k":"parsons2008","apa":"Parsons, M. B., Reid, D. H., Towery, D., England, P., & Darden, M. (2008). Remediating minimal progress on teaching programs by adults with severe disabilities in a congregate day setting. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(2), 59–67.","doi":"10.1007/bf03391729","y":2008,"sum":"Four adults with severe or profound cognitive disabilities plus sensory or motor impairments (three used wheelchairs; one had a history of self-injury and aggression) in an adult education program, who had made little or no progress for months on vocational, self-help and communication teaching programs."},{"k":"parsons2009","apa":"Parsons, M. B., Reid, D. H., & Lattimore, L. P. (2009). Increasing independence of adults with autism in community activities: A brief, embedded teaching strategy. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 2(2), 40–48.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391747","y":2009,"sum":"Staff who had been doing an activity for an adult with autism were taught to teach it on the spot instead: say what to do, wait and watch, act it out, then touch to guide, with praise (least-to-most prompting). Independence rose during work breaks, in a grocery store and in an office, and mostly held for months."},{"k":"parsons2016","apa":"Parsons, M. B., Bentley, E., Solari, T., & Reid, D. H. (2016). Familiarizing new staff for working with adults with severe disabilities: A case for relationship building. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 211–222.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0129-9","y":2016,"sum":"Four men aged 50–58 with severe intellectual disabilities and features of autism (past aggression and minor self-injury) in a day-program vocational setting, working with new, unfamiliar staff. Familiar staff got about 90% compliance compared with 59–70% for experienced but unfamiliar staff."},{"k":"parsonson1978","apa":"Parsonson, B. S., & Baer, D. M. (1978). Training generalized improvisation of tools by preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 363–380.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-363","y":1978,"sum":"Five preschool children (three typically developing 3-year-olds and two aged 5 and 6 with language and behavior needs) were shown a different unusual substitute tool at the start of each session, from conventional alternatives to items combined or altered, then asked to find something on a tray to finish the task; the first-ever success with a new item earned descriptive praise, and repeats got only a plain description."},{"k":"partington1993","apa":"Partington, J. W., & Bailey, J. S. (1993). Teaching intraverbal behavior to preschool children. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 11, 9–18.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392883","y":1993,"sum":"Eight typically developing 4-year-olds at a preschool took part. In Experiment 1, four children with slightly weaker verbal fluency first learned to name 20 pictures (echoic prompt and correction, to 95% in two sessions) and were then taught one category at a time to answer questions such as “What are some toys?”, with praise and a request for another answer after each one and a picture of a category member as a prompt when they stalled, until they…"},{"k":"pcooke1976","apa":"Cooke, T. P., & Apolloni, T. (1976). Developing positive social-emotional behaviors: A study of training and generalization effects. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 65–78.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-65","y":1976,"sum":"The four least socially active children (aged 6 to 9, with learning disabilities) in a seven-child experimental class were taught in daily 16-minute playroom sessions, with instructions, modeling, prompts and praise by name, to smile at, share with, touch kindly and compliment peers, adding one behavior at a time."},{"k":"peterson1968","apa":"Peterson, R. F. (1968). Some experiments on the organization of a class of imitative behaviors. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(3), 225–235.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-225","y":1968,"sum":"A 12-year-old girl with severe intellectual disability and no speech, who had learned generalized imitation in an earlier study, was seen at mealtimes and given spoonfuls of food with praise for copying some of the actions the experimenter modeled after the cue to do this, while other modeled actions, and some cued actions that did not match the cue, were never rewarded."},{"k":"peterson2017","apa":"Peterson, K. M., Volkert, V. M., & Milnes, S. M. (2017). Evaluation of practice trials to increase self-drinking in a child with a feeding disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(2), 167–171.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0147-7","y":2017,"sum":"Ella, a 6-year-old with Down syndrome who had depended on tube feeding, was seen weekly in an outpatient feeding clinic, where her parents set a small drink of formula in an open cup in front of her; if she did not drink within 8 s, the parent guided the drink with hands over hers and then guided five quick lifts of the empty cup to her lips."},{"k":"peysin2023","apa":"Peysin, J., Fienup, D., & Romas, S. (2023). An experimental comparison of in-person and remote instruction for preschoolers with disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(2), 558–572.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-022-00737-1","y":2023,"sum":"Two 10-year-olds with intellectual delays in a self-contained class practiced unknown words on a computer: each word appeared, a recording then read it, and the student tried to say it first and repeated it after, three times per word each session. Words read within 2 s on two tests in a row were counted as learned and swapped for new ones, and both students learned far more practiced than unpracticed words."},{"k":"phillips1968","apa":"Phillips, E. L. (1968). Achievement Place: Token reinforcement procedures in a home-style rehabilitation setting for \"pre-delinquent\" boys. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(3), 213–223.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-213","y":1968,"sum":"Three boys aged 12 to 14 placed by a juvenile court in a community group home run by house-parents earned points for listed behaviors, traded weekly for privileges such as television, snacks and bicycle use, and lost points for others."},{"k":"phillips1971","apa":"Phillips, E. L., Phillips, E. A., Fixsen, D. L., & Wolf, M. M. (1971). Achievement Place: Modification of the behaviors of pre-delinquent boys within a token economy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(1), 45–59.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-45","y":1971,"sum":"Four to six boys aged 12 to 15, placed by a juvenile court in a family-style group home run on points traded for privileges, took part in four experiments: point fines for lateness to dinner, points earned or lost for a 100-item room-cleaning checklist (faded to checks on 8% of days for six months), 10 points per penny put into a locked savings bank toward a chosen purchase, and points for answers on a nightly news quiz."},{"k":"phillips1973","apa":"Phillips, E. L., Phillips, E. A., Wolf, M. M., & Fixsen, D. L. (1973). Achievement Place: Development of the elected manager system. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 541–561.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-541","y":1973,"sum":"Boys aged 10 to 16 referred by the juvenile court to a family-style group home cleaned two bathrooms each day under several systems: tasks and points shared by the whole group, tasks and points for each boy's own items, or a peer manager (bought with points, elected or picked at random) who assigned the items and could give points, give or take points, or do neither."},{"k":"piazza2000","apa":"Piazza, C. C., Adelinis, J. D., Hanley, G. P., Goh, H.-L., & Delia, M. D. (2000). An evaluation of the effects of matched stimuli on behaviors maintained by automatic reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 33(1), 13–27.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2000.33-13","y":2000,"sum":"When problem behavior is maintained by its own sensory consequences, items that give a similar sensation compete with it best; engaging with them is prompted and praised, and the items are rotated."},{"k":"pierce1974a","apa":"Pierce, C. H., & Risley, T. R. (1974). Improving job performance of Neighborhood Youth Corps aides in an urban recreation program. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 207–215.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-207","y":1974,"sum":"Seven Black adolescents aged 14 to 16, paid by the hour through a federal summer youth job program as aides at a neighborhood recreation center, each had a written job description and a daily checklist of 10 to 25 duties (in the assigned area, and each task done correctly on time), with immediate feedback on missed duties and the day's percentage posted."},{"k":"pinkston1973","apa":"Pinkston, E. M., Reese, N. M., LeBlanc, J. M., & Baer, D. M. (1973). Independent control of a preschool child's aggression and peer interaction by contingent teacher attention. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(1), 115–124.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-115","y":1973,"sum":"A 3½-year-old boy in a university preschool, who attacked peers and seldom played with them successfully, was the focus. Teachers first turned their attention to the child he attacked instead of to him, then gave him attention mainly while he was interacting with peers, directed him to peers' play and asked peers to include him, each with a reversal."},{"k":"plummer1977","apa":"Plummer, S., Baer, D. M., & LeBlanc, J. M. (1977). Functional considerations in the use of procedural timeout and an effective alternative. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 689–705.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-689","y":1977,"sum":"Two 5-year-olds in university special preschool classes: a girl with autism whose grabbing and hoarding of toys, leaving activities, echolalic talk and tantrums followed adults' play instructions, and a boy with intellectual disability and autism who rocked, played with his fingers, threw food and spat after instructions to eat at lunch."},{"k":"polirstok1977","apa":"Polirstok, S. R., & Greer, R. D. (1977). Remediation of mutually aversive interactions between a problem student and four teachers by training the student in reinforcement techniques. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 707–716.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-707","y":1977,"sum":"An eighth-grade girl often sent to the dean for abusive language was taught through class and individual role play to give her teachers approval (thanks, appreciative comments, smiles, nods and appropriate hand raising), then cued by a recorded prompt every 30 seconds through an earpiece and given up to 50 points a day for doing so in four classes."},{"k":"powers1992","apa":"Powers, S., Thibadeau, S., & Rose, K. (1992). Antecedent exercise and its effects on self-stimulation. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 7(1), 15–22.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360070103","y":1992,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy with intellectual disability and autism-like behavior in a residential program roller skated for 10 minutes on a paved path just before a 30-minute free-play session with puzzles, blocks and clay, in an ABAB design."},{"k":"price2017","apa":"Price, R., Marsh, A. J., & Fisher, M. H. (2018). Teaching young adults with intellectual and developmental disabilities community-based navigation skills to take public transportation. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(1), 46–50.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0202-z","y":2018,"sum":"Four young adults aged 17 to 25 with autism, Down syndrome or mild intellectual disability in a university school-to-work transition program learned to use the Google Maps app on their own phones to take public buses to set destinations."},{"k":"pritchard2017","apa":"Pritchard, D., Hoerger, M., Penney, H., Eiri, L., Hellawell, L., Fothergill, S., & Mace, F. C. (2017). Training staff to avoid problem behavior related to restricting access to preferred activities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(1), 92–95.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0061-4","y":2017,"sum":"Students with intellectual disabilities at a residential special school whose serious problem behavior was linked to staff restricting access to requested activities (computer games, food, DVDs, outings); the 10 trained direct-care staff were the study participants."},{"k":"quigley2018","apa":"Quigley, J., Griffith, A. K., & Kates-McElrath, K. (2018). A comparison of modeling, prompting, and a multi-component intervention for teaching play skills to children with developmental disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 315–326.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0225-0","y":2018,"sum":"Children with autism learned to build Lego structures as chains of steps. Full physical guidance faded to lighter guidance (most-to-least) made fewer errors than least-to-most prompting but was sometimes slower; most-to-least with a short delay before each prompt was nearly as fast as least-to-most, with fewer errors."},{"k":"quilitch1973","apa":"Quilitch, H. R., & Risley, T. R. (1973). The effects of play materials on social play. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 573–578.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-573","y":1973,"sum":"Groups of six children about 7 years old at a recreation center in a low-income city neighborhood were given either six games made for two to four players or six toys usually used by one child, switched every 15 minutes within a session for four groups and across nine daily 15-minute sessions."},{"k":"radogna2024","apa":"Radogna, C., D'Angelo, G., & Lerman, D. C. (2024). Assessing and teaching job-related social skills to adults with neurodevelopmental disorders in Italy. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 17(3), 803–814.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-023-00873-2","y":2024,"sum":"Children whose problem behavior was maintained by escape from difficult tasks were taught to ask for assistance, and real help followed each request; problem behavior fell as the requests rose, and the effect carried to new teachers and settings. Teach on hard items mixed with easy ones, prompt the request before problem behavior, and fade the prompt."},{"k":"rafferty2011","apa":"Rafferty, L. A., Arroyo, J., Ginnane, S., & Wilczynski, K. (2011). Self-monitoring during spelling practice: Effects on spelling accuracy and on-task behavior of three students diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 4(1), 37–45.","doi":"10.1007/bf03391773","y":2011,"sum":"Three 10-year-old fifth graders with ADHD (plus LD or OHI) attended an enrichment period in a rural general education classroom. The teacher trained each one alone, using modeling, examples and non-examples, and rehearsal to 100% on three straight trials. During 15-minute independent spelling practice, a tone sounded through headphones at random times (about every 40 s); at each tone the student asked \"Am I on-task?\" and tallied yes or no on a card."},{"k":"rapport1976","apa":"Rapport, M. D., & Bostow, D. E. (1976). The effects of access to special activities on the performance in four categories of academic tasks with third-grade students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 372.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-372","y":1976,"sum":"Nineteen third-grade students could join the day's recreational activities only after completing at least 80% of each of four daily assignments correctly (writing, copying words, spelling and dictionary work). The teacher ran the plan alone in an ABAB design, and daily completion and accuracy in each category were measured; only a one-page summary was printed."},{"k":"raver1978","apa":"Raver, S. A., Cooke, T. P., & Apolloni, T. (1978). Generalization effects from intratherapy articulation training: A case study. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 436.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-436","y":1978,"sum":"Two boys aged 12 and 13 in a family-style group home for youths placed by the juvenile court, who substituted or dropped sounds such as l, r, th and z, practiced picture words with housemates aged 12 to 15 whose speech was typical. The peers, paid in the home's points, picked out wrong words, said them correctly and had the boy repeat them, often syllable by syllable, until they sounded right."},{"k":"re2024","apa":"Ré, T. C., Rieken, C. J., Brandt, J. A., Pacitto, G. O., & Yepez, J. (2024). Differential reinforcement of low frequency behavior as an interdependent group contingency for children diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder. Journal of Behavioral Education, 33(4), 949–964.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-023-09512-w","y":2024,"sum":"Five boys with autism (two also with ADHD, one also with epilepsy and bipolar disorder), ages 7–14, in a separate day school language-arts class with one-to-two staffing; the target was talking out without permission during 15 min of group instruction. Group call-outs fell from a baseline mean of 30.6 per 15 min (first phase) to 5.8 during intervention across three reversals; individual means dropped, for example, from 6.3 to 0.7 and from 12.1 to 4.1."},{"k":"redd1969","apa":"Redd, W. H. (1969). Effects of mixed reinforcement contingencies on adults' control of children's behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(4), 249–254.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-249","y":1969,"sum":"Two boys aged 12 and 16 with severe intellectual disability living in an institution, each in a playroom group of five who rarely played together, met three adults in turn each day: one who gave candy and praise only for cooperative play (shaped first from touching a toy or playing near a peer), one who gave them on a timer regardless of behavior, and one who switched between the two."},{"k":"redinger1986","apa":"Redinger, R., Alger, K., & Thomas, D. R. (1986). Arranging a residential environment for the developmentally disabled to generate and maintain verbal behavior. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 1(4), 231–253.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360010403","y":1986,"sum":"This descriptive report covers a residential unit for 15 adults aged 21 to 49 with severe or profound intellectual disability who had no functional speech but could imitate signs; staff built signing (and picture-symbol cards) into daily routines such as a group snack meeting, house jobs, cooperative table setting with peers and requests for missing items."},{"k":"reid1977","apa":"Reid, D. H., & Hurlbut, B. (1977). Teaching nonvocal communication skills to multihandicapped retarded adults. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 591–603.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-591","y":1977,"sum":"Four adults aged 31 to 34 with severe physical disabilities and severe intellectual disability, without functional speech and living on an institution ward, were taught after a physical therapy evaluation to point with a head pointer or a hand, first to blocks on a communication board and then to word-and-photograph pictures named by the trainer, through instructions, praise, corrective feedback, faded manual guidance and practice."},{"k":"reid1991","apa":"Reid, D. H., & Parsons, M. B. (1991). Making choice a routine part of mealtimes for persons with profound mental retardation. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(4), 249–261.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060403","y":1991,"sum":"Three nonspeaking women (20 to 37) with profound intellectual disability, who had already learned to pick one of two items in class, were offered at least four choices per lunch in a residential facility dining room (small samples of two items in view, asked to pick one, a taste of each if no pick in 10 seconds, food served as planned if still no pick), in a multiple baseline across women with one reversal."},{"k":"reid2010","apa":"Reid, D. H., Parsons, M. B., & Lattimore, L. P. (2010). Designing and evaluating assessment-based interventions to reduce stereotypy among adults with autism in a community job. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 3(2), 27–36.","doi":"10.1007/bf03391762","y":2010,"sum":"Three nonvocal men with severe autism (33–45 years) in part-time clerical jobs at a publishing company spent much of their waiting time rocking, hand flapping or staring. For the man whose stereotypy came almost only while he waited, staff set a second clerical task he already did well on a small table beside his station, told him to switch to it at each wait, and told him to switch back when new work was ready."},{"k":"renne1976","apa":"Renne, C. M., & Creer, T. L. (1976). Training children with asthma to use inhalation therapy equipment. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 1–11.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-1","y":1976,"sum":"Four children aged 7 to 12 with chronic asthma at a residential treatment center, who had not learned to use a positive-pressure breathing machine from repeated instructions, were taught in the hospital treatment room to sit upright facing the machine, keep a tight lip seal without cheek puffing or nostril flaring, and breathe with the belly, using instructions, praise, tickets toward a chosen gift for meeting a goal set from their own best trial, and a…"},{"k":"repp1974","apa":"Repp, A. C., & Deitz, S. M. (1974). Reducing aggressive and self-injurious behavior of institutionalized retarded children through reinforcement of other behaviors. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(2), 313–325.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-313","y":1974,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy with moderate intellectual disability, sight in one eye only and severe speech difficulty, in a classroom of nine at a state residential center, whose aggression included throwing objects, hitting, biting, kicking, pinching, tearing papers, spitting and knocking over furniture (a 13-year-old boy's aggression in another class was treated the same way with a reward menu)."},{"k":"reynolds1968","apa":"Reynolds, N. J., & Risley, T. R. (1968). The role of social and material reinforcers in increasing talking of a disadvantaged preschool child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(3), 253–262.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-253","y":1968,"sum":"A 4-year-old girl from a low-income family who rarely spoke in preschool was observed in free play while teachers gave attention whenever she talked and, when she asked for materials, handed them over only after she answered one to three questions about them; in other phases attention and materials followed silence, or materials were given at once on request."},{"k":"rincover1975","apa":"Rincover, A., & Koegel, R. L. (1975). Setting generality and stimulus control in autistic children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(3), 235–246.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-235","y":1975,"sum":"Ten children aged 6 to 13 with autism living in an institution, mostly mute or echolalic, were each taught one skill (copy a modeled action, touch a named body part, clap, or raise the right or left arm) in a small room through physical guidance that was faded, with candy and praise, until they made 20 unprompted correct responses in a row."},{"k":"ringer1973","apa":"Ringer, V. M. J. (1973). The use of a \"token helper\" in the management of classroom behavior problems and in teacher training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 671–677.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-671","y":1973,"sum":"In a fourth-grade class of 37 in Auckland, an adult helper initialed a square on each student's 20-square card, with praise, whenever the student was following the teacher's current instructions; a full card earned a visit to the principal for praise and a school stamp, and the teacher gradually took over the initialing."},{"k":"risley1968","apa":"Risley, T. R. (1968). The effects and side effects of punishing the autistic behaviors of a deviant child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(1), 21–34.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-21","y":1968,"sum":"A 6-year-old girl with a brain injury from meningitis in infancy, seizures and autism, who did not speak or imitate and climbed dangerously, was seen in a clinic room and at home with her mother. Looking at the adult's face was shaped with sips of milk and praise, and motor imitation (clapping, then patting the table) was taught with modeling, physical guidance faded to the model alone, and milk; climbing and rocking were reduced with electric shock and…"},{"k":"risley1968a","apa":"Risley, T. R., & Hart, B. (1968). Developing correspondence between the non-verbal and verbal behavior of preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(4), 267–281.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-267","y":1968,"sum":"Twelve 4- and 5-year-olds from low-income families in a half-day preschool were asked, in two groups at end-of-day snack, what good thing they had done that day; snack and praise followed first any statement that they had used a named material (blocks, paint, later other toys and books) and then only statements that matched teachers' records of what they had used in free play earlier that day, repeated across materials."},{"k":"risley1970","apa":"Risley, T. R., & Reynolds, N. J. (1970). Emphasis as a prompt for verbal imitation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(3), 185–190.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-185","y":1970,"sum":"Three 5-year-olds and three 4-year-olds from low-income families at a preschool were asked to repeat sentences made of one to five short phrases while the teacher stressed key words by raising her voice, with the share of stressed words varied, and a small candy followed any reply regardless of accuracy."},{"k":"robertson1976","apa":"Robertson, S. J., DeReus, D. M., & Drabman, R. S. (1976). Peer and college-student tutoring as reinforcement in a token economy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 169–177.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-169","y":1976,"sum":"A class of 18 second graders (16 analyzed) about a year behind in reading and described as disruptive (out of seat, noise, calling out, noncompliance, touching others' property, aggression) in a rural elementary school. Tickets alone did not reduce disruption reliably (1.38 to 1.21 behaviors per interval), but when tutoring depended on a ticket, disruption fell to about 0.63–0.68, lower than when the same tutoring was given freely (0.77–0.82); fifth-grade…"},{"k":"rock2005","apa":"Rock, M. L. (2005). Use of strategic self-monitoring to enhance academic engagement, productivity, and accuracy of students with and without exceptionalities. Journal of Positive Behavior Interventions, 7(1), 3–17.","doi":"10.1177/10983007050070010201","y":2005,"sum":"The student is taught what working looks like, then to mark the card at each cue; at first an adult marks too and the two compare, and agreement earns a small reward. The cues are spaced further apart as on-task behavior holds. Self-monitoring is among the best-supported classroom interventions for students with behavior problems."},{"k":"rodriguez2009","apa":"Rodriguez, B. J., Loman, S. L., & Horner, R. H. (2009). A preliminary analysis of the effects of coaching feedback on teacher implementation fidelity of First Step to Success. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 2(2), 11–21.","doi":"10.1007/bf03391744","y":2009,"sum":"Three typically developing boys (two 5-year-old kindergartners and one 6-year-old first grader) in general education classrooms, whose talking out, leaving the seat, noncompliance and aggression were judged by interview and ABC observation to be maintained by adult attention, with their teachers running the First Step to Success program."},{"k":"rogerswarren1976","apa":"Rogers-Warren, A., & Baer, D. M. (1976). Correspondence between saying and doing: Teaching children to share and praise. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 335–354.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-335","y":1976,"sum":"Thirty-two preschool children aged 3 to 5 at a university preschool, in groups of six to eight, took part in daily 10-minute art periods followed by a short report time in which an adult asked each child what they had done. After modelling by the adult and a second model, praise with a bit of candy or fruit was given first for any report of sharing or praising peers and then only for true reports, across three experiments with multiple baselines across…"},{"k":"rosenbaum1976","apa":"Rosenbaum, M. S., & Breiling, J. (1976). The development and functional control of reading-comprehension behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(3), 323–333.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-323","y":1976,"sum":"A 12-year-old girl with an autism-related diagnosis, whose test scores fell in the severe intellectual disability range and who could read aloud without understanding, was taught in 15- to 20-minute sessions near her classroom to carry out printed instructions: body actions and pointing to object or action pictures among four to six."},{"k":"rosenberg2010","apa":"Rosenberg, N. E., Schwartz, I. S., & Davis, C. A. (2010). Evaluating the utility of commercial videotapes for teaching hand washing to children with autism. Education and Treatment of Children, 33(3), 443–455.","doi":"10.1353/etc.0.0098","y":2010,"sum":"Hand washing is taught as a sequence of steps. A store-bought video of hand washing taught one of three preschoolers with autism most of the steps and the other two none; a video made for the child then brought partial gains, so plan in-person prompting for a child the video does not teach."},{"k":"rotta2022","apa":"Rotta, K., Rangler, K., Ragotzy, S., & Poling, A. (2022). Increasing physical activity in young adults with an intellectual disability via a classroom-wide treatment package. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(4), 1314–1325.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-022-00691-y","y":2022,"sum":"Three young people aged 16–18 with mild or borderline intellectual disability, living in a transitional residential unit, learned a 40-minute taped aerobic dance routine a few exercise sets at a time in one-to-one sessions. The trainer described and slowly modeled each move, guided arms and legs after repeated misses, praised correct moves and sped up to the tape's pace; the youths also earned a token for a late bedtime for attending."},{"k":"rowbury1976","apa":"Rowbury, T. G., Baer, A. M., & Baer, D. M. (1976). Interactions between teacher guidance and contingent access to play in developing preacademic skills of deviant preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 85–104.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-85","y":1976,"sum":"Children aged 4 to 7 with severe behavior problems in a special preschool class (four in the first experiment, three new children in the second) worked in a room divided into a work area of preacademic tasks (matching, puzzles, form boxes, pegboards, tracing) and a play area; each defined task completion earned praise and a token for 5 minutes of play, with a small snack if the child asked."},{"k":"russo1977","apa":"Russo, D. C., & Koegel, R. L. (1977). A method for integrating an autistic child into a normal public-school classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 579–590.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-579","y":1977,"sum":"A 5-year-old girl with autism in a public kindergarten class of 20 to 30 children received tokens and praise from a therapist beside her for social acts toward others and for answering questions, while self-stimulation was treated with token loss, a sharp \"No\" and brief manual blocking that was faded; the teacher was then trained to run and thin the program, and it was continued in first grade."},{"k":"sack1992","apa":"Sack, S. H., McLean, L. S., McLean, J. E., & Spradlin, J. E. (1992). Effects of increased opportunities within scripted activities on communication rates of individuals with severe retardation. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 7(3), 235–257.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360070306","y":1992,"sum":"Staff in a state residential facility were taught to run a scripted six-step snack routine and art routine with delayed prompting (show the item, look and wait, then prompt) for five youths aged 15 to 18 with severe or profound intellectual disability, in a multiple baseline and probe design across activities and groups."},{"k":"sailor1968","apa":"Sailor, W., Guess, D., Rutherford, G., & Baer, D. M. (1968). Control of tantrum behavior by operant techniques during experimental verbal training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(3), 237–243.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-237","y":1968,"sum":"A 9-year-old girl with intellectual disability living in an institution, who had stopped speaking at about age 2 and had frequent brief tantrums that seemed to end demands, was taught in two 15-minute sessions a day to repeat words and then phrases of growing length after being told to say them, earning bites of preferred food; silent and whispered attempts were shaped into clear, audible ones first."},{"k":"sailor1971","apa":"Sailor, W. (1971). Reinforcement and generalization of productive plural allomorphs in two retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(4), 305–310.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-305","y":1971,"sum":"Two girls in an institution, aged 15 with severe and 8 with moderate intellectual disability, who named single objects but used no plurals, were shown one object and then a pair and asked what they saw, earning a bite of ice cream or a sip of a drink for correct answers, with a model and a 15-second pause after errors and three correct in a row to move on."},{"k":"sailor1972","apa":"Sailor, W., & Taman, T. (1972). Stimulus factors in the training of prepositional usage in three autistic children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(2), 183–190.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-183","y":1972,"sum":"Three children with autism and limited speech (a girl of 7 and two boys of 5) in a day treatment program were taught to answer a where question with in or on plus the object’s name, with food and praise for correct answers and a pause and a model after errors, using either the same objects for both words or different objects for each."},{"k":"salomonsen2024","apa":"Salomonsen, R., & Eldevik, S. (2024). Effects of serial multiple exemplar training on bidirectional naming in children with autism. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 40(1), 28–52.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-024-00203-9","y":2024,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"salzberg1971","apa":"Salzberg, B. H., Wheeler, A. J., Devar, L. T., & Hopkins, B. L. (1971). The effect of intermittent feedback and intermittent contingent access to play on printing of kindergarten children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(3), 163–171.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-163","y":1971,"sum":"Six kindergartners aged 5 to almost 6 in a rural school printed a daily target letter and the letter Z on lined paper and then went to play; about half the papers, chosen at random each day, were graded in front of the child, at first with neutral feedback only and then with a rule that a graded paper below the child's own accuracy goal had to be redone before play."},{"k":"sances2019","apa":"Sances, J., Day-Watkins, J., & Connell, J. E. (2019). Teaching an adult with autism spectrum disorder to use an activity schedule during a vocational beekeeping task. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 435–439.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00306-5","y":2019,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to follow photo schedules with manual guidance from behind, faded gradually (graduated guidance), so that no adult prompt became part of the chain; they kept following them when the activities were reordered and new pictures were added."},{"k":"sanders1971","apa":"Sanders, R. M., & Hanson, P. J. (1971). A note on a simple procedure for redistributing a teacher's student contacts. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 157–161.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-157","y":1971,"sum":"In a rural classroom shared by two grades, the 12 third graders were required to go to a partitioned play area as soon as they finished each day's assignment, which counted as done only when every answer was correct. Observers counted teacher contacts with each student, and the percent of assignments completed was recorded for 12 to 14 work periods before and after the change."},{"k":"sanders1977","apa":"Sanders, M. R., & Glynn, T. (1977). Functional analysis of a program for training high and low preference peers to modify disruptive classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 503.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-503","y":1977,"sum":"Ten fourth-grade pupils in one New Zealand elementary class with high rates of disruptive behavior, each paired with one classmate manager (five managers the target pupil rated high in preference and five rated low). Target pupils' behavior improved significantly after the first, instructions-only phase and improved further once managers earned backup rewards for monitoring, alongside fewer manager reactions to problem behavior and more, and more…"},{"k":"santogrossi1973","apa":"Santogrossi, D. A., O'Leary, K. D., Romanczyk, R. G., & Kaufman, K. F. (1973). Self-evaluation by adolescents in a psychiatric hospital school token program. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 277–287.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-277","y":1973,"sum":"Nine boys aged 12 to 15 living in a children's psychiatric hospital, with high rates of classroom disruption and reading delays, attended a 30-minute after-school reading class for 51 days with five posted rules. Conditions compared public self-ratings of rule-following with no rewards, teacher-awarded points traded for snacks and small prizes, self-awarded points with the same prizes, and a one-day rule giving or removing points for matching the…"},{"k":"sarokoff2001","apa":"Sarokoff, R. A., Taylor, B. A., & Poulson, C. L. (2001). Teaching children with autism to engage in conversational exchanges: Script fading with embedded textual stimuli. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 34(1), 81–84.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2001.34-81","y":2001,"sum":"The student first reads short scripts (written or recorded) to start a conversation; the scripts are faded from the end of each line backwards until the student speaks without them, and the student goes on to say things never scripted."},{"k":"saudargas1977","apa":"Saudargas, R. W., Madsen, C. H., Jr., & Scott, J. W. (1977). Differential effects of fixed- and variable-time feedback on production rates of elementary school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 673–678.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-673","y":1977,"sum":"Twenty-six typically developing third graders working on weekly individual assignment sheets received a home report of work completed either every Friday or on unpredictable days, when seven to nine randomly chosen students received one each day."},{"k":"saunders2016","apa":"Saunders, K. J., Hine, K., Hayashi, Y., & Williams, D. C. (2016). Adventitious reinforcement of maladaptive stimulus control interferes with learning. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 223–229.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0131-2","y":2016,"sum":"One 14-year-old boy with intellectual disability, autism and a history of screaming outbursts during one-to-one instruction, learning on a wall touch screen to select a blue square instead of a yellow circle. With 10% steps judged every 20 trials, accuracy was near perfect up to 60% size, but one full-size probe dropped him to chance and revived a sweeping pattern that kept him from passing 30% in five restarts."},{"k":"scheithauer2023","apa":"Scheithauer, M., Bernstein, A., & Stremel, J. M. (2023). Treatment of self-injury in Bainbridge-Ropers syndrome: Replication and extensions of behavioral assessments. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(2), 611–616.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-022-00749-x","y":2023,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"schmick2018","apa":"Schmick, A. M., Stanley, C. R., & Dixon, M. R. (2018). Teaching children with autism to identify private events of others in context. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 400–405.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0214-3","y":2018,"sum":"Three adolescents with autism at a specialized school were taught in 8-trial blocks to say what was happening in short video clips and then how a person doing that in that place would feel, with praise for correct answers and say-and-repeat correction for errors."},{"k":"schmidt1969","apa":"Schmidt, G. W., & Ulrich, R. E. (1969). Effects of group contingent events upon classroom noise. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(3), 171–179.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-171","y":1969,"sum":"In a fourth-grade class of 29, every unbroken 10-minute period of free study below 42 decibels on a sound meter earned the class 2 extra minutes of gym and a 2-minute break, and a whistle and timer reset followed any louder noise; a second-grade class of 28 earned gym minutes for 5-minute quiet periods and later lost 5 gym minutes per student for noise made alone or for being out of seat when a timer bell rang."},{"k":"schreibman1975","apa":"Schreibman, L. (1975). Effects of within-stimulus and extra-stimulus prompting on discrimination learning in autistic children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(1), 91–112.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-91","y":1975,"sum":"Six children with autism aged 8 to 14, inpatients in a state hospital with little or no communicative speech, were taught four hard two-choice discriminations each (two picture tasks answered by pointing to the correct card and two nonsense-word tasks answered by pressing a bar), first with no prompt, then with a pointing finger or buzzer prompt that was faded, and with a prompt built into the critical feature (exaggerated, with the wrong choice faded in…"},{"k":"schreibman1978","apa":"Schreibman, L., & Carr, E. G. (1978). Elimination of echolalic responding to questions through the training of a generalized verbal response. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(4), 453–463.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-453","y":1978,"sum":"Preschoolers were taught to say they did not know, and then to add a request to be told, for questions they could not answer; both responses carried over to new questions and teachers. For three of four children it also spread to questions they could answer, so known questions are kept in the mix and answering them is reinforced."},{"k":"schreibman2000","apa":"Schreibman, L., Whalen, C., & Stahmer, A. C. (2000). The use of video priming to reduce disruptive transition behavior in children with autism. Journal of Positive Behavior Interventions, 2(1), 3–11.","doi":"10.1177/109830070000200102","y":2000,"sum":"Showing the coming transition on a short video just before it (video priming) reduced disruption for children with autism. A warning, a toy to carry or a visual schedule alone did not help in other studies; they worked once moving on earned reinforcement and problem behavior no longer delayed the move."},{"k":"schroeder2023","apa":"Schroeder, C., Ragotzy, S., & Poling, A. (2023). Young adults with intellectual and other developmental disabilities acquire vocational skills with video prompting. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 56(1), 181–200.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.963","y":2023,"sum":"Four adolescents and young adults with autism watched a video model and then learned to wear a mascot costume and entertain customers in a retail store, checked in role play and on the job; all four learned the skills and said they enjoyed the work."},{"k":"schuebel1992","apa":"Schuebel, C. L., & Lalli, J. S. (1992). A program for increasing manual signing by a nonvocal adult within the daily environment. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 7(4), 277–282.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360070403","y":1992,"sum":"A 39-year-old man with intellectual disability who had never spoken was taught the manual signs for soap, shampoo and hairbrush during his nightly shower in a community living arrangement, using modified incidental teaching with most-to-least prompting, in a multiple baseline across signs."},{"k":"schulman1978","apa":"Schulman, J. L., Stevens, T. M., Suran, B. G., Kupst, M. J., & Naughton, M. J. (1978). Modification of activity level through biofeedback and operant conditioning. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(1), 145–152.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-145","y":1978,"sum":"An 11-year-old boy described as highly active with a short attention span, attention-seeking and aggressive outbursts, in a children's day-hospital classroom, whose movement during daily class assignments was reduced; a second experiment raised the activity of a 10-year-old boy on an inpatient unit."},{"k":"schumaker1970","apa":"Schumaker, J., & Sherman, J. A. (1970). Training generative verb usage by imitation and reinforcement procedures. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(4), 273–287.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-273","y":1970,"sum":"Three residents of a state institution aged 14, 16 and 18 with moderate to severe intellectual disability, who spoke in phrases but did not use the regular past tense, were taught one verb at a time to give its -ing or -ed form when asked about now or yesterday, with praise and a chip for correct answers, a brief correction and pause after errors, and a model to repeat after four misses."},{"k":"schumaker1977","apa":"Schumaker, J. B., Hovell, M. F., & Sherman, J. A. (1977). An analysis of daily report cards and parent-managed privileges in the improvement of adolescents' classroom performance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(3), 449–464.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-449","y":1977,"sum":"Seventh- and eighth-grade students referred for disruption, truancy and incomplete work carried a daily report card on which each teacher marked 10 class rules, classwork points from 0 to 4, grades and whether the teacher was satisfied; parents praised improvement and exchanged card points for daily and weekly home privileges."},{"k":"schutte1970","apa":"Schutte, R. C., & Hopkins, B. L. (1970). The effects of teacher attention on following instructions in a kindergarten class. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 117–122.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-117","y":1970,"sum":"Five girls aged 4 years 10 months to 6 years in a rural public kindergarten were given 10 routine group instructions, once each and 2 minutes apart, and the teacher praised or patted each child who followed an instruction within 15 seconds. The teacher recorded each child's response to each instruction (observer agreement 94 to 100%) across 20 sessions in an ABAB design; no mastery criterion was set."},{"k":"schwartz1977","apa":"Schwartz, G. J. (1977). College students as contingency managers for adolescents in a program to develop reading skills. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 645–655.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-645","y":1977,"sum":"Seventh graders aged 12 to 14 in regular classes who read 1.5 to 4.5 years below grade level met weekly for 10 weeks with trained college tutors for graded reading-skill exercises and a contingency contract, set with the language arts teacher, that awarded points for books read (after a summary form and oral questions) and for skill work toward a report card grade, while tutors praised positive statements about reading."},{"k":"schwarz1970","apa":"Schwarz, M. L., & Hawkins, R. P. (1970). Application of delayed reinforcement procedures to the behavior of an elementary school child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 85–96.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-85","y":1970,"sum":"A 12-year-old sixth-grade girl in a general education class, referred through a district program for students with emotional difficulties, watched a recording of that day's math class with an adult after school, timed her own stretches of keeping her hands from her face, then of sitting upright, then of speaking loudly enough (watching a loudness meter), and earned poker chips toward items such as a bracelet and a dress, after an earlier phase in which…"},{"k":"schweitzer1988","apa":"Schweitzer, J. B., & Sulzer-Azaroff, B. (1988). Self-control: Teaching tolerance for delay in impulsive children. Journal of the Experimental Analysis of Behavior, 50(2), 173–186.","doi":"10.1901/jeab.1988.50-173","y":1988,"sum":"Children and adults who always chose the small immediate reward came to choose the larger one when the wait (or the work) required for it began at zero and grew gradually. In children with autism, waits filled with a task were preferred and brought less disruptive behavior than empty waits."},{"k":"scott1974","apa":"Scott, J. W., & Bushell, D., Jr. (1974). The length of teacher contacts and students' off-task behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 39–44.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-39","y":1974,"sum":"Six third graders (four boys, two girls) with low work completion in a 20-minute small-group math lesson with individual workbooks in a Follow Through classroom; off-task behavior was measured for the students not being helped at the moment."},{"k":"sellers2016","apa":"Sellers, T. P., Alai-Rosales, S., & MacDonald, R. P. F. (2016). Taking full responsibility: The ethics of supervision in behavior analytic practice. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9, 299–308.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0144-x","y":2016,"sum":"What a supervisor owes the people they supervise: supervise only what you know, and only as many people as you can supervise well; hand over a task only once the skill is there (an RBT helps with caregiver training, and does not run it); set out the terms of supervision in writing at the start; give feedback soon and specifically, and write it down; and check that supervision works, in the client’s progress, the RBT’s skills and the RBT’s view of it."},{"k":"seymour1976","apa":"Seymour, F. W., & Stokes, T. F. (1976). Self-recording in training girls to increase work and evoke staff praise in an institution for offenders. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 41–54.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-41","y":1976,"sum":"Four girls aged 14 to 18 in the secure unit of a residential center for adolescent offenders, who had not responded to the center's token program, were taught by a therapist outside class to record their own steady work on a card during vocational training (classroom, bag-assembly workshop, office, kitchen), and then to record comments to staff that invited praise, which were taught through role play; the therapist gave tokens for the marks, and tokens…"},{"k":"shafto1977","apa":"Shafto, F., & Sulzbacher, S. (1977). Comparing treatment tactics with a hyperactive preschool child: Stimulant medication and programmed teacher intervention. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 13–20.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-13","y":1977,"sum":"A 4½-year-old boy with intellectual disability and delayed language in a university preschool class serving young children with serious learning and behavior difficulties, who moved from toy to toy and wandered during free play, was helped to stay with one activity: a teacher joined and talked about his play once he played appropriately and gave praise and a piece of sweetened cereal after each 15 seconds of continued play."},{"k":"sherman1974","apa":"Sherman, T. M., & Cormier, W. H. (1974). An investigation of the influence of student behavior on teacher behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 11–21.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-11","y":1974,"sum":"Two 11-year-old fifth graders in a suburban school, a boy described as one of the school's most serious behavior problems and a girl who ignored class rules; target behaviors were challenging the teacher's instructions, talking without permission, leaving the seat and not paying attention."},{"k":"shillingsburg2018","apa":"Shillingsburg, M. A., Frampton, S. E., Wymer, S. C., & Bartlett, B. (2018). A preliminary procedure for teaching children with autism to mand for social information. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(1), 34–38.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0163-7","y":2018,"sum":"Two 6-year-old boys with autism at a clinic, who could already ask wh- questions about missing items, were taught to ask an adult about herself (for example, what she likes to eat) when the therapist asked them something about her that they did not know, while trials with known or visible answers were mixed in. Prompts to walk over and echo the question were delayed and then dropped, and a wrong guess brought the same question back until the boy asked."},{"k":"shore1997","apa":"Shore, B. A., Iwata, B. A., DeLeon, I. G., Kahng, S., & Smith, R. G. (1997). An analysis of reinforcer substitutability using object manipulation and self-injury as competing responses. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 30(1), 21–41.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1997.30-21","y":1997,"sum":"When problem behavior is maintained by its own sensory consequences, items that give a similar sensation compete with it best; engaging with them is prompted and praised, and the items are rotated."},{"k":"siegel1977","apa":"Siegel, R. K. (1977). Stimulus selection and tracking during urination: Autoshaping directed behavior with toilet targets. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 255–265.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-255","y":1977,"sum":"After finding that ten adult men aimed most at a small floating target placed in a toilet, the author tested a striped float tied in one ward toilet with nine boys aged 8 to 14 with moderate intellectual disability living in an institution, three of whom often urinated on seats, walls and floors, and later added staff reminders to try to hit the ball; a 15-year-old with severe intellectual disability was also followed at home with a float in his favorite…"},{"k":"sigafoos1992","apa":"Sigafoos, J., Roberts, D., Couzens, D., & Caycho, L. (1992). Improving instruction for adults with developmental disabilities: Evaluation of a staff training package. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 7(4), 283–297.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360070404","y":1992,"sum":"Three adult day-program staff were trained (inservice, review of their own video, self-monitoring and feedback) to use a 3-second time delay, least-to-most prompting and praise while teaching three nonspeaking men (23 to 32) with severe intellectual disability to make their lunch sandwich, in a multiple baseline across staff–client pairs."},{"k":"sigafoos1994","apa":"Sigafoos, J., & Kerr, M. (1994). Provision of leisure activities for the reduction of challenging behavior. Behavioral Interventions, 9(1), 43–53.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360090105","y":1994,"sum":"Three men aged 19, 29 and 30 with severe intellectual disability, living in houses on the grounds of a residential facility, who hit others, hurt themselves, broke things or rocked mostly when they had nothing to do, were seated at a table with magazines and a snack (chips and juice) during free time."},{"k":"sigafoos2005","apa":"Sigafoos, J., O'Reilly, M., Cannella, H., Upadhyaya, M., Edrisinha, C., Lancioni, G. E., Hundley, A., Andrews, A., Garver, C., & Young, D. (2005). Computer-presented video prompting for teaching microwave oven use to three adults with developmental disabilities. Journal of Behavioral Education, 14(3), 189–201.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-005-6297-2","y":2005,"sum":"Each step is shown on a short video clip just before the student does it (video prompting). Secondary students with moderate disabilities learned to make a microwave food, a stovetop food and a sandwich with video prompts and constant time delay; with computer-presented video prompts, two of three adults with developmental disabilities learned a ten-step microwave task."},{"k":"silbaugh2019","apa":"Silbaugh, B. C., & Falcomata, T. S. (2019). Effects of a lag schedule with progressive time delay on sign mand variability in a boy with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 124–132.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00273-x","y":2019,"sum":"A 23-year-old man with autism whose self-injury was maintained by access to preferred items and a 10-year-old boy with autism whose aggression was maintained by escape from work first practiced each way of asking (a tablet app, a picture card, a switch and, for the man, a spoken word) with brief prompting."},{"k":"singh2003","apa":"Singh, N. N., Wahler, R. G., Adkins, A. D., & Myers, R. E. (2003). Soles of the feet: A mindfulness-based self-control intervention for aggression by an individual with mild mental retardation and mental illness. Research in Developmental Disabilities, 24(3), 158–169.","doi":"10.1016/S0891-4222(03)00026-X","y":2003,"sum":"The routine is practiced daily, first when calm, then while recalling an event that made the student angry, then in real situations: attention moves from the event to the soles of the feet until the student is calm. Aggression fell and stayed low in adolescents and adults with intellectual disability and mental illness."},{"k":"singh2007","apa":"Singh, N. N., Lancioni, G. E., Winton, A. S. W., Adkins, A. D., Wahler, R. G., Sabaawi, M., & Singh, J. (2007). Individuals with mental illness can control their aggressive behavior through mindfulness training. Behavior Modification, 31(3), 313–328.","doi":"10.1177/0145445506293585","y":2007,"sum":"The routine is practiced daily, first when calm, then while recalling an event that made the student angry, then in real situations: attention moves from the event to the soles of the feet until the student is calm. Aggression fell and stayed low in adolescents and adults with intellectual disability and mental illness."},{"k":"sivaraman2017","apa":"Sivaraman, M. (2017). Using multiple exemplar training to teach empathy skills to children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 337–346.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0183-y","y":2017,"sum":"Two 5-year-old boys with autism at a private school in India were taught to respond to adults' displays of happiness, frustration and sadness or pain (for example, asking \"Are you okay?\" after an adult stubbed a toe, or offering and then giving help when an adult could not find or fix something)."},{"k":"skiba1971","apa":"Skiba, E. A., Pettigrew, L. E., & Alden, S. E. (1971). A behavioral approach to the control of thumbsucking in the classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(2), 121–125.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-121","y":1971,"sum":"Three 8-year-old third-grade girls in three public school classrooms of 30 to 35 students, each with a long history of thumbsucking during lessons. Thumbsucking time dropped for all three girls when praise followed the hands-busy behaviors. When praise was withheld it rose sharply for one girl (to about three times her baseline in the first such lesson) and a little for another, while the third stayed low; it dropped again when praise resumed."},{"k":"solomon1973","apa":"Solomon, R. W., & Wahler, R. G. (1973). Peer reinforcement control of classroom problem behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(1), 49–56.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-49","y":1973,"sum":"Five sixth-grade boys in a rural class of 30 who talked without permission, left their seats and played with objects during social studies, together with five popular classmates trained as helpers. Rule-breaking fell from 68% to 87% of intervals in baseline to 17% to 55% after peer training, rose again (46% to 75%) when the helpers were asked to act as before, and dropped to 10% to 41% when they resumed."},{"k":"soutor1994","apa":"Soutor, T. A., Houlihan, D., & Young, A. (1994). An examination of response covariation in the behavioral treatment of identical twin boys with multiple behavioral disorders. Behavioral Interventions, 9(3), 141–155.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360090302","y":1994,"sum":"One of two 3-year-8-month-old twin boys with autism and very limited speech in a special-needs preschool was given 10 one-step instructions (such as sit down, stand up, come here, touch your nose) in each of two 20-minute table sessions a day, with the adult modeling each action; his twin was probed as an untreated comparison."},{"k":"spreat1990","apa":"Spreat, S., Lamina, J., Jefferys, J., Axelrod, S., Murphy, B. J., & McGuffin, P. (1990). The impact of environmental noise on mealtime social interactions in a residential facility for persons with mental retardation. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(1), 1–13.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050102","y":1990,"sum":"Ten residents aged 13 to 34 with moderate to profound intellectual disability and severe aggression, self-injury or pica, living on one residential treatment unit, and their 15 direct-care staff, observed at lunch and dinner. At lunch, residents interacted in about 10% of observations with the radio and TV loud versus 14 to 16% under the three quieter conditions, and switching loud, soft, loud gave 4%, 15% and 8%."},{"k":"stafford1988","apa":"Stafford, M. W., Sundberg, M. L., & Braam, S. J. (1988). A preliminary investigation of the consequences that define the mand and the tact. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 6, 61–71.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392829","y":1988,"sum":"Once a one-word request is reliable, longer and more socially expected requests are shaped one part at a time (getting attention, then the request, then the polite word), each part reinforced as it is added, while problem behavior stays low."},{"k":"stahmer1992","apa":"Stahmer, A. C., & Schreibman, L. (1992). Teaching children with autism appropriate play in unsupervised environments using a self-management treatment package. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 25(2), 447–459.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1992.25-447","y":1992,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to tell appropriate play from inappropriate play, to record their own play and to take rewards for meeting goals; appropriate play rose and stayed up when no adult was present."},{"k":"stclair2024","apa":"St. Clair, M., Massoudi, K., Tarbox, J., Najdowski, A., Simchoni, L., Jackson, M., & Persicke, A. (2024). Making deception fun: Teaching Autistic individuals how to play friendly tricks. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 17(4), 1134–1146.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-024-00935-z","y":2024,"sum":"Four boys with autism aged 8–16 with fluent language were taught in home or clinic sessions with a rule defining a trick, examples and non-examples, a rule that tricks must not hurt feelings or break things, an adult model of a nice trick, and practice tricks on family or staff with descriptive praise and a chosen item or activity for a fully correct trick."},{"k":"stephens1975","apa":"Stephens, C. E., Pear, J. J., Wray, L. D., & Jackson, G. C. (1975). Some effects of reinforcement schedules in teaching picture names to retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 435–447.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-435","y":1975,"sum":"Children aged 4 to 11 with severe intellectual disability or autism, living in a residential centre, who could repeat words but named few pictures, were taught picture names in two 20-minute sessions a day: each new picture was imitated and then named on 12 trials mixed with three known pictures, an error moved the child back several steps, and a picture counted as learned when it was named at the start of three later sessions."},{"k":"stevenslong1974","apa":"Stevens-Long, J., & Rasmussen, M. (1974). The acquisition of simple and compound sentence structure in an autistic child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(3), 473–479.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-473","y":1974,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy with autism in a special class, who spoke mostly in one or two words, was shown nine teaching pictures and asked What do you see?, and had to give two full sentences of set forms for each (later joined into one sentence with and), with a model after errors, a 10-second turn-away by the adult if he did not copy it, and a bite of food for each correct answer, in twice-weekly 30-minute sessions held just before lunch."},{"k":"stevenson2016","apa":"Stevenson, M. T., Ghezzi, P. M., & Valenton, K. G. (2016). FCT and delay fading for elopement with a child with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(2), 169–173.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0089-5","y":2016,"sum":"The request is taught to produce the item the problem behavior used to produce, honored at once at first; the item no longer follows problem behavior. The prompt is faded, then the delay between request and item is lengthened (see Wait for a requested item)."},{"k":"stocco2017","apa":"Stocco, C. S., Thompson, R. H., Hart, J. M., & Soriano, H. L. (2017). Improving the interview skills of college students using behavioral skills training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 50(3), 495–510.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.385","y":2017,"sum":"College students were taught job-interview skills (answering and asking questions, body language) with behavioral skills training and self-evaluation, practiced in simulated interviews; all learned the skills taught."},{"k":"stocks1987","apa":"Stocks, J. T., Thyer, B. A., & Kearsley, M. (1987). Using a token economy in a community-based residential program for disabled adults: An empirical evaluation leads to program modification. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 2(3), 173–185.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360020306","y":1987,"sum":"Twelve adults aged 19 to 42 (ten aged 19 to 26; eight with severe or profound hearing loss, others with epilepsy or psychiatric diagnoses) living in two vocational-rehabilitation halfway houses earned points for cleaning their bedroom, showering, cooking a balanced meal, household chores and laundry, lost points for missed tasks and rule breaking, and used points for daily rent, curfew extensions and promotion through a level system."},{"k":"stokes1974","apa":"Stokes, T. F., Baer, D. M., & Jackson, R. L. (1974). Programming the generalization of a greeting response in four retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 599–610.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-599","y":1974,"sum":"Four residents of a state institution aged 10 to 13 with severe or profound intellectual disability, who had no greeting, were taught to wave when an adult came within about 3 feet, with prompts (holding up the reward, guiding the arm) that were faded within days and M&Ms or potato chips plus a smile, a hello by name and a pat after each wave, by one trainer and, if needed, a second."},{"k":"stokes1978","apa":"Stokes, T. F., Fowler, S. A., & Baer, D. M. (1978). Training preschool children to recruit natural communities of reinforcement. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(2), 285–303.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-285","y":1978,"sum":"Fourth graders with developmental disabilities were taught in a special education room to show their work to the general education teacher and ask for feedback a few times each session; teacher praise, the work completed and its accuracy rose in the general education classroom."},{"k":"stolz1969","apa":"Stolz, S. B., & Wolf, M. M. (1969). Visually discriminated behavior in a \"blind\" adolescent retardate. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(1), 65–77.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-65","y":1969,"sum":"A 16-year-old with moderate intellectual disability and a history of aggression, living on an institutional ward and treated as blind although eye examinations found only a mild refractive error, was taught in his room to choose pictures and letters by sight and to look at the trainer when asked, with praise and sips of soda or snacks."},{"k":"strain1974","apa":"Strain, P. S., & Timm, M. A. (1974). An experimental analysis of social interaction between a behaviorally disordered preschool child and her classroom peers. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(4), 583–590.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-583","y":1974,"sum":"A girl aged 3 years 8 months with delayed language, the most isolated child in a state early-intervention language class, was observed during free play while an adult gave specific praise and a pat first to classmates for each positive contact with her and later to her for each positive contact with classmates."},{"k":"strain1976","apa":"Strain, P. S., Shores, R. E., & Kerr, M. M. (1976). An experimental analysis of \"spillover\" effects on the social interaction of behaviorally handicapped preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(1), 31–40.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-31","y":1976,"sum":"Three 4-year-old boys with delayed speech, tantrums and poor peer play (two grabbed toys and hit, one stayed alone) in the language classroom of a parent-staffed early intervention program received teacher prompts to play with peers and praise by name for positive play during free play."},{"k":"strain1977","apa":"Strain, P. S., Shores, R. E., & Timm, M. A. (1977). Effects of peer social initiations on the behavior of withdrawn preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 289–298.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-289","y":1977,"sum":"Six boys aged 3 and 4 with developmental delays (IQ 25 to 58), delayed language and frequent tantrums, enrolled in a private treatment center and rarely playing with peers, were seen in two groups of three during 20-minute playroom sessions."},{"k":"strang1975","apa":"Strang, H. R., & George, J. R., III (1975). Clowning around to stop clowning around: A brief report on an automated approach to monitor, record, and control classroom noise. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(4), 471–474.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-471","y":1975,"sum":"A rural first-grade class of 26 students and a third-grade class of 29 with too much noise during daily seatwork, with the share of session time above the teacher's noise threshold as the target. Noisy time fell from 39.4% of the session over 17 baseline sessions to 3.1% over 42 sessions with the display in the first grade, and from 44.0% over 10 sessions to 10.3% over 24 sessions in the third grade."},{"k":"striefel1973","apa":"Striefel, S., & Wetherby, B. (1973). Instruction-following behavior of a retarded child and its controlling stimuli. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 663–670.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-663","y":1973,"sum":"An 11-year-old boy with profound intellectual disability living in a state institution was taught to carry out one-step instructions about his body or four objects on the table, using physical guidance faded over eight steps, praise and ice cream, with each instruction counted as learned at 14 correct of 15 in mixed trials with instructions already known."},{"k":"striefel1974","apa":"Striefel, S., Bryan, K. S., & Aikins, D. A. (1974). Transfer of stimulus control from motor to verbal stimuli. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 123–135.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1974.7-123","y":1974,"sum":"Three adolescents aged 11 to 15 with profound intellectual disability in a state hospital and training center, who already copied the target actions, were taught to follow up to 25 one-step instructions (such as raising a hand or putting on a glove) with four objects on the table."},{"k":"strohmeier2018","apa":"Strohmeier, C. W., Crysdale, C., & Schwandtner, S. (2018). Rapid assessment of attention types for the treatment of attention-maintained problem behavior. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(4), 417–423.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00300-x","y":2018,"sum":"Children whose problem behavior was maintained by attention were taught short phrases that brought an adult over, and each one was answered at once at first; problem behavior no longer brought attention. Once the bids are reliable, signals show when the adult is free and when the student is to wait."},{"k":"stromer1975","apa":"Stromer, R. (1975). Modifying letter and number reversals in elementary school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(2), 211.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-211","y":1975,"sum":"Six children from regular and special-education elementary classes who reversed letters or numbers (naming p for q, writing 31 for 13, writing a digit backward, or reversing letters when copying or writing from dictation) were tutored in four small experiments."},{"k":"suarez2022","apa":"Suarez, V. D., Najdowski, A. C., Tarbox, J., Moon, E., St. Clair, M., & Farag, P. (2022). Teaching individuals with autism problem-solving skills for resolving social conflicts. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 15(3), 768–781.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-021-00643-y","y":2022,"sum":"Three males with autism (10, 11 and 22) used a worksheet with the 13 steps, earned praise for each independent step and time with a chosen activity scaled to their score, and were prompted least-to-most after errors across many set-up and captured conflicts. They reached 80% in 6 to 9 sessions, solved earlier unsolved conflicts at 70–100%, and resolved new real conflicts with no worksheet for up to 10 weeks."},{"k":"sump2019","apa":"Sump, L. A., Mottau, B. C., & LeBlanc, L. A. (2019). Evaluating behavioral skills training to teach basic computer skills to a young adult with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 331–334.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00295-5","y":2019,"sum":"A 15-year-old high-school student with autism who read at a fifth-grade level was taught 11-step tasks in three office programs by behavioral skills training: a reason for learning the skill and the printed task list, a model of every step, role-play with behavior-specific praise, and a pointing prompt after errors."},{"k":"sundberg1990b","apa":"Sundberg, M. L., San Juan, B., Dawdy, M., & Arguelles, M. (1990). The acquisition of tacts, mands, and intraverbals by individuals with traumatic brain injury. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 8, 83–99.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392850","y":1990,"sum":"Two men aged 40 and 33, injured in motorcycle accidents 19 and 10 years earlier and attending a rehabilitation day program, could repeat and read words but had great difficulty naming, asking for or talking about objects; each was taught the names of nine familiar tools, three words by naming the object, three by asking for it when it was missing from a task set up for them, and three by answering a spoken question about it, each with an echoic prompt…"},{"k":"surratt1969","apa":"Surratt, P. R., Ulrich, R. E., & Hawkins, R. P. (1969). An elementary student as a behavioral engineer. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(2), 85–92.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-85","y":1969,"sum":"Four first graders who seldom worked during a 20-minute independent arithmetic period were timed by a fifth-grade student at a console, which in later phases lit a lamp on each child's desk while the child worked; a child whose working time met a criterion (raised from 12 to 19 of 20 minutes) earned a ticket for a chosen 15-minute activity the next morning."},{"k":"switzer1977","apa":"Switzer, E. B., Deal, T. E., & Bailey, J. S. (1977). The reduction of stealing in second graders using a group contingency. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 267–272.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-267","y":1977,"sum":"Three second-grade classes of about 26 children each in one public elementary school, where money, pens, pencils and toys went missing two or three times a day and the teachers could not tell who was taking them. Daily thefts averaged 1.5 to 2.9 items per class in baseline and rose to 3.4 and 4.0 when teachers gave a talk on honesty every fifth day, but fell almost at once under the group contingency: one class lost only 2 items in 26 days, and in each of…"},{"k":"tanner2015","apa":"Tanner, A., & Andreone, B. E. (2015). Using graduated exposure and differential reinforcement to increase food repertoire in a child with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 8(2), 233–240.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0077-9","y":2015,"sum":"For children with established but narrow eating, procedures that work without escape extinction include praise or a preferred food for each accepted bite, preferred items available through the meal, the new food presented together with a preferred one, a quick sequence of easy requests (empty spoon presentations) before a bite, and bite size or texture faded in gradually."},{"k":"tarbox2007","apa":"Tarbox, R. S. F., Wallace, M. D., Penrod, B., & Tarbox, J. (2007). Effects of three-step prompting on compliance with caregiver requests. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 40(4), 703–706.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2007.703-706","y":2007,"sum":"High-probability sequences: three quick instructions the student nearly always follows, each praised, are given just before the instruction the student usually refuses; the idea rests on behavioral momentum, first studied in the laboratory. The sequence is then faded. Three-step prompting (tell, show, guide) with praise for following at each step raised compliance with caregivers’ requests. Harder instructions can be faded in gradually."},{"k":"tarbox2026","apa":"Tarbox, C., Tarbox, J., Bermudez, T. L., Silverman, E., & Servellon, L. (2026). Kind extinction: A procedural variation on traditional extinction. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 19(1), 88–98.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-023-00833-w","y":2026,"sum":"Four children aged 3–4 with autism in center- or home-based ABA programs whose tantrums, protests and throwing during teaching were maintained by escape from work and access to preferred items (one by interview, three by a synthesized functional analysis). Tantrums fell from a baseline mean of 0.47–1.25 per minute to 0.13–0.22 per minute (62–88% lower) and to 0–0.07 per minute at 2–4 week follow-up (94–100% lower)."},{"k":"tawney1972","apa":"Tawney, J. W. (1972). Training letter discrimination in four-year-old children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(4), 455–465.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-455","y":1972,"sum":"Thirty 4-year-olds from a summer Head Start program who made the most errors on a letter-matching test were assigned to training on letter-like shapes that rewarded matching by the features that separate letters (added or curved lines, open or closed forms, rotation and reversal), training that rewarded matching by size or line width, or no training, using a laboratory match-to-sample panel with a chime, light and bead for each correct choice and beads…"},{"k":"taylor1995","apa":"Taylor, B. A., & Harris, S. L. (1995). Teaching children with autism to seek information: Acquisition of novel information and generalization of responding. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 28(1), 3–14.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1995.28-3","y":1995,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to ask about items they could not name, with a prompt that was faded; they learned the names they were told and asked in new places and with new items."},{"k":"taylor2004","apa":"Taylor, B. A., Hughes, C. E., Richard, E., Hoch, H., & Coello, A. R. (2004). Teaching teenagers with autism to seek assistance when lost. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 37(1), 79–82.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2004.37-79","y":2004,"sum":"Teenagers with autism with severe communication deficits were taught at school and then in the community to answer a cell phone when lost and follow the directions given, seeking help from an unfamiliar adult by handing over a communication card; they did so in community sites where they were never taught and with their parents."},{"k":"tenenbaum1989","apa":"Tenenbaum, H. A., & Wolking, W. D. (1989). Effects of oral reading rate and inflection on intraverbal responding. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 7, 83–89.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392839","y":1989,"sum":"Three readers with dyslexia and no other disability (ages 7, 11 and 32) first read 1-minute passages with and without the colored overlay they had picked as clearest, and the overlay never raised correct words per minute (it lowered the adult's)."},{"k":"thomas1968","apa":"Thomas, D. R., Becker, W. C., & Armstrong, M. (1968). Production and elimination of disruptive classroom behavior by systematically varying teacher's behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(1), 35–45.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-35","y":1968,"sum":"The teacher of a public-school class of 28 typically developing children aged about 7 to 8 switched, phase by phase, between her usual praise and approval, no praise, and no praise with about three times her usual reprimands, during a morning reading and workbook period."},{"k":"thomas1976","apa":"Thomas, J. D. (1976). Accuracy of self-assessment of on-task behavior by elementary school children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(2), 209–210.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-209","y":1976,"sum":"In one second-grade class of 31 children aged 7 to 8, every child marked a card during 40-minute math lessons if he or she was on task when a signal sounded (15 to 20 signals per lesson) and chose games after the lesson according to the marks earned."},{"k":"thompson2024","apa":"Thompson, H. E., & Hanson, R. J. (2024). The use of a behavior chain interruption strategy to teach mands for help with an adult with intellectual disability and deaf-blindness. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 40(1), 88–98.","doi":"10.1007/s40616-024-00204-8","y":2024,"sum":"Children whose problem behavior was maintained by escape from difficult tasks were taught to ask for assistance, and real help followed each request; problem behavior fell as the requests rose, and the effect carried to new teachers and settings. Teach on hard items mixed with easy ones, prompt the request before problem behavior, and fade the prompt."},{"k":"thorne2008","apa":"Thorne, S., & Kamps, D. (2008). The effects of a group contingency intervention on academic engagement and problem behavior of at-risk students. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(2), 12–18.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391723","y":2008,"sum":"Teachers in four elementary classrooms added a group contingency to the school’s warning system: tickets for following the rules, a lottery for small prizes, rewards that depended on the whole class, and a chart where each student recorded the tickets earned. Engaged time rose and inappropriate behavior fell for all twelve at-risk students aged 7 to 9."},{"k":"tiger2004","apa":"Tiger, J. H., & Hanley, G. P. (2004). Developing stimulus control of preschooler mands: An analysis of schedule-correlated and contingency-specifying stimuli. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 37(4), 517–521.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2004.37-517","y":2004,"sum":"Requests are answered in one signaled period and not in the other, and the rule is told to the student; the periods alternate. Once requests come mostly in the \"available\" period, it is shortened step by step. This let teachers thin the answers to requests without problem behavior returning, and worked classwide in preschools."},{"k":"tiger2008","apa":"Tiger, J. H., Hanley, G. P., & Bruzek, J. (2008). Functional communication training: A review and practical guide. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(1), 16–23.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391716","y":2008,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"togashi2023","apa":"Togashi, K., Minagawa, Y., Hata, M., & Yamamoto, J. (2023). Evaluation of a telehealth parent-training program in Japan: Collaboration with parents to teach novel mand skills to children diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(3), 783–794.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-022-00752-2","y":2023,"sum":"The request is taught to produce the item the problem behavior used to produce, honored at once at first; the item no longer follows problem behavior. The prompt is faded, then the delay between request and item is lengthened (see Wait for a requested item)."},{"k":"torelli2016","apa":"Torelli, J. N., Lambert, J. M., Da Fonte, M. A., Denham, K. N., Jedrzynski, T. M., & Houchins-Juarez, N. J. (2016). Assessing acquisition of and preference for mand topographies during functional communication training. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(2), 165–168.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0083-y","y":2016,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"torelli2026","apa":"Torelli, J. N., Snyder, S. K., Griffin, M. L., Hellemn, M. P., Cagliani, R. R., & Morgan, G. A. (2026). Life skills evaluation in a kindergarten special education classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 59(1), e70041.","doi":"10.1002/jaba.70041","y":2026,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"torres2018","apa":"Torres, R., DeBar, R. M., Reeve, S. A., Meyer, L. S., & Covington, T. M. (2018). The effects of a video-enhanced schedule on exercise behavior. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(2), 85–96.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0224-1","y":2018,"sum":"Children with autism were taught to follow photo schedules with manual guidance from behind, faded gradually (graduated guidance), so that no adult prompt became part of the chain; they kept following them when the activities were reordered and new pictures were added."},{"k":"towery2014","apa":"Towery, D., Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H. (2014). Increasing independence within adult services: A program for reducing staff completion of daily routines for consumers with developmental disabilities. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 7(2), 61–69.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0013-4","y":2014,"sum":"Staff in three classrooms of an adult day program received a routines teaching program (the routine's task list, their baseline results, a demonstration of least-to-most prompting with praise, and feedback after observations) for snack and arrival routines with 15 adults with severe intellectual disability, most of them also nonambulatory."},{"k":"trace1977","apa":"Trace, M. W., Cuvo, A. J., & Criswell, J. L. (1977). Teaching coin equivalence to the mentally retarded. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 85–92.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-85","y":1977,"sum":"Fourteen adolescents aged 14 to 18 with mild to moderate intellectual disability living in a residential children's center, who already knew coin names and values and could count by ones and fives, were matched in pairs, and one of each pair was taught to make amounts from 5 to 50 cents with different mixes of nickels, dimes and quarters, one combining method per stage over six stages."},{"k":"trap1978","apa":"Trap, J. J., Milner-Davis, P., Joseph, S., & Cooper, J. O. (1978). The effects of feedback and consequences on transitional cursive letter formation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 381–393.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-381","y":1978,"sum":"Twelve first graders aged 6–7 copied the cursive alphabet in 15-minute sessions three to five times a week after the adult modelled each letter once. Three steps were added in turn for 10 target letters: the adult laid a transparent overlay on the sample and described each correct and incorrect stroke with praise; the student then rewrote once any target letter with an error; finally a certificate of achievement, presented in front of classmates, was…"},{"k":"turner1990","apa":"Turner, J. M., Green, G., & Braunling-McMorrow, D. (1990). Differential reinforcement of low rates of responding (DRL) to reduce dysfunctional social behaviors of a head injured man. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(1), 15–27.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050103","y":1990,"sum":"One 21-year-old man with a traumatic brain injury in a residential rehabilitation program, living in a group house, who swore, threatened, yelled, made sexual and personal remarks to strangers, shook his fist or objects at people, hit or shoved, and touched women inappropriately, 12 or more times a day."},{"k":"twardosz1972","apa":"Twardosz, S., & Sajwaj, T. (1972). Multiple effects of a procedure to increase sitting in a hyperactive, retarded boy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 73–78.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-73","y":1972,"sum":"A 4-year-old boy with mild intellectual disability (IQ 74) in a remedial preschool was seated at the free-play table with toys and given praise and token marks for staying seated, thinned from about every 10 seconds to about once a minute, in an ABAB design. Sitting, floor posturing, walking, toy use and nearness to other children were recorded in 10-second blocks during free play, with no mastery criterion."},{"k":"twardosz1973","apa":"Twardosz, S., & Baer, D. M. (1973). Training two severely retarded adolescents to ask questions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(4), 655–661.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-655","y":1973,"sum":"Two 16-year-old boys with severe intellectual disability living at a state institution, who spoke in short phrases, were taught in 30-minute table sessions to name seven letters and numbers or colors, and then to ask \"What letter?\" when a known card was held blank side out, by prompting the question in parts and then answering it so the boy could name the card for a token traded for candy after the session."},{"k":"tyner2016","apa":"Tyner, S., Brewer, A., Helman, M., Leon, Y., Pritchard, J., & Schlund, M. (2016). Nice doggie! Contact desensitization plus reinforcement decreases dog phobias for children with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(1), 54–57.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0113-4","y":2016,"sum":"Three children with autism aged 5 to 10 who avoided dogs were treated in a church gym: while watching a preferred video or playing a game, each was prompted to move toward a leashed therapy dog in 5-ft and then 1-ft steps, earning praise and edibles, and was moved back a step at any sign of distress. The measure was the closest distance reached on an avoidance test after each session, with goals set by parents (touching the dog, or calm approach)."},{"k":"valentino2019","apa":"Valentino, A. L., LeBlanc, L. A., Veazey, S. E., Weaver, L. A., & Raetz, P. B. (2019). Using a prerequisite skills assessment to identify optimal modalities for mand training. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(1), 22–32.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-0256-6","y":2019,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"vargo2019","apa":"Vargo, K. K., & Becknell, K. (2019). An application of the group-oriented concurrent-chains arrangement. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 310–319.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00286-6","y":2019,"sum":"Teachers in four elementary classrooms added a group contingency to the school’s warning system: tickets for following the rules, a lottery for small prizes, rewards that depended on the whole class, and a chart where each student recorded the tickets earned. Engaged time rose and inappropriate behavior fell for all twelve at-risk students aged 7 to 9."},{"k":"vasquez2017","apa":"Vasquez, S., Brewer, A., Leon, Y., & Vasquez, J. (2017). The effects of advance notice on problem behavior occasioned by interruptions of an ongoing activity in a young girl with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(4), 417–421.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0187-7","y":2017,"sum":"A trial-based functional analysis in the home of a 7-year-old girl with autism showed that her screaming, crying and aggression followed interruptions of an activity and were maintained by getting the activity back. In treatment, 2 minutes before each interruption an adult started a timer, set out the next activity's materials and told her when she would switch; when the timer sounded, starting the new activity within 30 s earned praise and playful…"},{"k":"veazey2016","apa":"Veazey, S. E., Valentino, A. L., Low, A. I., McElroy, A. R., & LeBlanc, L. A. (2016). Teaching feminine hygiene skills to young females with autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(2), 184–189.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0065-0","y":2016,"sum":"Two girls, a 9-year-old with autism and an 11-year-old with Down syndrome and PDD-NOS, practiced at home with their mothers present, using pads and underwear colored with red dye to look soiled and a written step-by-step routine. Each step was taught by total-task chaining (the 11-year-old began with forward chaining) using a verbal, then gestural, then hand-over-hand prompt sequence, with praise for each correct step and a preferred item at the end."},{"k":"vedora2017","apa":"Vedora, J., Barry, T., & Ward-Horner, J. C. (2017). An evaluation of differential observing responses during receptive label training. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(3), 290–295.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0188-6","y":2017,"sum":"A 15-year-old with autism who used a communication device and a 21-year-old with Down syndrome, both at a private school, were taught to touch named flag pictures in arrays of three. Teaching used an identical picture held up as a prompt, with a delay that grew from 0 to 4 s and a chosen edible for correct responses, either with or without first repeating the flag's name aloud before each trial; mastery was 92% unprompted correct in two consecutive…"},{"k":"vedora2019","apa":"Vedora, J., Barry, T., & Ward-Horner, J. C. (2019). Sample first versus comparison first stimulus presentations: Preliminary findings for two individuals with autism. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 423–429.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00299-1","y":2019,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"vincent2017","apa":"Vincent, L. B., Openden, D., Gentry, J. A., Long, L. A., & Matthews, N. L. (2018). Promoting social learning at recess for children with ASD and related social challenges. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 11(1), 19–33.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-017-0178-8","y":2018,"sum":"Seven students in kindergarten to fourth grade at a suburban public school (six receiving services for autism and one for an emotional or behavioral disorder) who spent recess alone or with adults took part in a 20-minute daily recess program run by trained facilitators."},{"k":"volkert2009","apa":"Volkert, V. M., Lerman, D. C., Call, N. A., & Trosclair-Lasserre, N. (2009). An evaluation of resurgence during treatment with functional communication training. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 42(1), 145–160.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2009.42-145","y":2009,"sum":"The request is taught to produce what the problem behavior produced: a short break from the task. At first every request is honored at once, and it is prompted as soon as the task is presented, before problem behavior can occur; the prompt is then faded. Problem behavior no longer produces a break. Once requests are reliable, the work required before a break is honored grows step by step."},{"k":"volkert2016","apa":"Volkert, V. M., Piazza, C. C., & Ray-Price, R. (2016). Further manipulations in response effort or magnitude of an aversive consequence to increase self-feeding in children with feeding disorders. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(2), 103–113.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0124-1","y":2016,"sum":"Three boys aged 2 to 4 with feeding disorders, who could feed themselves but rarely did, were given a choice on each trial in a hospital feeding program between feeding themselves one bite or drink and being fed under less attractive conditions (an added bite of a disliked food, a flipped-spoon presentation, or redistribution and swallow facilitation)."},{"k":"vorndran2008","apa":"Vorndran, C. M., Pace, G. M., Luiselli, J. K., Flaherty, J., Christian, L., & Kleinmann, A. (2008). Functional analysis and treatment of chronic hair pulling in a child with Cri du Chat syndrome: Effects on co-occurring thumb sucking. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(1), 10–15.","doi":"10.1007/bf03391715","y":2008,"sum":"One 8-year-old girl with Cri du Chat syndrome who used about 15 signs, seen in a therapy room and then her classroom at a private school, with chronic hair pulling that had left bald areas, plus co-occurring thumb sucking during independent play. Hair pulling, seen in 30% to 100% of intervals in baseline, dropped sharply with treatment and the effect was replicated in an ABAB reversal; untreated thumb sucking dropped along with it during the evaluation."},{"k":"voulgarakis2015","apa":"Voulgarakis, H., & Forte, S. (2015). Escape extinction and negative reinforcement in the treatment of pediatric feeding disorders: A single case analysis. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 8(2), 212–214.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-015-0086-8","y":2015,"sum":"For children with established but narrow eating, procedures that work without escape extinction include praise or a preferred food for each accepted bite, preferred items available through the meal, the new food presented together with a preferred one, a quick sequence of easy requests (empty spoon presentations) before a bite, and bite size or texture faded in gradually."},{"k":"wahler1969b","apa":"Wahler, R. G. (1969). Setting generality: Some specific and general effects of child behavior therapy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(4), 239–246.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-239","y":1969,"sum":"Two boys aged 5 and 8 referred by their teachers were observed in weekly 30-minute sessions both at home and at school; parents, and later teachers, were coached to praise cooperation (the 5-year-old, with a 5-minute timeout for refusal) or studying (the 8-year-old, whose distraction was ignored), in a design that changed home contingencies first and school contingencies last."},{"k":"walker1968","apa":"Walker, H. M., & Buckley, N. K. (1968). The use of positive reinforcement in conditioning attending behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(3), 245–250.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-245","y":1968,"sum":"A 9-year-old fourth grader enrolled in a class for students with behavior disorders did programmed math in a quiet room in three 10-minute blocks a day and earned points toward a model of his choice for each interval of unbroken attention, the required interval doubling from 30 seconds to 10 minutes after each 10 minutes of success. After a reversal, his regular-class teacher gave him a point for about every 30 minutes of attending, with praise."},{"k":"walker1972","apa":"Walker, H. M., & Buckley, N. K. (1972). Programming generalization and maintenance of treatment effects across time and across settings. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(3), 209–224.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-209","y":1972,"sum":"Eleven students in grades 3 to 6 (of 44 in the study) with average or higher ability, referred for defiance, tantrums, verbal and physical aggression toward peers and off-task behavior, during their first two months back in their general classrooms after two months in a special token classroom."},{"k":"ward1968","apa":"Ward, M. H., & Baker, B. L. (1968). Reinforcement therapy in the classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(4), 323–328.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1968.1-323","y":1968,"sum":"Four first graders in an urban public school (three boys with frequent disruptive behavior and one withdrawn, inattentive girl) were observed in 15-minute periods while their three teachers, after four weekly seminars and classroom coaching, praised task-related work and ignored minor misbehavior; two boys also had six sessions in which a desk light signaled approval for steady work."},{"k":"ward2017","apa":"Ward, S., Parker, A., & Perdikaris, A. (2017). Task as reinforcer: A reactive alternative to traditional forms of escape extinction. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(1), 22–34.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0139-7","y":2017,"sum":"Five children with autism aged 4 to 9 had work materials and attention briefly taken away (a \"wait out\") after noncompliance, protest or aggression, until they had been calm for about 5 s and signaled they were ready to work again."},{"k":"warner1977","apa":"Warner, S. P., Miller, F. D., & Cohen, M. W. (1977). Relative effectiveness of teacher attention and the \"good behavior game\" in modifying disruptive classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(4), 737.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-737","y":1977,"sum":"Four teachers of fourth- and fifth-grade classes of 25 students each used both the good behavior game and a teacher-attention method over five weeks, in counterbalanced order. Talking out and leaving seats without permission were recorded as present or absent in 15-second intervals, and teacher preference was asked; only the abstract was printed (full text deposited)."},{"k":"warren1976","apa":"Warren, S. F., Rogers-Warren, A., & Baer, D. M. (1976). The role of offer rates in controlling sharing by young children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 9(4), 491–497.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1976.9-491","y":1976,"sum":"Nine preschool children in two small groups (aged 3 to 7, two of them with mild intellectual disability) played for two 5-minute segments a day with shared building or drawing materials. First, a piece of cereal or candy with praise followed any segment in which a child offered to share; then children were told to offer only things they had finished using and to ask before handing them over, and rewards depended on making only one or two offers (versus…"},{"k":"waters2009","apa":"Waters, M. B., Lerman, D. C., & Hovanetz, A. N. (2009). Separate and combined effects of visual schedules and extinction plus differential reinforcement on problem behavior occasioned by transitions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 42(2), 309–313.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.2009.42-309","y":2009,"sum":"Giving up a preferred item is taught like waiting: at first the item comes back soon after it is handed over, and the time without it grows. A visual schedule alone did not reduce problem behavior at transitions; it fell when calm transitions earned reinforcement and problem behavior no longer delayed them."},{"k":"watkins1989","apa":"Watkins, C. L., Pack-Teixeira, L., & Howard, J. S. (1989). Teaching intraverbal behavior to severely retarded children. The Analysis of Verbal Behavior, 7, 69–81.","doi":"10.1007/bf03392838","y":1989,"sum":"Four children aged 9 to 12 with severe intellectual disability, who could already name pictured items, were taught one at a time at school to name a color, a size or a texture and then members of five categories on request, giving a different member each time they were asked for another, and later two-word answers such as \"big bear\"."},{"k":"weisberg1973","apa":"Weisberg, P., Passman, R. H., & Russell, J. E. (1973). Development of verbal control over bizarre gestures of retardates through imitative and nonimitative reinforcement procedures. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(3), 487–495.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-487","y":1973,"sum":"Two men aged 23 and 28 with severe and profound intellectual disability living in a state institution, who made frequent repetitive hand movements and imitated nothing, were taught to copy an arm raise and two other actions that kept the hands busy, using guidance, shaping, food and praise (criterion 28 of 35 trials), and then not to make their hand movement when told \"Do not do this\" along with a head shake and a model of the movement."},{"k":"weisberg1977","apa":"Weisberg, P., & Clements, P. (1977). Effects of direct, intermittent, and vicarious reinforcement procedures on the development and maintenance of instruction-following behaviors in a group of young children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 314.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-314","y":1977,"sum":"Abstract only (the full report was deposited with an auxiliary publication service). A group of 12 children averaging 2½ years in a day-care center received a teacher's requests while watching an adult, and then several peers, be rewarded for following them, and later each child received direct but intermittent reinforcement for following."},{"k":"wendt2019","apa":"Wendt, O., Hsu, N., Simon, K., Dienhart, A., & Cain, L. (2019). Effects of an iPad-based speech-generating device infused into instruction with the Picture Exchange Communication System for adolescents and young adults with severe autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Modification, 43(6), 898–932.","doi":"10.1177/0145445519870552","y":2019,"sum":"Picture exchange is taught in phases: a single picture exchanged for the item with a second adult prompting from behind, then travelling to the book and the adult, then choosing between pictures, then a sentence strip; in the study, speech and social communication rose and problem behavior fell. Students who learned to request with assistive communication devices at school went on to use them in the community with people who had not been trained."},{"k":"wesolowski1992","apa":"Wesolowski, M. D., & Zencius, A. H. (1992). Treatment of aggression in a brain injured adolescent. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 7(3), 205–210.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360070303","y":1992,"sum":"One 16-year-old with traumatic brain injury living in a residential rehabilitation program with seven other adolescents and attending special education classes, who hit, kicked, shoved and bit peers and staff about 2.4 times a week."},{"k":"wesolowski1993","apa":"Wesolowski, M. D., Zencius, A., & Burke, W. H. (1993). Effects of feedback and behavior contracting on head trauma persons’ inappropriate sexual behavior. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 8(2), 89–96.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360080203","y":1993,"sum":"Three people with traumatic brain injury (women of 18 and 31, a man of 25) at a residential head-injury rehabilitation center who touched or kissed people of the opposite sex without invitation, in the center and the community; the man also made sexual remarks."},{"k":"whalen2003","apa":"Whalen, C., & Schreibman, L. (2003). Joint attention training for children with autism using behavior modification procedures. Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry, 44(3), 456–468.","doi":"10.1111/1469-7610.00135","y":2003,"sum":"Young children with autism were taught to respond to joint attention bids, then to start them, with behavioral prompting, reinforcement and gradual fading, in play."},{"k":"wheeler1970","apa":"Wheeler, A. J., & Sulzer, B. (1970). Operant training and generalization of a verbal response form in a speech-deficient child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 139–147.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-139","y":1970,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy with autism and intellectual disability who spoke mostly in single words and shortened phrases was taught to describe action pictures in a full sentence (article and noun, the helping verb with an -ing action word, then article and object) by treating the sentence as a three-part chain, modeling any part he got wrong for him to repeat before going on, and giving a token after each complete sentence."},{"k":"whelan2019","apa":"Whelan, C. M., & Penrod, B. (2019). An evaluation of sequential meal presentation with picky eaters. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 12(2), 301–309.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-018-00277-7","y":2019,"sum":"For children with established but narrow eating, procedures that work without escape extinction include praise or a preferred food for each accepted bite, preferred items available through the meal, the new food presented together with a preferred one, a quick sequence of easy requests (empty spoon presentations) before a bite, and bite size or texture faded in gradually."},{"k":"white2011","apa":"White, E. R., Hoffmann, B., Hoch, H., & Taylor, B. A. (2011). Teaching teamwork to adolescents with autism: The cooperative use of activity schedules. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 4(1), 27–35.","doi":"10.1007/BF03391772","y":2011,"sum":"Six young men with autism (ages 16–19) at a behavioral school could already follow schedules alone. They were taught in three pairs to share one written or photo schedule to clean a kitchen, restock kitchen supplies or clean an office. The teacher used graduated guidance and gave edibles for correct steps, then faded her prompts and her presence."},{"k":"whiteblackburn1977","apa":"White-Blackburn, G., Semb, S., & Semb, G. (1977). The effects of a good-behavior contract on the classroom behaviors of sixth-grade students. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(2), 312.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-312","y":1977,"sum":"Abstract only (the full report was deposited with an auxiliary publication service). Four sixth graders in a public school class negotiated a contract with the teacher at the start of each morning work period, listing conduct and assignment goals, the classroom privileges they could earn and the penalties for disruption, and at the end the teacher decided, with the student agreeing, whether a reward or a penalty had been earned; a separate work area was…"},{"k":"whitman1970","apa":"Whitman, T. L., Mercurio, J. R., & Caponigri, V. (1970). Development of social responses in two severely retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 3(2), 133–138.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1970.3-133","y":1970,"sum":"A 6-year-old girl with a brain disorder and a 10-year-old boy with Down syndrome, both with severe intellectual disability and no spoken words, were taught by two undergraduates in daily 30-minute classroom sessions to roll a ball and pass a block to each other, with hand guidance at first and candy plus praise on a schedule thinned from every exchange to every 30th, and later with a third child added to each game."},{"k":"whitman1971","apa":"Whitman, T. L., Zakaras, M., & Chardos, S. (1971). Effects of reinforcement and guidance procedures on instruction-following behavior of severely retarded children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 4(4), 283–290.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1971.4-283","y":1971,"sum":"Two children with severe intellectual disability at a school for children with intellectual disability, a 4.5-year-old boy and a 7-year-old girl, were taught to follow one-step instructions (sit down, give me the pencil, put the pencil in the box) in daily 30-minute sessions using full physical guidance that was faded from the end of the movement backward, with praise and a bite of cereal or chocolate or a sip of sweetened drink."},{"k":"willis1972","apa":"Willis, J., & Crowder, J. (1972). A portable device for group modification of classroom attending behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(2), 199–202.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1972.5-199","y":1972,"sum":"A first-grade teacher carried a pocket wireless switch to run a wall clock whenever every child was attending (with a red light on whenever anyone was not), and a class movie depended on reaching the day’s announced number of attending minutes, raised from 1 to 31 of 60. Whole-class attending minutes were timed daily over 46 days by the clock and by an independent observer’s stopwatch; there was no mastery criterion."},{"k":"wilson1973b","apa":"Wilson, C. W., & Hopkins, B. L. (1973). The effects of contingent music on the intensity of noise in junior high home economics classes. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 6(2), 269–275.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1973.6-269","y":1973,"sum":"Four junior high home economics sewing classes of 17 to 19 girls aged 12 to 15 in a rural Kansas school, some with serious discipline histories, worked on individual projects while a radio tuned to the students' preferred station played only as long as class noise stayed below a level set by the teacher (70 or 76 dB)."},{"k":"wilson1991","apa":"Wilson, P. G., Reid, D. H., & Korabek-Pinkowski, C. A. (1991). Analysis of public verbal feedback as a staff management procedure. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(4), 263–277.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060404","y":1991,"sum":"Direct care staff on an institution unit for 33 residents aged 12 to 20 with profound intellectual disability received brief public verbal feedback at staff meetings on how often they ran handwashing training, in a multiple baseline across three staff groups."},{"k":"wilson2015","apa":"Wilson, A. N., Kasson, E. M., Gratz, O., & Guercio, J. M. (2015). Exploring the clinical utility of a stimulus avoidance assessment to enhance a relaxation training model. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 8(1), 57–61.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-014-0035-y","y":2015,"sum":"A 29-year-old man with autism and escape-maintained verbal and physical aggression, living in a community home with 24-hour staff, ranked 12 upsetting situations in a multiple-stimulus-without-replacement format, which sorted them into low, medium and high levels."},{"k":"wilson2016","apa":"Wilson, A. N., & Gratz, O. H. (2016). Using a progressive ratio schedule of reinforcement as an assessment tool to inform treatment. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 257–260.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-016-0107-2","y":2016,"sum":"An 8-year-old boy with emotional disturbance whose aggression and property destruction were maintained by getting an iPad was taught in one session to ask for it politely, and a progressive-ratio test (one more request needed for each access until he aggressed or stopped) found he would make 14 requests."},{"k":"wood1978","apa":"Wood, R., & Flynn, J. M. (1978). A self-evaluation token system versus an external evaluation token system alone in a residential setting with predelinquent youth. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(4), 503–512.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-503","y":1978,"sum":"In 10 combined first- and second-grade public school classes, 16 of the most disruptive children first earned points toward small school prizes for few talk-outs, hits and trips out of their seats during 30 minutes of seatwork, then tallied these on a card that was checked against an observer's count, and finally awarded their own points with no check."},{"k":"wulbert1977","apa":"Wulbert, M., & Dries, R. (1977). The relative efficacy of methylphenidate (Ritalin) and behavior-modification techniques in the treatment of a hyperactive child. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 10(1), 21–31.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1977.10-21","y":1977,"sum":"One boy aged 8 years 11 months of average intelligence, described as hyperactive, who during one-to-one table tasks in a clinic often raised his hands to smell them, rolled imaginary objects between his fingers, and made piercing noises or blew on his hands."},{"k":"yang1988","apa":"Yang, L.-Z. (1988). Elimination of habitual rumination through the strategies of food satiation and fading: A case study. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 3(3), 223–234.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360030306","y":1988,"sum":"One 17-year-old young man with profound intellectual disability living in a state residential facility, who fed himself but had ruminated for years (21 to 36 episodes a day) and was dangerously underweight with no medical cause found. Rumination fell from 21 to 36 episodes a day to zero within 5 days of extra food, rose a little when the extra food was briefly removed, and dropped again when it returned."},{"k":"yeaton1978","apa":"Yeaton, W. H., & Bailey, J. S. (1978). Teaching pedestrian safety skills to young children: An analysis and one-year followup. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 11(3), 315–329.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1978.11-315","y":1978,"sum":"Students with intellectual disabilities were taught the parts of crossing the street on a classroom model of city traffic; checked in real traffic, they crossed correctly, and training some parts also improved parts not yet trained."},{"k":"zencius1990a","apa":"Zencius, A., & Wesolowski, M. D. (1990). Brief report: Using stress management to decrease inappropriate behavior in a brain injured adult. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(1), 61–64.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360050107","y":1990,"sum":"Progressive relaxation was taught step by step with modeling and practice until the boy could relax on cue; his disruptive behavior fell."},{"k":"zencius1991a","apa":"Zencius, A. H., Wesolowski, M. D., Burke, W. H., & McQuade, P. (1991). Memory checklists: A method of teaching functional skills to brain-damaged adults. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(1), 1–10.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060102","y":1991,"sum":"Three adults (24 to 38) with memory loss after traumatic brain injury in a residential rehabilitation center were given typed task-analysis checklists with a box to tick beside each step, after a trainer showed them how to tick each step once it was done; two men used them for woodshop tasks and one woman for morning grooming, in a multiple baseline across tasks."},{"k":"zencius1991b","apa":"Zencius, A. H., & Wesolowski, M. D. (1991). Reducing verbal aggression in adults with brain injury. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 6(3), 155–164.","doi":"10.1002/bin.2360060302","y":1991,"sum":"A 26-year-old man with traumatic brain injury in a transitional living rehabilitation center who cursed at, threatened and verbally abused staff and peers about 4.7 times a day. Verbal abuse fell from 4.7 a day in baseline to 1.0 a day under contracts, rose to 1.6 when contracts stopped, and dropped to 0.5 a day when they resumed, with only 2 episodes in a 1-month follow-up."},{"k":"zhi2023","apa":"Zhi, H., Fienup, D. M., Chan, K., & Cariveau, T. (2023). A component analysis of skill acquisition consequences with listener responses. Journal of Behavioral Education, 16(4), 1138–1150.","doi":"10.1007/s10864-023-09509-5","y":2023,"sum":"Two 4-year-old boys with autism were taught, in short 9-trial preschool sessions, to pick the named picture out of three (animals, food groups, tastes, kinds of animals). A right answer earned praise and a snack at the table or, in the game version, praise and the next move in a game, such as jumping to the picture on a mat or adding a piece to a toy-train track; after an error the teacher gave the direction again and pointed to the answer."},{"k":"zhi2023b","apa":"Zhi, H., Fienup, D. M., Greer, R. D., & Henderson, S. S. (2023). A comparison of stimulus set sizes: Systematic replication with operant analysis acquisition criteria. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 16(4), 1138–1150.","doi":"10.1007/s40617-023-00793-1","y":2023,"sum":"Two 10-year-olds with intellectual delays in a self-contained class practiced unknown words on a computer: each word appeared, a recording then read it, and the student tried to say it first and repeated it after, three times per word each session. Words read within 2 s on two tests in a row were counted as learned and swapped for new ones, and both students learned far more practiced than unpracticed words."},{"k":"zimmerman1969","apa":"Zimmerman, E. H., Zimmerman, J., & Russell, C. D. (1969). Differential effects of token reinforcement on instruction-following behavior in retarded students instructed as a group. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(2), 101–112.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1969.2-101","y":1969,"sum":"Seven boys aged 8 to 15 with intellectual disability (one also autistic, one deaf), chosen for not following instructions in class, met for 30 minutes a day for 7 weeks as a group in which a teacher read 30 simple instructions (sit and raise a hand, copy an action or drawing, point to a named picture, bring scissors, say a greeting)."},{"k":"zlutnick1975","apa":"Zlutnick, S., Mayville, W. J., & Moffat, S. (1975). Modification of seizure disorders: The interruption of behavioral chains. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 8(1), 1–12.","doi":"10.1901/jaba.1975.8-1","y":1975,"sum":"One 17-year-old young woman with intellectual disability and major motor epilepsy at a training center for adolescents, having about 16 seizures a day despite anticonvulsants, whose seizures always began with her body tensing and then her fists clenching and arms rising to shoulder height."}];
